{"generated_at": "2026-07-27T18:12:52.955000+00:00", "record_count": 118, "records": [{"title": "Who is doing this, and on whose money", "url": "/about.html", "category": "Site page", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Who runs Lubbock", "text": "About this chapter | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content MW A chapter of the Move Weight Foundation Lubbock, TX of 14 Oklahoma 4 Miami Oklahoma City Tulsa Claremore Texas 7 San Angelo Houston Dallas Austin San Antonio Lubbock Abilene Mississippi 3 Southaven Jackson Olive Branch All 14 chapters and the map → About · Lubbock, TX Who is doing this, and on whose money This is the Lubbock chapter of the Move Weight Foundation , a 501(c)(3) nonprofit. It is independent of the City of Lubbock, of Lubbock County, and of the State of Texas. It takes no money from any of them. How we source claims Every factual claim on this site links to a primary document or to a named outlet. Where a document exists, we publish the document rather than our description of it. We never invent a government phone number, address or docket number. Every contact datum is transcribed from an official published source and dated on the page. If we have not transcribed it yet, the page says so and links to the city's own directory instead of guessing. Every external link is fetched before it is published , and re-checked automatically every week. The result of that check is public on the automation page . Sources for this chapter were last verified 2026-07-26 . We publish released records whole. No redaction of a public record the government has already released. We never allege a crime. We publish the document, the amount and the date, and let readers and prosecutors do their own jobs. A submitter's identity is never published — not in a page, not in a filename, not in file metadata. We link out to local news and never republish it. What this chapter is not It is not a newspaper, not a campaign, and not aligned with any candidate, party or slate. It does not endorse. Its output is records, an archive, and the plain fact of whether a public body answered a lawful request on time. Funding The Foundation is funded by individual donations. Records cost money — Texas allows a body to charge for search and copying under § 552.2615, and a single large request can run into real numbers. Donations pay those invoices, the servers that hold the archive, and nothing else. Contributions are tax-deductible. Zelle: team@moveweight.com . Financial disclosures and the Form 990 are published by the Foundation at foundation.moveweight.com . Corrections If something here is wrong, we want to fix it and we will say that we did. Send the correction and, if you have it, the document — here . A correction is never quietly edited in; the change and its date stay on the page. Contact Records and requests: foia@moveweight.com Everything else: expose@moveweight.net"}, {"title": "What is stale", "url": "/automation.html", "category": "Site page", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "When every automated pull for this chapter last ran, whether it succeeded, and what is out of date right now. Published whether it is flattering or not.", "text": "What is stale — Lubbock automation status | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content MW A chapter of the Move Weight Foundation Lubbock, TX of 14 Oklahoma 4 Miami Oklahoma City Tulsa Claremore Texas 7 San Angelo Houston Dallas Austin San Antonio Lubbock Abilene Mississippi 3 Southaven Jackson Olive Branch All 14 chapters and the map → Automation · Lubbock, TX What is stale This chapter refreshes itself on a weekly timer: it pulls new agendas and meeting video from Lubbock's portal, re-checks every external link on the site, and rebuilds the search index. This page reports what actually happened, including when it failed. A site that quietly rots looks identical to one that is current, right up until somebody relies on it. So the status below is generated by the jobs themselves — it is not maintained by hand and cannot be made to look better than it is. Status Loading the last run… Job What it does Last run Result Detail Loading… What each job is allowed to do Agendas and meetings. Fetches only documents Lubbock has already published, from CivicPlus AgendaCenter at https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter . It never logs in, never submits anything, and never touches a page the city has not made public. Link check. Re-fetches every external link this chapter publishes. Cities move pages, and a records-request link that 404s fails a reader at the exact moment they were about to act. Search index. Rebuilds the site's index from the pages and the extracted agenda text, so a resident can search a phrase and land on the agenda that carried it. Nothing writes to a government system. Records requests are sent by a person, deliberately, never by a timer. If a job has been red for a while That is a bug, not a policy. Tell us — the whole point of publishing this page is that somebody outside the project can see the failure before we do."}, {"title": "Every official link, in one place", "url": "/city-desk.html", "category": "Site page", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Verified links to Lubbock city government, Lubbock County offices, records portals, budgets and Texas oversight bodies. Checked 2026-07-26.", "text": "Lubbock City Desk — every official link in one place | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content Records & transparency Open records requests Lubbock's GovQA portal — where a Texas Public Information Act request to the City is filed and tracked. https://lubbocktx.govqa.us/WEBAPP/_rs/supporthome.aspx County & state Lubbock County https://www.lubbockcounty.gov/ Texas AG — Open Government https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/open-government Texas oversight Where a Lubbock decision gets reviewed by somebody who does not work for Lubbock. Texas Attorney General — Open Government A body that wants to withhold must ask the AG within 10 business days. Those rulings are public and searchable, and they are the single best record of what a city tried to hide. Texas Comptroller — Transparency Local government debt, tax rates and economic-development agreements, filed by the entities themselves. Texas Open Data Portal Statewide datasets. Meetings, and how to be in the room Lubbock publishes its agendas through CivicPlus AgendaCenter : https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter Agendas are posted ahead of the meeting because the law requires it. That posting is the single most useful document in local government and almost nobody opens it — it is where a contract award, a rate change or a tax abatement appears in public for the first time, usually as one line in a consent agenda. Reading a consent agenda Items on a consent agenda are voted through together, with no discussion, in a few seconds. That is not a trick — it is how a body gets through routine business. But it means the only chance to see an item is before the meeting, in the packet. Any member can pull an item out for discussion, and so can a resident who asks one in time. Where to watch → Open the agenda portal → A link that does not work Cities move pages. Every link on this page is re-checked automatically each week and the result is published on the automation page . If one is broken and we have not caught it, tell us ."}, {"title": "A city that owns its power company, on a shrinking aquifer", "url": "/", "category": "Home", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "A city that owns its power company, on a shrinking aquifer — a Move Weight Foundation chapter covering Lubbock, Lubbock County. File a records request, read the agendas, watch the meetings.", "text": "Lubbock, TX — public records and meeting archive Skip to content Why Lubbock Lubbock runs a municipal electric utility and sits on the Ogallala. Industrial load growth — including data-centre-scale load — lands on ratepayers through the utility rather than through the tax roll, which means the decision that raises a household bill is made in a utility board packet, not in a budget hearing. The Move Weight Foundation's first chapter is a town of thirteen thousand people in Ottawa County, Oklahoma, and it now holds 522 archived meetings with machine transcripts. The second is San Angelo, Texas. The third is Southaven, Mississippi, where the City's own released documents show an ad valorem agreement abating two thirds of property tax on a recital of twenty new jobs. None of those started with a tip. They started with somebody reading the agenda. If you already know something Send it anonymously . A submitter's identity is never published — not in a page, not in a filename, not in metadata."}, {"title": "115 meetings, indexed", "url": "/meetings/", "category": "Meetings", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Every Lubbock public meeting we have indexed - 115 of them, back to 2026-03-05 - with the city", "text": "Lubbock meeting archive | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content MW A chapter of the Move Weight Foundation Lubbock, TX of 14 Oklahoma 4 Miami Oklahoma City Tulsa Claremore Texas 7 San Angelo Houston Dallas Austin San Antonio Lubbock Abilene Mississippi 3 Southaven Jackson Olive Branch All 14 chapters and the map → Meetings · Lubbock, TX 115 meetings, indexed Every public meeting this chapter has found in Lubbock's own portal, newest first, each one linked to the agenda, minutes and recording the City published for it. Refreshed automatically every week - see what is stale . Source: civicplus at https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter . Last pulled 2026-07-27. These agendas are searchable The text of each agenda below is indexed, so you can search a phrase and land on the meeting that carried it. Search the archive → Date Body Agenda Minutes Video 2026-08-11 Animal Services Advisory Board Regular Meeting Agenda August 11, 2026 11:30 a.m. 1306 9th Street Lubbock Public Library – Mahon Branch Agenda — — 2026-08-04 08-04-2026 Planning and Zoning Commission Regular Meeting Agenda — — 2026-07-3