l Services Advisory Board - 11:30 AM Agenda — — 2026-06-09 June 9, 2026 Regular City Council Meeting Agenda — — 2026-06-09 Upland Crossing Public Improvement District Advisory Board Regular Meeting Agenda — — 2026-06-08 June 2026 Library Board Meeting Agenda — — 2026-06-04 Transportation Advisory Committee Agenda — — 2026-06-02 Appointments Advisory Board - 11:30 AM Agenda — — 2026-05-28 Public Transit Advisory Board - 11:30 AM Agenda — — 2026-05-28 Veterans Advisory Committee - 12:00 PM Agenda — — 2026-05-27 Model Codes and construction Advisory Board-3:30PM *Canceled* Agenda — — 2026-05-27 Meet and Confer Negotiations - Police - 3:00 PM Agenda — — 2026-05-26 Regular City Council Meeting, May 26, 2026 Agenda — — 2026-05-21 ZBA meeting scheduled for 5-21-2026 in the City Council Chambers. Agenda — — 2026-05-20 Special City Council Meeting - Swearing-In Ceremony, May 20, 2026 Agenda — — 2026-05-20 Civil Service Commission - 8:30 AM Agenda — — 2026-05-20 Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee 12:00pm Agenda — — 2026-05-20 Lubbock Economic Development Alliance Board of Directors Regular Board Meeting Agenda — — 2026-05-20 Permit and License Appeal Board - 11:30 AM Agenda — — 2026-05-19 The Electric Utility Board of Lubbock Power & Light Meeting - 11:00 AM Agenda — — 2026-05-19 Citizen&#x27;s Traffic Commission Meeting Agenda — — 2026-05-19 Transportation Policy Committee Agenda — — 2026-05-19 Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission Agenda — — 2026-05-18 May 2026 Library Board Meeting Agenda — — 2026-05-15 Board of Health-12:30 PM. Agenda — — 2026-05-13 The Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board of Trustees will conduct a regular meeting on Wednesday, May 13, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. in the City Bank Conference Room, located at 611 University Avenue, Lubbock, TX, for the purposes set forth in the following agenda: Agenda — — 2026-05-13 Lubbock Emergency Communication District Board of Managers Meeting Agenda — — 2026-05-13 CANCELED - Meet and Confer Negotiations - Police - 12:00 PM Agenda — — 2026-05-12 Regular City Council Meeting, May 12, 2026 Agenda — — 2026-05-12 Public Hearing - Centrepointe Public Improvement District (PID) Creation - 2:00 PM Agenda — — 2026-05-12 Transportation Advisory Committee Agenda — — 2026-05-07 5.07.2026 Planning and Zoning Commission Meeting Agenda — — 2026-05-07 Planning and Zoning Commission Agenda — — 2026-05-05 Appointments Advisory Board – 11:30 AM Agenda — — 2026-04-29 Airport Advisory Board - 12:00PM Agenda — — 2026-04-28 Regular City Council Meeting, April 28, 2026 Agenda — — 2026-04-28 Lake Alan Henry Board of Appeals - 10:00 AM Agenda — — 2026-04-23 Planning and Zoning Commission Agenda — — 2026-04-23 Planning and Zoning Regular Meeting Agenda for April 23, 2026 Agenda — — 2026-04-23 Citizens Tower, 2nd Floor Conference Center Rooms 201A & 201B 1314 Avenue K Lubbock, TX 79401 Meeting Begins at 12:00pm Agenda — — 2026-04-22 Model Codes and Construction Advisory Board Agenda — — 2026-04-22 Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee Meeting - 12:00pm Agenda — — 2026-04-22 Lubbock Economic Development Alliance, Inc. (\"LEDA\") Board of Directors Regular Board Meeting Agenda — — 2026-04-20 Library Board-12:00pm, April 20, 2026 Agenda — — 2026-04-08 Community Development & Services Board Regular Meeting Agenda — — 2026-03-05 Planning and Zoning Commission Agenda — — 2026-03-05 Planning and Zoning Commission Regular Meeting Materials Agenda — — These are the City's documents, on the City's servers This page is an index, not a mirror. Each link goes to Lubbock's own copy so you are reading the authoritative version. When a document disappears from the City's site - and they do - tell us , because the City still holds the file whether or not it publishes it, and that makes it a records request."}, {"title": "Read the people who cover this every day", "url": "/news.html", "category": "Site page", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Local and statewide newsrooms covering Lubbock and Texas. We link out; we never republish another newsroom", "text": "Lubbock news & allies | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content Why we link instead of republish Local newsrooms are paid by attention. An aggregator that lifts a paragraph and keeps the reader on its own page takes that attention and gives nothing back, and then the newsroom shrinks and there is less reporting for everyone — including for us, because most of what a records request confirms was first noticed by a reporter. So the rule here is absolute: link out, never republish . The only documents hosted on this site are public records, which belong to the public already. Working on Lubbock? If you are a reporter, a nonprofit or a resident group working on something here, the archive and the request pipeline are yours to use, and we will file on your behalf if a request under your own name would cost you something. Get in touch ."}, {"title": "File a request the city has to answer", "url": "/records/", "category": "Open Records", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "How to file a public records request with the City of Lubbock under Tex. Gov", "text": "File a Texas Public Information Act request in Lubbock | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content Build your letter This runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you type here is sent anywhere, stored, or seen by us — copy the result and send it yourself from your own email. Your name Your email What you are asking for Date range Ask for electronic delivery Require a cost estimate before charges Require an itemised legal basis for anything withheld Copy the letter Write the request narrow, then widen it “All emails about the project” gets refused as overbroad and burns your deadline. “Emails between the City Manager and [named party] between 1 March and 30 April” gets answered. Once you see what came back, ask again with what you learned. Two narrow requests beat one that never gets filled. Where to send it Lubbock's own records channel is listed on the City Desk , with the exact URL the City publishes. Use it — a request filed through the body's own portal is the hardest one for it to say it never received. Send a copy to foia@moveweight.com at the same time. We log the send date, watch the deadline, and if it lapses we publish that fact. A request nobody knows about is a request the city can ignore quietly. Track the clock § 552.301 gives the City ten (10) business days. Put the send date in your calendar with that many days added, and put a second reminder a week later. Most requests are not denied — they are simply never answered, and the only thing that changes that is somebody noticing on the exact day. If you are refused Texas has no free citizen appeal board. If the City wants to withhold, it must ask the Attorney General for a ruling within ten business days (§ 552.301) and send you a copy of what it asked — that request and the ruling are both public, and they are the best record of what a city tried to hide. If the City simply never answers, a complaint goes to the county or district attorney under § 552.3215, or you can sue for mandamus under § 552.321. Keep the refusal Whatever the outcome, keep the exact wording of the denial and the date. A refusal that cites no statute, or cites one that does not apply, is itself the story — and it is the document an appeal turns on."}, {"title": "Search everything we publish", "url": "/search.html", "category": "Site page", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Search every page, document and record published by ExposeTexas — the Lubbock, Texas chapter of the Move Weight Foundation.", "text": "Search this site | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content MW A chapter of the Move Weight Foundation Lubbock, TX of 14 Oklahoma 4 Miami Oklahoma City Tulsa Claremore Texas 7 San Angelo Houston Dallas Austin San Antonio Lubbock Abilene Mississippi 3 Southaven Jackson Olive Branch All 14 chapters and the map → Search · Lubbock, TX Search everything we publish Every page and every document on this site, searched in your browser. Nothing you type is sent anywhere — the index downloads once and the search runs locally. Search Loading the index… Search tips Two or three words beats a sentence. Every word must appear somewhere in the page. Quote a phrase — \"no quid pro quo\" — to match it exactly. Filter by category using the buttons above the results. Agendas and minutes are indexed by their full text, so a phrase from inside a document will find the meeting that carried it. Those results open the city’s own copy — we index the words, they keep the file."}, {"title": "A city that owns its power company, on a shrinking aquifer", "url": "/the-question.html", "category": "Site page", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "The structural question this chapter was opened on, the specific public record that answers it, and what has been asked for so far.", "text": "What we are asking Lubbock | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content MW A chapter of the Move Weight Foundation Lubbock, TX of 14 Oklahoma 4 Miami Oklahoma City Tulsa Claremore Texas 7 San Angelo Houston Dallas Austin San Antonio Lubbock Abilene Mississippi 3 Southaven Jackson Olive Branch All 14 chapters and the map → The Question · Lubbock, TX A city that owns its power company, on a shrinking aquifer Lubbock runs a municipal electric utility and sits on the Ogallala. Industrial load growth — including data-centre-scale load — lands on ratepayers through the utility rather than through the tax roll, which means the decision that raises a household bill is made in a utility board packet, not in a budget hearing. This is a question, not an accusation No one at this Foundation has alleged that anybody in Lubbock has done anything wrong, and nothing on this site should be read that way. Chapters are opened on structural risk — large capital programmes, fast growth, a single dominant employer, a utility or authority that meets away from the council — because those are the conditions under which decisions get made with nobody watching. That is a statement about attention, not about anybody's conduct. Naming a city next to an accusation we have not documented would be precisely the thing this project exists to oppose. So the page you are reading names the question and the record that settles it, and then we go and ask for the record. The record that answers it Lubbock Power & Light board agendas and rate ordinances, and the City's water supply and wholesale-power contracts. How this gets resolved Ask. A request goes to the City of Lubbock under Tex. Gov't Code ch. 552. The City has ten (10) business days. Publish what comes back, whole. Released public records go up unredacted. We do not summarise a document in place of showing it, and we do not redact what the government already released. Publish what does not come back. A missed deadline, a refusal with no statute cited, an estimate that prices a resident out — those get published too, with dates. Silence is a finding. Rewrite this page. When the documents are in, this page stops being a question and becomes what the records showed — including if they showed nothing. A chapter that only ever reports wrongdoing is not doing arithmetic, it is doing marketing. What you can do right now You do not need us. The statute is yours, and a request from a resident of Lubbock is harder to wave away than one from a nonprofit two states over. Build the letter → Where to send it → Send us something anonymously →"}, {"title": "Send a tip", "url": "/tips.html", "category": "Site page", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "A confidential tip line for anyone with information about local government in Lubbock, Texas. We do not log IP addresses and we do not publish who sent us anything.", "text": "Send an anonymous tip | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content"}, {"title": "Watch your government", "url": "/watch.html", "category": "Site page", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Where to watch Lubbock government on video — meeting recordings, agendas, and the archives that keep them after the city takes them down.", "text": "Watch your government | Lubbock | ExposeTexas Skip to content MW A chapter of the Move Weight Foundation Lubbock, TX of 14 Oklahoma 4 Miami Oklahoma City Tulsa Claremore Texas 7 San Angelo Houston Dallas Austin San Antonio Lubbock Abilene Mississippi 3 Southaven Jackson Olive Branch All 14 chapters and the map → Video · Lubbock, TX Watch your government Almost every decision that costs you money is made in a room with a camera in it. The recordings are public. Most people never find them because they are buried three clicks deep on a government site. Here they are. Where the video lives These are Lubbock's own recordings and portals. We link to them rather than rehosting, so you are watching the authoritative copy. Meetings — agendas and video City of Lubbock Open → City news channel (MyLBK Connect) City of Lubbock Carries Council live and every past Council meeting. Open → Council meeting video archive City of Lubbock Swagit archive of Council meetings. Open → We link, we do not rehost These are the government's own recordings on the government's own platforms. We send you there rather than mirroring them, so you are watching the authoritative copy and the view count lands where it belongs. The exception is when a recording disappears — see below. When a recording goes missing Meeting video gets taken down. Sometimes it is a retention policy, sometimes a platform migration, sometimes nobody can say why. Either way the public record evaporates, and the one meeting you needed is the one that is gone. That is why the Foundation's Miami, Oklahoma chapter keeps 522 meetings archived with machine transcripts and captions , mirrored to the Internet Archive. It is the deepest archive we run and the model for every other chapter. See what a full archive looks like → If you spot a gap A meeting with no video, a recording that ends mid-item, a link that used to work — tell us . A missing recording is itself a records request: the city holds the raw file whether or not it publishes it, and we will ask for it. Getting more out of a meeting recording Read the agenda packet first. It usually contains the actual contract or ordinance. The video is people discussing a document you can already have in front of you. Note the timestamp of anything that matters. A citation like \"14 January, 1:42:10\" is something a reporter or an auditor can verify in a minute. Watch the vote, not the speech. Who moved it, who seconded, who was absent, and whether it was on a published agenda. Executive session is a flag, not a crime. Bodies may close a meeting for defined reasons and must certify which. If a payment or a contract was discussed behind a closed door, the certification is a public record. Ask for it → Follow this chapter No social accounts yet for this chapter. We publish to the site first. Social accounts are a distribution channel, never the record — anything we post there exists here with its source document attached."}, {"title": "2026-08-11 - Animal Services Advisory Board Regular Meeting Agenda August 11, 2026 11:30 a.m. 1306 9th Street Lubbock Public Library – Mahon Branch (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_08112026-161", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Animal Services Advisory Board Regular Meeting Agenda August 11, 2026 11:30 a.m. 1306 9th Street Lubbock Public Library – Mahon Branch 1. CALL TO ORDER 2. CITIZEN COMMENTS – According to Lubbock Animal Services Advisory Board Rules, any citizens wishing to appear in-person before a regular meeting of the Advisory Board, regarding any matter posted on the Advisory Board Agenda below, sh", "text": "Animal Services Advisory Board \nRegular Meeting Agenda \nAugust 11, 2026 \n11:30 a.m. \n1306 9th Street \nLubbock Public Library – Mahon Branch \n\n1. CALL TO ORDER \n \n2. CITIZEN COMMENTS – According to Lubbock Animal Services Advisory Board Rules, \nany citizens wishing to appear in-person before a regular meeting of the Advisory Board, \nregarding any matter posted on the Advisory Board Agenda below, shall complete the sign-\nup form provided at the meeting, no later than 11:30 a.m. on August 11, 2026. Citizen \nComments provide an opportunity for citizens to make comments and express a position on \nthe agenda items. Citizen comments will be limited to 3 minutes each. \n \n3. APPROVAL OF June 9, 2026, MEETING MINUTES \n \n4. WORK SESSION DIRECTOR’S REPORT \n \n4.1 Staffing \n4.1.1 \nVacant Positions \n4.2 Operations \n4.2.1 \nBite Cases \n4.2.2 \nBite Reporting FY 25/26 \n4.3 Performance Metrics \n4.4 Events \n \n5. GENERAL DISCUSSION \n \n6. DATE AND TIME OF NEXT MEETING \n \n7. ADJOURN \n \nThe above-referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted on the bulletin board outside the south entrance \nof Citizens Tower, 1314 Ave K, Lubbock, Texas, on the ___ day __________________ at ______AM/PM. \nBy: _____________________________________ \n \nAnimal Services Advisory Board meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you \nrequire special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806-775-2710 or write to P.O. Box \n2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79401, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-08-04 - 08-04-2026 Planning and Zoning Commission Regular Meeting (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_08042026-157", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Planning and Zoning Commission Agenda August 4, 2026 Regular Meeting 6:00 P.M. Citizens Tower Council Chambers 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas Commission members Chair James Bell Vice-Chair Tarek Redwan Renee Cage Kirk Franklin Scott Gloyna Drew Gray Terri Morris Stacey Rogers Robert Wood The Planning and Zoning Commission meetings are available to all persons regardless of disabi", "text": "Planning and Zoning Commission Agenda \nAugust 4, 2026 \nRegular Meeting 6:00 P.M. \nCitizens Tower Council Chambers \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n \nCommission members \nChair James Bell \nVice-Chair Tarek Redwan \nRenee Cage \nKirk Franklin \nScott Gloyna \nDrew Gray \nTerri Morris \nStacey Rogers \nRobert Wood \n \nThe Planning and Zoning Commission meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you \nrequire \nspecial \nassistance, \nplease \ncontact \nthe \nCity \nof \nLubbock \nat \n806-775-2108, \nemail \ncityplanning@mylubbock.us, or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours in advance \nof the meeting. \n \nThe Planning and Zoning Commission of the City of Lubbock, Texas reserves the right to meet in closed \nsession on any of the items listed below should the need arise and if applicable pursuant to authorization \nby Section 551.071 of the Texas Government Code. \n \nRegular Agenda \n \n1. Call to Order \n \n1.1 Citizen Comments – According to Lubbock Planning and Zoning Commission Rules, \nany citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Planning and Zoning \nCommission, regarding any matter posted on the Planning and Zoning Commission \nAgenda below, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than \n6:00 p.m. \n \n2. Approval of Minutes \n \n2.1 July 2, 2026, Regular Planning and Zoning Commission Meeting minutes. \n \n3. Zone Changes - Public Hearings \nPlanning & Zoning Commission makes recommendation; City Council has final authority for \napproval. \n\nDistrict 1 \n3.1 Zone Case 0470-A: Robert G Lines for Hicks Oilfield, LLC, request for a Specific Use \nfor a Permanent Batch Plant on property zoned General Industrial District (GI), at: \n• 1501 Marshall Street, located north of Grinnell Street and west of North Avenue \nN, on 4.359 acres of unplatted land out of Block A, Section 15. \nDistrict 1 - WITHDRAWN \n3.2 Zone Case 0809-B: AMD Engineering, LLC, for West Texas Land Guys, request for a \nzone change from Auto-Urban Commercial District (AC) to Low Density Single-Family \nDistrict (SF-2), at: \n• 2820 North University Avenue, located east of University Avenue and north of \nDrake Street, on 7.61 acres of unplatted land out of Block A, Section 11. \nDistrict 4 \n3.3 Zone Case 3310-E: AMD Engineering, LLC, for Craig Ray Custom Homes, request for \na zone change from Office District (OF) to Neighborhood Commercial District (NC), \nat: \n• 14002 Quaker Avenue, located south of 139th Street and west of Quaker Avenue, \non 1.723 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 5. \nDistrict 5 \n3.4 Zone Case 2538-TT: West Texas Engineering, LLC, for E.C. Hatton, request for a zone \nchange from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2) to Neighborhood Commercial \nDistrict (NC), at: \n• 7515 34th Street, located east of Alcove Avenue and south of 34th Street, on \n2.957 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 39. \nDistrict 5 \n3.5 Zone Case 3557: Seventeen Services, LLC, for PlainsCapital Bank, request for a zone \nchange from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2) to Auto-Urban Commercial \nDistrict (AC), at: \n• 6714 104th Street, located north of 104th Street and west of Milwaukee Avenue, \non 15.536 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 22. \nDistrict 5 \n3.6 Zone Case 3558: Rasmussen Development, Inc. for Timothy Mandrell, request for a \nzone change from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2) to High Density \nResidential District (HDR), at: \n\n• 6620 110th Street, located west of Milwaukee Avenue and north of 110th Street, \non 1.85 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 22. \nDistrict 5 \n3.7 Zone Case 3559: Hugo Reed and Associates, Inc. for Loop 88, LLC, request for a zone \nchange from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2) to General Industrial District \n(GI), at: \n• 7401 Alcove Avenue, located east of Alcove Avenue and north of 76th Street, \non 33.3 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 31. \nDistrict 6 \n \n3.8 Zone Case 1864-J: Tim Khater, request for a zone change from Medium Density \nResidential District (MDR) to Office District (OF), at: \n• 3418 La Salle Avenue, located south of 34th Street and west of La Salle Avenue, \nPark Lorraine Addition, Tract G. \nDistrict 6 \n3.9 Zone Case 2538-RR: Gary Garza, request for a zone change from Low Density \nSingle-Family District (SF-2) to Heavy Commercial District (HC), at: \n• 7509 and 7513 19th Street, located south of 19th Street and west of Wausau \nAvenue, on 2.5 acres of unplatted land out Block AK, Section 42. \n \nDistrict 6 \n \n 3.10 Zone Case 3289-B: AMD Engineering, LLC, for Betenbough Homes, LLC, request for a \nzone change from Auto-Urban Commercial District (AC) to Low Density Single-Family \nDistrict (SF-2), at: \n \n• 8101 4th Street, located south of 4th Street and west of Alcove Avenue, on 40.53 \nacres of unplatted land out of Block D-6, Section 2. \n \n4. Adjourn \n \nThe above-referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted on the south entrance of Citizens Tower, \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the_________ day of July at _________AM./P.M. \n\nBy: _____________________________________________"}, {"title": "2026-07-30 - Bell Farms Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting - 5:30 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07302026-123", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Bell Farms Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3082 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower, West of the revolving d", "text": "Bell Farms Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability.\nIf you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3082 or write to P.O. Box\n2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower, West of the\nrevolving doors at 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the _______ day of _________, 2026 at \n___________ A.M./P.M. by Molly Ballesteros.\nBell Farms Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 30, 2026\n5:30 P.M.\nLubbock Police Department South Division Station | Community Room\n14005 Indiana Ave, Lubbock, TX\n1.0\nWelcome\nTrace Moore, Chair\n2.0\nIntroduction of the Bell Farms PID Advisory Board Members\nTrace Moore, Chair\n3.0\nUpdate from the Board on Fiscal Year 2025-26 Board Activities and Projects\nTrace Moore, Chair\n4.0 Discussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan\nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n5.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the Bell Farms PID to voice concerns,\nsuggestions, and preferences for Bell Farms PID and the Bell Farms PID Service and\nAssessment Plan.\n6.0\nVoting on Three Positions for the Bell Farms PID Advisory Board"}, {"title": "2026-07-29 - Upland Crossing Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting - 5:30 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07292026-126", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Upland Crossing Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting Agenda July 29, 2026 5:30 P.M. City Council Chambers Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas 1.0 Welcome Austin Lear, Chair 2.0 Introduction of the Upland Crossing PID Advisory Board Members Austin Lear, Chair 3.0 Update from the Board of FY 2025-26 Board Activities and Projects Austin Lear 4.0 Discussion of Proposed Fisca", "text": "Upland Crossing Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 29, 2026\n5:30 P.M.\nCity Council Chambers\n Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas\n1.0\nWelcome\nAustin Lear, Chair\n2.0\nIntroduction of the Upland Crossing PID Advisory Board Members\nAustin Lear, Chair\n3.0 Update from the Board of FY 2025-26 Board Activities and Projects\n Austin Lear\n4.0 Discussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n5.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the Upland Crossing PID to voice concerns,\nsuggestions, and preferences for Upland Crossing PID and the Upland Crossing PID\nService and Assessment Plan.\n6.0 Voting on Two Positions for the Upland Crossing PID Advisory Board\nUpland Crossing Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of\ndisability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write to\nP.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at 1314\nAvenue K, West of the revolving doors, Lubbock, Texas, on the ______ day of ______, 2026 at\n_______AM/PM By: Molly Ballesteros"}, {"title": "2026-07-29 - Public Transit Advisory Board - 11:30 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07292026-159", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "NOTICE OF LUBBOCK PUBLIC TRANSIT ADVISORY BOARD MEETING JULY 29, 2026 – 11:30 A.M. CITIBUS CONFERENCE ROOM, 801 TEXAS LUBBOCK, TEXAS 1. Call to Order 2. Citizen Comments - According to HB 2840, any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Public Transit Advisory Board, regarding any matter posted on the Public Transit Advisory Board Agenda, shall sign-up no later than 1", "text": "NOTICE OF LUBBOCK PUBLIC TRANSIT ADVISORY BOARD MEETING \nJULY 29, 2026 – 11:30 A.M. \nCITIBUS CONFERENCE ROOM, 801 TEXAS \nLUBBOCK, TEXAS \n \n1. Call to Order \n \n2. Citizen Comments - According to HB 2840, any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting \nof the Public Transit Advisory Board, regarding any matter posted on the Public Transit Advisory \nBoard Agenda, shall sign-up no later than 11:15 a.m. on the day of the meeting by the following \nmethods: \n2.1.1. E-mail sgarrett@citibus.com; \n2.1.2. Call Citibus at 806-775-3640; or \n2.1.3. In person at 801 Texas Avenue \n \n3. Ceremonial Items \n \n3.1. Employee Recognition \n \n4. Minutes \n\n4.1. Consider approval of the minutes of the July 1, 2026 regular meeting of the Lubbock Public \nTransit Advisory Board (TAB) \n \n5. Regular Session/Action Items \n \n5.1. Acceptance of Citibus Financial and Operating Reports \n \n5.2. Consider Approval of the Route and Network Redesign Phase 1 Implementation Plan \n \n5.3. Consider Ratification/Recommendation of Items Presented to City Council for Approval \n5.3.1 Ratify - Approval of Purchase Order Contract No. 27126858 with Gillig, LLC for the \npurchase of six (6) 40' hybrid-electric buses and two (2) 35' hybrid-electric buses. \n5.3.2 Recommendation - Resolution authorizing the approval of a FY 2027 Section 5307 \nGrant with the Texas Department of Transportation for operating assistance \n \n6. Work Session \n \n6.1. Update on FY 2027 Citibus Operating Budget \n \n6.2. Update on Citibus Marketing \n \n6.3. Update on Citibus Operations and Safety \n \n6.4. Update on Citibus Maintenance Operations \n \n6.5. General Manager Update \n\n7. Adjourn \nNOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the South entrance \nof Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the 23rd \nday of July 2026 at ______P.M. \n \nBy: ______________________________________ \n Shannon Garrett, Board Support Staff Member \n \nThe Lubbock Public Transit Advisory Board meetings are available to all persons regardless of \ndisability. If you require special assistance, please contact Shannon Garrett at (806) 775-3434, or \nwrite to her at P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-07-29 - Permit and License Appeal Board - 11:30 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07292026-155", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "1. Call to order 2. Public Comment - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Permit & License Appeal Board regarding any matter posted on the Permit & License Appeal Board Agenda may speak. 3. Minutes 3.1 June 17, 2026 Permit & License Appeal Board Meeting 4. Regular Session 4.1 Lubbock Animal Services Appeals 4.1.1 The Permit & License Appeal Board w", "text": "1. \nCall to order \n \n2. \nPublic Comment - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Permit \n& License Appeal Board regarding any matter posted on the Permit & License Appeal \nBoard Agenda may speak. \n \n3. \nMinutes \n \n3.1 \nJune 17, 2026 Permit & License Appeal Board Meeting \n \n4. \nRegular Session \n\n4.1 \nLubbock Animal Services Appeals \n \n4.1.1 The Permit & License Appeal Board will hear the appeal of Roger Settler \nregarding the decision made by Lubbock Animal Services, declaring a \npublic nuisance at 2120 Broadway Street, in accordance with Chapter 4 of \nthe City of Lubbock Code of Ordinances, and render a decision in \nconnection therewith. \n\nGarrott Nalle, Chair \nJillian Nava, Vice Chair \nCecile Garcia \nBrooks Jones \nThomas Nichols \nDavid Saunders \nDonald Stubbs \n \nPermit & License Appeal Board \nAgenda \nJuly 29, 2026 \n11:30 A.M. \nCourtney Paz, City Secretary \nJimmy Maynard, Deputy City \nSecretary \nRyan Brooke, Assistant City \nAttorney \n \nCitizens Tower \nCity Council Chambers, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n\nPermit & License Appeal Board meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special \nassistance, please contact the Office of the City Secretary at 806.775.2061, e-mail ocs@mylubbock.us, or write to \nPost Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \nExecutive Session Disclosure Statement: The Permit & License Appeal Board reserves the right to recess into \nexecutive session at any time during the course of the meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized \nby Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code, including but not necessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with \nAttorney), §551.072 (Deliberations regarding Real Property), §551.073 (Deliberations regarding Gifts and \nDonations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076 (Deliberations regarding Security Devices), §551.086 (Public \nPower Utilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations regarding Economic Development Negotiations)."}, {"title": "2026-07-29 - Model Codes and Construction Advisory Board Meeting 3:30pm (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07292026-146", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Current Board Members Gary Ferguson, Chair James Jordan, Vice Chair Kendall Wood, Master Electrician Ryan Farquhar, Commercial Contractor David Small, Engineer Logan West, Facilities Manager Craig Ray, WTHBA Brian Robertson, Engineer Wendell Burris, Lubbock Apartment Marvin White, Licensed HVAC George Henson, Licensed Plumber Jacob Cline, Electrician Model Codes and Construction Advi", "text": "Current Board Members \n\nGary Ferguson, Chair \nJames Jordan, Vice Chair \nKendall Wood, Master Electrician \nRyan Farquhar, Commercial Contractor \nDavid Small, Engineer \nLogan West, Facilities Manager \nCraig Ray, WTHBA \nBrian Robertson, Engineer \nWendell Burris, Lubbock Apartment \nMarvin White, Licensed HVAC \nGeorge Henson, Licensed Plumber \nJacob Cline, Electrician \nModel Codes and Construction Advisory - Meeting Agenda \nJuly 29, 2026, 3:30 P.M. \nCitizens Tower \n1st Floor Council Workroom 101A \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \nModel Codes and Construction Advisory Board meetings are available to all \npeople regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact \nthe Building Safety Department at 806.775.3191, e-mail \nmvillagrana@mylubbock.us, or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas \n79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \nCall to Order for Regular Meeting \n1. \nApproval of Minutes \na. \nJune 24, 2026, Regular meeting \nAction Items \n2. \nThe Board will discuss and take appropriate action on the following items: \nDates at the end of each code set are proposed for presentations (may fluctuate) \n \na. \n 2021/2024 International Existing Building Code – Completed -July 2025 \n \nb. \n2021/2024 International Building Code-Completed- January 2026 \nc. \n2021/2024 International Fire Code – Partial Completion -January 2026 \nd. \n2021/2024 International Fuel Gas Code – Completed-March 2026 \ne. \n2021/2024 International Swimming Pool and Spa Code – Completed-June \n2026 \nf. \n2021/2024 International Plumbing Code-Completed- June 2026 \ng. \n2021/2024 International Mechanical Code – Completed-June 2026 \n\nh. \n2021/2024 International Residential Code- July 2026 \ni. \n2020/2023/2026 National Electric Code – August 2026 \nj. \n2021/2024 International Energy Conservation Code –August 2026 \n \nNext meeting is scheduled for August 26, 2026."}, {"title": "2026-07-29 - Meet and Confer Negotiations - Police - 3:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07292026-158", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Notice of Open Deliberations Pursuant to Tex. Loc. Gov’t Code § 142.063, this public notice is provided relating to meet and confer deliberations between: City of Lubbock, Texas and Lubbock Professional Police Association Date/Time: July 29, 2026 at 3:00 P.M. Location: Citizens Tower, 2nd Floor Conference Center, 201A/B, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas 79401 Agenda: Deliberations and/o", "text": "Notice of Open Deliberations \nPursuant to Tex. Loc. Gov’t Code § 142.063, this public notice is provided relating to meet and confer \ndeliberations between: \nCity of Lubbock, Texas \nand \nLubbock Professional Police Association \n \nDate/Time: \nJuly 29, 2026 at 3:00 P.M. \nLocation: \nCitizens Tower, 2nd Floor Conference Center, 201A/B, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n79401 \nAgenda: \nDeliberations and/or related action relating to an agreement or a proposed agreement \nbetween the City of Lubbock, Texas (“City”) and the Lubbock Professional Police \nAssociation (“Association”) as provided for in Texas Local Government Code Chapter \n142, Subchapter B. \nPublic Notice: \nMembers of the public and press are encouraged to observe and monitor the deliberation. A recording of \nthe public portion of the meeting will be made available, but active participation in discussions or \npresentations is expressly prohibited unless permitted by the Ground Rules established by the City and the \nAssociation. \nRepresentatives for either the City or Association may suspend the meeting at any time during the \nproceedings for consultation with their attorney or to have a private caucus with their respective \nrepresentatives. Pursuant to Section 551.071 of the Texas Government Code and Section 142.063(b) of the \nTexas Local Government Code the deliberations during this period are closed to the public \nThe use of cellular phones or sound activated pagers is prohibited while deliberations are being conducted. \nPlease step outside the meeting room should you elect to utilize these devices. \nThis meeting is available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please \ncontact the City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas \n79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-07-29 - Meet and Confer Negotiations - Fire - 11:00 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07292026-160", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Notice of Open Deliberations Pursuant to Tex. Loc. Gov’t Code § 142.113, this public notice is provided relating to meet and confer deliberations between: City of Lubbock, Texas and Lubbock Professional Firefighters Association Date/Time: July 29, 2026 at 11:00 A.M. Location: Citizens Tower, 2nd Floor Conference Center, 201A and 201B, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, TX 79401 Agenda: Delibera", "text": "Notice of Open Deliberations \nPursuant to Tex. Loc. Gov’t Code § 142.113, this public notice is provided relating to meet and confer \ndeliberations between: \nCity of Lubbock, Texas \nand \nLubbock Professional Firefighters Association \n \nDate/Time: \nJuly 29, 2026 at 11:00 A.M. \nLocation: \nCitizens Tower, 2nd Floor Conference Center, 201A and 201B, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, \nTX 79401 \nAgenda: \nDeliberations and/or related action relating to an agreement or a proposed agreement \nbetween the City of Lubbock, Texas (“City”) and the Lubbock Professional Firefighters \nAssociation (“Association”) as provided for in Texas Local Government Code Chapter \n142, Subchapter C. \nPublic Notice: \nMembers of the public and press are encouraged to observe and monitor the deliberation. A recording of \nthe public portion of the meeting will be made available, but active participation in discussions or \npresentations is expressly prohibited unless permitted by the Ground Rules established by the City and the \nAssociation. \nRepresentatives for either the City or Association may suspend the meeting at any time during the \nproceedings for consultation with their attorney. Pursuant to Section 551.071 of the Texas Government \nCode, the deliberations during this period are closed to the public. \nThe use of cellular phones or sound activated pagers is prohibited while deliberations are being conducted. \nPlease step outside the meeting room should you elect to utilize these devices. \nThis meeting is available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please \ncontact the City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas \n79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-07-29 - Central Business District Tax Increment Financing Reinvestment Zone Advisory Board - 7:30 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07292026-116", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the South entrance of Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______________, 2026 at___________ A.M./P.M. _________________________________________ Dan Williams, Chair Jeff Dane, Vice Chair Jason Coleman Jeremy Hamilton Brett McDowell Judge Curtis Parrish Robert Taylor Laura Vinson Gabe Vitela Cen", "text": "The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the South entrance of Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, \nLubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______________, 2026 at___________ A.M./P.M. \n\n_________________________________________ \n \nDan Williams, Chair \nJeff Dane, Vice Chair \nJason Coleman \nJeremy Hamilton \nBrett McDowell \nJudge Curtis Parrish \nRobert Taylor \nLaura Vinson \nGabe Vitela \n\nCentral Business District Tax Increment Financing \nReinvestment Zone Advisory Board Agenda \n July 29, 2026 | 7:30 A.M. \nCitizens Tower 1st Floor, Room 101A \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n \nDanielle Williams, Business \nDevelopment Director \nMolly Ballesteros, Special \nDistricts Manager \n\nCentral Business District Tax Increment Financing Reinvestment Zone Board meetings are available to all persons regardless \nof disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the Business Development Department at 806.775.3082, e-mail \nbusinessdevelopment@mylubbock.us, or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of \nthe meeting. \n \n1. Call to order \n \n2. Citizen Comments - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the CBD TIF Advisory Board, \nregarding any matter posted on the agenda below, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no \nlater than 7:30 a.m. Speakers may only address items listed on the current agenda. Speakers are limited to three \n(3) minutes maximum, and if there are more than three speakers who wish to address the same item, the Board \nreserves the right to limit speakers. THE BOARD CANNOT DELIBERATE OR ACT ON MATTERS NOT \nLISTED ON THE AGENDA. \n \n3. Regular Session \n \n3.1. Approval of Minutes – March 26, 2026 \n \n3.2. Discuss and take appropriate action on the Financial Report \n \n3.3. Discuss and take appropriate action on the Fiscal Year 2026-27 Budget \n \n4. Work Session \n \n4.1. Convention Center Project Presentation \n \n4.2. Construction within the TIF – Gilbert Quant, Chief Building Official \n \n4.3. Public Improvement Project Updates – Danielle Williams, Business Development Director \n \n4.4. Chairman’s Report – Dan Williams \n \n5. Schedule Next Meeting – The suggested meeting date is October 7, 2026, at 7:30 A.M."}, {"title": "2026-07-29 - Airport Advisory Board - 12:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07292026-154", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Airport Advisory Board Agenda July 29, 2026 12:00 P.M. – Airport Blue Room 5401 North Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd. Lubbock, TX Any citizen wishing to address the Airport Advisory Board, regarding any matter posted on this Agenda, may submit a public comment by email to tshelley@mylubbock.us, no later than 4:00 p.m. on July 28, 2026. Submitted comments must include name and address. Each co", "text": "Airport Advisory Board Agenda \nJuly 29, 2026 \n12:00 P.M. – Airport Blue Room \n5401 North Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd. Lubbock, TX \n \nAny citizen wishing to address the Airport Advisory Board, regarding any matter posted on this Agenda, \nmay submit a public comment by email to tshelley@mylubbock.us, no later than 4:00 p.m. on July 28, \n2026. Submitted comments must include name and address. Each comment will be read aloud for a period \nnot to exceed three minutes pursuant to Airport Advisory Board Rules. Public Comment is an opportunity \nfor citizens to make comments and express a position on agenda items. Any comment received that is in \nthe nature of asking a question will be referred to an appropriate member of the City staff for a response. \n \n1) Call to Order \nWelcome and Introductions \n \n2) Approval of Minutes \nJune 24, 2026 \n \n3) Citizen Comments \n \n4) Action Items \n \n4.1 \nConsider a recommendation to City Council authorizing the Mayor to execute Job Order \nContract (JOC) 19808 with JK Dennard Construction, LLC for Repairs to Rental Car \nCarwash at Lubbock Preston Smith International Airport. \n \n4.2 \nConsider a recommendation to City Council authorizing the Mayor to accept a Federal \nAviation Grant Agreement for the Reconstruction Taxiway M, F, R – Phase 1 Preliminary \nEngineer’s Report (PER) at Lubbock Preston Smith International Airport. \n \n4.3 \nConsider a recommendation to City Council authorizing the Mayor to execute Work Order \nNo. 004 with RS&H, Inc. for the LBB Taxiway M, F, R - Rehabilitation Preliminary \nEngineer’s Report (PER) at Lubbock Preston Smith International Airport. \n \n4.4 \nConsider approval of the Certificated Passenger Airline Operating Agreement and \nTerminal Building Lease for Signatory Airline Carriers at Lubbock Preston Smith \nInternational Airport. \n \n4.5 \nConsider appointing a committee to recommend nominations for Airport Board Officers \nfor Fiscal Year 2026-27. \n \n4.6 \nBudget presentation for Fiscal Year 2026-27. \n \n5) Work Session \n \n5.1 \nAdministration Report – Melissa Trevino \n5.2 \nOperations Report – Chuck Farina \n5.3 \nExecutive Director’s Report – Kelly Campbell \n\n6) Adjourn \nNext Airport Advisory Board Meeting will be held August 26, 2026."}, {"title": "2026-07-28 - Regular City Council Meeting, July 28, 2026 (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07282026-156", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Regular City Council Meeting July 28, 2026 Mark W. McBrayer, Mayor Dr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5 Christy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1 Gordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 David Bruegel, Councilman, District 3 Brayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 Tim Collins, Councilman, District 6 W. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager Matt Wade, City Attorney Cou", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nRegular City Council Meeting\nJuly 28, 2026\nMark W. McBrayer, Mayor\nDr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5\nChristy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1\nGordon Harris, Councilman, District 2\nDavid Bruegel, Councilman, District 3\nBrayden Rose, Councilman, District 4\nTim Collins, Councilman, District 6\n \nW. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager\nMatt Wade, City Attorney\nCourtney Paz, City Secretary\nhttp://www.mylubbock.us\nCity Council Chambers, Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas\n \nCity of Lubbock City Council Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance,\nplease contact the City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48\nhours in advance of the meeting. \nExecutive Session Disclosure Statement: The City Council reserves the right to adjourn into executive session at any time\nduring the course of the meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized by Chapter 551 of the Texas\nGovernment Code, including but not necessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with Attorney), §551.072 (Deliberations\nregarding Real Property), §551.073 (Deliberations regarding Gifts and Donations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076\n(Deliberations regarding Security Devices), §551.086 (Public Power Utilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations\nregarding Economic Development Negotiations). \nA quorum of the City Council will be physically present in City Council Chambers located in Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K,\nLubbock, Texas, as it is the intent of the City Council to have a quorum physically present at this location. One or more\nmembers of the City Council, however, may participate in the meeting by video-conference call as permitted under Section\n551.127 of the Texas Government Code. \nNote: On occasion, the City Council may consider agenda items out of order. \n. \n \n12:00 p.m. - City Council convenes in City Council Chambers and immediately recesses into\nExecutive Session.\n\n1.\nExecutive Session\n \n1. 1.\nHold an executive session in accordance with Texas Government Code, Section 551.071, with legal counsel for\nadvice about pending or contemplated litigation or settlement agreement and on matters in which the duty of the\nattorney under the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct conflicts with Chapter 551. \n \n1. 1.\n1.\nLake 7\n \n1. 1.\n2.\nTexas Tech Parkway\n \n1. 1.\n3.\n82nd Street: Martin Luther King, Jr. Boulevard to I-27 Street Project\n\n1. 1.\n4.\nMartin Luther King, Jr. Boulevard: 74th Street to 82nd Street Project \n \n1. 1.\n5.\nUpland Avenue Project - 66th Street to 82nd Street Project\n \n1. 2.\nHold an executive session in accordance with the Texas Government Code, Section 551.072, to deliberate the\npurchase, exchange, lease, or value of real property. \n \n1. 2.\n1.\nLake 7\n \n1. 2.\n2.\nTexas Tech Parkway\n \n1. 2.\n3.\n82nd Street: Martin Luther King, Jr. Boulevard to I-27 Street Project\n \n1. 2.\n4.\nMartin Luther King, Jr. Boulevard: 74th Street to 82nd Street Project \n \n1. 2.\n5.\nUpland Avenue Project - 66th Street to 82nd Street Project\n \n1. 2.\n6.\nLP&L Purchase of Property\n \nAdjourn from Executive Session\n \n2:00 p.m. - City Council reconvenes in Open Session in City Council Chambers.\n \n2.\nCeremonial Items\n \n2. 1.\nInvocation \n \n2. 2.\nPledges of Allegiance \n \nCall to Order\n \n3.\nCitizen Comments - According to Lubbock City Council Rules, any citizen wishing to appear in-person\nbefore a regular meeting of the City Council, regarding any matter posted on the City Council Agenda\nbelow, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than 2:00 p.m. on July 28,\n2026. Citizen Comments provide an opportunity for citizens to make comments and express a position on\nagenda items. \n \n4.\nMinutes\n\n4. 1.\nJune 23, 2026 Regular City Council Meeting \n \n5.\nConsent Agenda - Items considered to be routine are enacted by one motion without separate discussion. If\nthe City Council desires to discuss an item, the item is removed from the Consent Agenda and considered\nseparately.\n \n5. 1.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance No. 2026-O0082,\nAmendment 31, respecting the STD/HIV-DIS Grant Fund for the appropriation of funding from the Texas\nDepartment of State Health Services; providing for filing; and providing for a savings clause. \n \n5. 2.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance No. 2026-O0083,\nAmendment 32, respecting the Bell Farms Public Improvement District Fund, increasing the appropriation for\npublic improvements related to removing a damaged retaining wall and adding pedestrian lighting at Playa Lake\nPark; providing for filing; and providing for a savings clause. \n \n5. 2.\n1.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Ordinance No.2026-O0084, amending Ordinance No.\n2025-O0108, Exhibit “B”, the 2025 Service and Assessment Plan for the Bell Farms Public Improvement District,\nby including additional funding for public improvements for Contract 19454, related to public improvements at\nthe Playa Lake Park; providing for filing; and providing for a savings clause. \n \n5. 2.\n2.\nResolution - Business Development: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Contract 19454,\nand related documents, with West Texas Services, Inc. dba Tom's Tree Place, for Bell Farms Public Improvment\nDistrict Playa Lake Retaining Wall Construction. \n \n5. 3.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance No. 2026-O0085,\nAmendment 33, respecting the Cemetery Fund and Cemetery Capital Project Fund in connection with Capital\nImprovement Project 92889, Cemetery Irrigation System; providing for filing; and providing for a savings clause. \n \n5. 4.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance No. 2026-O0086,\nAmendment 34, respecting the General Fund and General Facilities System Improvements Capital Project Fund in\nconnection with Capital Improvement Project 8675, Employee Education Assistance Program - ARPA; providing\nfor filing; and providing for a savings clause. \n \n5. 5.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0087, abandoning and closing a\nportion of a cross access easement and drainage easement, located in the Action Gypsum Addition in Section 21,\nBlock E-2, north of 116th Street, and east of Indiana Avenue; providing a savings clause; and providing for\npublication. \n \n5. 6.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0088, and related documents,\nabandoning and closing a portion of a slope and drainage easement on the southwest corner of 98th Street and\nMilwaukee Avenue, in Section 22, Block AK, Lubbock; providing for filing; and providing for a savings clause. \n \n5. 7.\nOrdinance 1st Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider an ordinance, and related documents, abandoning and closing\nof an alley located between Snow Davis Addition and Lot 1 McGuire Addition located in Section 44, Block AK,\nLubbock County, Texas, south of 19th Street and west of Frankford Avenue; providing for filing; and providing\nfor a savings clause. \n\n5. 8.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Memorandum of\nAgreement, with Klein Commercial Properties, LLC., a Texas limited liability company, for the purchase of a\n0.204-acre tract of land located in Section 22, Block AK, Lubbock County, Texas (Parcel 22), located on the east\nside of Upland Avenue, south of 109th Street, north of 114th Street, to be utilized for the 2024 Street Bond\nProject. \n \n5. 9.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Memorandum of\nAgreement, with Klein Commercial Properties, LLC., a Texas limited liability company, for the purchase of a\n0.139-acre tract of land located in Section 22, Block AK, Lubbock County, Texas (Parcel 23), located on the east\nside of Upland Avenue, north of 109th Street, south of 105th Street, to be utilized for the 2024 Street Bond\nProject. \n \n5. 10.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Amendment No. 1 to\nProfessional Services Agreement, Contract 18233, and related documents, by and between the City of Lubbock\nand Freese and Nichols, Inc., for the Final Design, Bid, and Construction Phase Services for the 66th Street\nDrainage Channel Project, to extend the period of time for performance and expand the scope of engineering\nservices to be rendered. \n \n5. 11.\nResolution - Public Works, Paved Streets: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Public\nWorks Contract 19692, by and between the City of Lubbock and Lone Star Dirt & Paving Ltd., for Asphalt at\nVarious Locations - 2026 Contractor Rebuilds Project. \n \n5. 12.\nResolution - Public Health Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Amendment No. 2\nto the Health and Human Services Commission Grant Agreement, Contract No. HHS001081400002, and all\nrelated documents, by and between the City of Lubbock and the Texas Health and Human Services Commission,\nunder the Prevention and Behavioral Health Promotion Grant Program. \n \n5. 13.\nResolution - Public Health Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Amendment No. 1\nto the Subaward Agreement No. UTAUS-SUB00001984, and all related documents under the Health and Human\nServices Commission's Innovative Healing Center Project - Youth Expansion Strategy, by and between the City\nof Lubbock and the University of Texas at Austin, under the Health and Human Services Commission's\nInnovative Healing Center Project - Youth Expansion Strategy. \n \n5. 14.\nResolution - Public Health Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Amendment No. 8\nto the Department of State Health Services Contract No. HHS001120300005, and all related documents, by and\nbetween the City of Lubbock and the Texas Department of State Health Services, for the Sexually Transmitted\nDisease/Human Immunodeficiency Virus Disease Intervention Specialists Contract Grant Program. \n \n5. 15.\nResolution - Community Development: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Professional\nServices Consulting Agreement, Contract No. 19694, and all related documents, by and between the City of\nLubbock, Community Development and Civitas Consulting, LLC, for consulting and oversight services for U.S.\nDepartment of Housing and Urban Development Grants. \n \n5. 16.\nResolution - Public Transit Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor, or his designee, to execute\nan interlocal agreement, by and between the City of Lubbock and Texas Tech University, for Transit System\nManagement for campus bus service. \n\n5. 17.\nResolution - Public Transit Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Purchase Order\n27126858, under Federal Transit Administration Grant 5339, with Gillig, LLC, for the purchase of six 40-foot\nhybrid-electric buses, and two 35-foot hybrid-electric buses for Citibus. \n \n5. 18.\nResolution - Fleet Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Contract 19712 with AZ Bus\nSales, Inc., Contract 19713 with Briggs Truck and Rail, Contract 19714 with Lubbock Motors-F, Inc. dba Group\n1 Ford of Hub City, Contract 19715 with Integrity Auto Service, Contract 19716 with Nebraskaland dba\nMcWhorter’s Tire and Service, Contract 19717 with Pinkerton’s Garage, Contract 19718 with Hometown Tire\ndba Sundown Tire and Lube, LLC, and Contract 19589 with Williams Brake Tune & Tire, LLC, for the\nmaintenance and repair of City Light and Medium-Duty Vehicles.\n \n5. 19.\nResolution - Police: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Contract 19638, with West Texas\nMowing, LLC, to provide Landscaping and Ground Maintenance Services for North, South, and East Patrol\nDivision Stations. \n \n5. 20.\nResolution - Lubbock Power & Light: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a contract, by\nand between Alliance Credit Union and the City of Lubbock, a Texas home-rule municipal corporation, regarding\na tract of land to be used for a Lubbock Power & Light future substation, approving and holding a structure\nauthorizing all payments for and any associated costs and transfer fees, for land purchases, and recommending\napproval by the City Council. \n \n6.\nRegular Agenda\n \n6. 1.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 1): Consider a request for Zone Case 3555, a request of Hugo Reed and\nAssociates, Inc. for Lubbock Economic Development Alliance, for a zone change from Low Density\nSingle-Family District (SF-2) to General Industrial District (GI), generally located north of Keuka Street and west\nof I-27, on 52.4 acres of unplatted land out of Block A, Section 35, and consider an ordinance. \n \n6. 2.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 1): Consider a request for Zone Case 3145-H, a request of Brad Andrews\nRealty, LLC, for Borde Management Corp, for a zone change from Broadway District (MU-2) to Neighborhood\nCommercial District (NC), at 1701 Broadway, 1705 Broadway, and 1216 Avenue Q, located south of Broadway\nand west of Avenue Q, Overton Addition, Block 11, Lots 1 through 5, and consider an ordinance. \n \n6. 3.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 5): Consider a request for Zone Case 3417-B, a request of Centerline for\nAmicus Trust Company, LLC, FBO Holly Betenbough Self-Directed IRA, for a zone change from Office District\n(OF) to Light Industrial District (LI), at 6605, 6607, 6609, 6611, 6803, and 6805 Alcove Avenue and 7804 68th\nStreet, located east of Alcove Avenue and north of 69th Street, West Business Park Addition, Tracts B,C,D,E,F,Z,\nand AA, and consider an ordinance. \n \n6. 4.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 5): Consider a request for Zone Case 3556, a request of Hugo Reed and\nAssociates, Inc. for Duhan Family Limited Partnership, for a zone change from Low Density Single-Family\nDistrict (SF-2) to Neighborhood Commercial District (NC) and Auto-Urban Commercial District (AC), at 7801\n34th Street, and 7301, 7402, 7403, 7502, and 7528 50th Street, located west of Upland Avenue and north and\nsouth of 50th Street, on 75.8 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Sections 34 and 39, and consider an\nordinance. \n \n6. 5.\nPublic Hearing - Planning: Hold a public hearing and consider an ordinance to amend Sections 39.02.016 and\n39.2.004.a.3 of the Unified Development Code to allow Manufactured Homes as a permitted use in accordance\nwith the requirements of Texas Senate Bill 785, effective September 1, 2026. \n\n6. 6.\nPublic Hearing - Planning: Hold a public hearing and consider an ordinance for a request for annexation from\nLubbock Economic Development Alliance, Inc., to annex an area of approximately 7.92 acres, adjacent to the\nnorthern city limits of the City of Lubbock, south of Drew Street and west of Interstate 27, into Lubbock's\ncorporate limits. \n \n6. 7.\nPublic Hearing - Business Development: Hold a public hearing on the proposed 2026 assessment rate for the\nCenterpointe Public Improvement District (PID), and consider an ordinance reviewing classifications for the\nmethods of assessing special benefits for the services and improvements of property in the Centerpointe PID;\napproving, adopting, and filing with the City Secretary the assessment role; levying 2026 assessments for the cost\nof certain services and improvements to be provided in the district during FY 2026-27; fixing charges and liens\nagainst the property in the district and against the owners thereof; and providing for the collection of the\nassessment."}, {"title": "2026-07-28 - Public Hearing for the Review of the Assessment Rate for the Centerpointe Public Improvement District - 2:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07282026-138", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Notice of Public Hearing for the Review of the Assessment Rate for the Centerpointe Public Improvement District Please take notice that on July 28, 2026, at 2:00 P.M., the City Council of Lubbock, Texas, will conduct a public hearing in the City Council Chamber of Citizens Tower at 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, TX 79401. The purpose of this hearing is to consider the 2026 assessment rate for property o", "text": "Notice of Public Hearing for the Review of the Assessment Rate for the \nCenterpointe Public Improvement District\nPlease take notice that on July 28, 2026, at 2:00 P.M., the City Council of Lubbock, Texas, will conduct a public\nhearing in the City Council Chamber of Citizens Tower at 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, TX 79401. The purpose of this\nhearing is to consider the 2026 assessment rate for property owners in the Centerpointe Public Improvement District\n(“District”) pursuant to Chapter 372 of the Texas Local Government Code (The “Act”).\nAll interested persons will be given the opportunity to appear and be heard at such public hearing. Written or oral\nobjections will be considered. The Assessment roll will be available for viewing in the City Secretary’s Office after\nJuly 1, 2026.\nThe following information is hereby provided concerning the matters to be considered at such hearing:\nNature of the Services and Improvements: The general nature of the proposed public improvements is: (i)\nacquisition, construction, or drainage facilities or improvements, the design, construction, and maintenance of parks\nand green spaces, together with any ancillary structures, features or amenities such as playgrounds, splash pads,\npool(s), receptacles and any similar items located there in along with all necessary grading, drainage, and similar\ninfrastructure involved in the construction of such parks and greens; landscaping, hardscaping and irrigation; and\n(ii) payment of costs associated with developing and financing the public improvements listed in subdivision,\nincluding costs of establishing, administering and operating the District. The District is to supplement and enhance\nservices within the District, but not replace or supplant existing City services provided within the District.\nEstimated Cost of the Services and Improvements: The total estimated cost of services provided by the District\nis estimated at $168,534 over the next five years. The estimated annual cost rises from $0 in FY 2026-27 to $72,320 in\nFY 2030-31. The District shall not incur bonded indebtedness. The costs are based on a proposed assessment rate of\n$0.17 per $100.00 of valuation through tax year 2030.\nBoundaries: The District is located within the City of Lubbock, Texas. The proposed Centerpointe Public\nImprovement District (PID) generally covers a portion of an area in the City of Lubbock, Lubbock County, Texas,\ngenerally bounded on the east by North Avenue U, on the south by Drake Street, on the west by North University,\nand on the north by Georgetown Street. A detailed map of the District and a legal description as well as the\nAssessment Roll is available in the Business Development Department on the 9th Floor of Citizens Tower.\nPublic hearings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact\nthe City Secretary’s Office at 775-2025 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours in\nadvance of the public hearing.\nAny questions concerning the matters described in this notice should be directed to: Molly Ballesteros, City of\nLubbock Special Districts Manager at 775-3083."}, {"title": "2026-07-28 - Public Hearing for the Reauthorization of the City's Industrial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria - 2:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07282026-140", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Public Hearing for the Reauthorization of the City's Industrial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria July 28, 2026 at 2:00 P.M. Citizens Tower, Council Chambers Notice is hereby given that the City Council of the City of Lubbock, Texas, will conduct a public hearing to consider the reauthorization of the City's Industrial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria pursuant to Ch", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nPublic Hearing for the Reauthorization of the City's\nIndustrial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria\nJuly 28, 2026 at 2:00 P.M.\nCitizens Tower, Council Chambers\nNotice is hereby given that the City Council of the City of Lubbock, Texas, will conduct a public hearing\nto consider the reauthorization of the City's Industrial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria pursuant to\nChapter 312 of the Texas Tax Code. \nThe public hearing will be held:\nDate: July 28, 2026\nTime: 2:00 P.M. (or as soon thereafter as the item may be heard)\nLocation: City Council Chambers\n1314 Avenue K\nLubbock, Texas 79401\nThe purpose of the public hearing is to receive comments from interested persons regarding the proposed\nreauthorization of the City of Lubbock's Industrial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria, which establish\nthe eligibility requirements and standards governing the consideration of property tax abatements within\nthe City. \nFollowing the public hearing, the City Council may consider action to reauthorize the Industrial Tax\nAbatement Guidelines and Criteria in accordance with Chapter 312 of the Texas Tax Code. \nAll interested persons are invited to attend and be heard.\nPublic hearings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please\ncontact the City Secretary’s Office at 775-2025 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least\n48 hours in advance of the public hearing. \nAny questions concerning the matters described in this notice should be directed to: Danielle Williams,\nCity of Lubbock Business Development Director at 775-3082."}, {"title": "2026-07-28 - Public Hearing for the Reauthorization of the City's Commercial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria - 2:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07282026-139", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Public Hearing for the Reauthorization of the City's Commercial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria July 28, 2026 at 2:00 P.M. Citizens Tower, Council Chambers Notice is hereby given that the City Council of the City of Lubbock, Texas, will conduct a public hearing to consider the reauthorization of the City's Commercial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria pursuant to Ch", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nPublic Hearing for the Reauthorization of the City's\nCommercial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria\nJuly 28, 2026 at 2:00 P.M.\nCitizens Tower, Council Chambers\nNotice is hereby given that the City Council of the City of Lubbock, Texas, will conduct a public hearing\nto consider the reauthorization of the City's Commercial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria pursuant\nto Chapter 312 of the Texas Tax Code. \nThe public hearing will be held:\nDate: July 28, 2026\nTime: 2:00 P.M. (or as soon thereafter as the item may be heard)\nLocation: City Council Chambers\n1314 Avenue K\nLubbock, Texas 79401\nThe purpose of the public hearing is to receive comments from interested persons regarding the proposed\nreauthorization of the City of Lubbock's Commercial Tax Abatement Guidelines and Criteria, which\nestablish the eligibility requirements and standards governing the consideration of property tax abatements\nwithin the City. \nFollowing the public hearing, the City Council may consider action to reauthorize the Commercial Tax\nAbatement Guidelines and Criteria in accordance with Chapter 312 of the Texas Tax Code. \nAll interested persons are invited to attend and be heard.\nPublic hearings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please\ncontact the City Secretary’s Office at 775-2025 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least\n48 hours in advance of the public hearing. \nAny questions concerning the matters described in this notice should be directed to: Danielle Williams,\nCity of Lubbock Business Development Director at 775-3082."}, {"title": "2026-07-24 - Capital Improvements Advisory Committee Regular Meeting Agenda July 24, 2026 10:00 A.M. (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07242026-153", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Chris Berry, Chair Clark Lammert Clay Enger Keith Patrick Matt Vance Monica Delgado Norma Johnson Victor Flores-Moguel Capital Improvements Advisory Committee Regular Meeting Agenda July 24, 2026 10:00 A.M. Second Floor Conference Room A & B 1314 Ave K, Lubbock, TX John Turpin, City Engineer Bailey Ratcliffe, Assistant City Engineer Kerry Koehler, Assistant City Engineer April Chavez, Management A", "text": "Chris Berry, Chair\nClark Lammert\nClay Enger\nKeith Patrick\nMatt Vance\nMonica Delgado\nNorma Johnson\nVictor Flores-Moguel\nCapital Improvements Advisory Committee\nRegular Meeting Agenda\nJuly 24, 2026\n10:00 A.M.\nSecond Floor Conference Room A & B\n1314 Ave K, Lubbock, TX\nJohn Turpin, City Engineer\nBailey Ratcliffe, Assistant City Engineer\nKerry Koehler, Assistant City Engineer\nApril Chavez, Management Assistant\nCapital Improvements Advisory Committee meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special\nassistance, please contact the City of Lubbock-Engineering at 806-775-2382, e-mail achavez@mylubbock.us, or write to Post\nOffice Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79401 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting.\n1.\nCall to Order\n2.\nCitizen Comments – Any citizen wishing to appear in-person before a regular meeting of the Capital \nImprovements Advisory Committee, regarding any matter posted on the Capital Improvements Advisory \nAgenda below, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than 1:30 p.m. on July 24, \n2026. Citizen Comments provide an opportunity for citizens to make comments and express a position on \nagenda items.\n3.\nDiscuss and take appropriate action regarding minutes from the May 12, 2025, meeting.\n4.\nNew committee member orientation\n5.\nDiscuss impact fee balances and expenses to date\n6.\nDiscuss, review and take appropriate action regarding Semi-Annual Report\n7.\nDiscuss date and topics for next meeting.\n8.\nAdjourn meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted on the bulletin board outside the south entrance at Citizens\nTower, 1314 Ave K, Lubbock, Texas, on the 20 day of July, 2026 at\n4:00 P.M.\n____________________________________"}, {"title": "2026-07-23 - Quincy Park Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting - 5:30 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07232026-119", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Quincy Park Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower, West of the revolving", "text": "Quincy Park Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of\ndisability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write\nto P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower, West of\nthe revolving doors at 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the _______ day of _________, 2026 at\n___________ A.M./P.M. by Molly Ballesteros. \nQuincy Park Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 23, 2026\n5:30 P.M.\nCitizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers\n1.0\nWelcome\nPaul Shuey, Chair\n2.0\nIntroduction of the Quincy Park PID Advisory Board Members\nPaul Shuey, Chair\n3.0\nUpdate from the Board on FY 2025-26 Board Activities and Projects\nPaul Shuey, Chair\n4.0 Quincy Park PID Proposed FY 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan\nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n5.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the Quincy Park PID to voice concerns, suggestions,\nand preferences for Quincy Park PID and the Quincy Park PID Service and Assessment Plan.\n6.0 Special Election: Voting on One Position for the Quincy Park PID Advisory Board"}, {"title": "2026-07-22 - Valencia Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting - 5:30 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07222026-121", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Valencia Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting Agenda July 22, 2026 5:30 P.M. Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers 1.0 Welcome Jon Jackson, Chair 2.0 Introduction of the Valencia PID Advisory Board Members Jon Jackson, Chair 3.0 Update from the Board on Fiscal Year 2025-26 Board Activities and Projects Jon Jackson, Chair 4.0 Valencia PID Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27", "text": "Valencia Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 22, 2026\n5:30 P.M.\nCitizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers\n1.0\nWelcome\nJon Jackson, Chair\n2.0\nIntroduction of the Valencia PID Advisory Board Members\nJon Jackson, Chair\n3.0 Update from the Board on Fiscal Year 2025-26 Board Activities and Projects\nJon Jackson, Chair\n4.0 Valencia PID Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n5.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the Valencia PID to voice concerns, suggestions,\nand preferences for Valencia PID and the Valencia PID Service and Assessment Plan.\n6.0 Voting on Two Positions for the Valencia PID Advisory Board\nValencia Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability.\nIf you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write to P.O. Box\n2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower, West of the\nrevolving doors at 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the _______ day of _________, 2026 at\n___________ A.M./P.M. by Molly Ballesteros."}, {"title": "2026-07-22 - Meet and Confer Negotiations - Police (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07222026-149", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Notice of Open Deliberations Pursuant to Tex. Loc. Gov’t Code § 142.063, this public notice is provided relating to meet and confer deliberations between: City of Lubbock, Texas and Lubbock Professional Police Association Date/Time: July 22, 2026 at 3:00 P.M. Location: Citizens Tower, 2nd Floor Conference Center, 201A/B, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas 79401 Agenda: Deliberations and/o", "text": "Notice of Open Deliberations \nPursuant to Tex. Loc. Gov’t Code § 142.063, this public notice is provided relating to meet and confer \ndeliberations between: \nCity of Lubbock, Texas \nand \nLubbock Professional Police Association \n \nDate/Time: \nJuly 22, 2026 at 3:00 P.M. \nLocation: \nCitizens Tower, 2nd Floor Conference Center, 201A/B, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n79401 \nAgenda: \nDeliberations and/or related action relating to an agreement or a proposed agreement \nbetween the City of Lubbock, Texas (“City”) and the Lubbock Professional Police \nAssociation (“Association”) as provided for in Texas Local Government Code Chapter \n142, Subchapter B. \nPublic Notice: \nMembers of the public and press are encouraged to observe and monitor the deliberation. A recording of \nthe public portion of the meeting will be made available, but active participation in discussions or \npresentations is expressly prohibited unless permitted by the Ground Rules established by the City and the \nAssociation. \nRepresentatives for either the City or Association may suspend the meeting at any time during the \nproceedings for consultation with their attorney or to have a private caucus with their respective \nrepresentatives. Pursuant to Section 551.071 of the Texas Government Code and Section 142.063(b) of the \nTexas Local Government Code the deliberations during this period are closed to the public \nThe use of cellular phones or sound activated pagers is prohibited while deliberations are being conducted. \nPlease step outside the meeting room should you elect to utilize these devices. \nThis meeting is available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please \ncontact the City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas \n79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-07-22 - Lubbock Emergency Communication District Board of Managers Meeting (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07222026-151", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "The meeting of the Lubbock Emergency Communication District Board of Managers at the office of Lubbock Emergency Communication District is available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact Annie Wall at (806) 747-6911, or inquire at the LECD offices, 6032 43rd Street, Lubbock, Texas at least 48 hours in advance of the Board of Managers Meeting.", "text": "The meeting of the Lubbock Emergency Communication District Board of Managers at the office of Lubbock \nEmergency Communication District is available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special \nassistance, please contact Annie Wall at (806) 747-6911, or inquire at the LECD offices, 6032 43rd Street, Lubbock, \nTexas at least 48 hours in advance of the Board of Managers Meeting. \nLubbock Emergency Communication District \nBoard of Managers Meeting \nJuly 22, 2026 \n6032 43rd Street \nLubbock, Texas \n12:00 P.M. \n1. Public Comments: \nAny citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Lubbock Emergency \nCommunication District Board of Managers, regarding any matter posted on the July 22, 2026 \nagenda, shall sign-in on the sheet provided at the meeting, no later than 12:00pm. \n2. Consider approval of minutes from May 13, 2026 Board of Managers meeting. \n3. Financial and investment reports. \n4. Review of vendor list for Local Government Officer Conflict of Interest Disclosure. \n5. Discuss and take action as necessary regarding approving the proposed FY 2026-2027 budget for \ndistribution. \nCERTIFICATE OF POSTING \nThis is to certify that a true and correct copy of the forgoing agenda has been posted on this 17th day of July 2026, \non the Lubbock Emergency Communication District website (www.lubbock911.org) and has been submitted \nelectronically to the City of Lubbock, as written notice of the dates, place, and subjects of the Lubbock Emergency \nCommunication District Board of Managers meeting. A copy of the agenda has been posted at the LECD \nadministrative offices. \n\nNathan Kizer, Executive Director"}, {"title": "2026-07-21 - UDHPC meeting at 5:30 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07212026-142", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Deborah Bigness, Chair Baynetta Jordan, Vice-Chair Daniel Biase Mary Crites John Garcia III Randy Henson Patsy Jackson Chad Plunket Sheila Powell Sally Shelton Matthew Weaver Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission Agenda Regular Meeting July 21, 2026 5:30pm Citizens Tower, Council Chambers 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas The Urban Design and Historic Preservation Com", "text": "Deborah Bigness, Chair \nBaynetta Jordan, Vice-Chair \nDaniel Biase \nMary Crites \nJohn Garcia III \nRandy Henson \nPatsy Jackson \nChad Plunket \nSheila Powell \nSally Shelton \nMatthew Weaver \n \nUrban Design and Historic Preservation Commission Agenda \nRegular Meeting \nJuly 21, 2026 \n5:30pm \n \nCitizens Tower, Council Chambers \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n\nThe Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission meetings are available to all persons \nregardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at \n806-775-2108, email cityplanning@mylubbock.us, or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, \n79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \nRegular Agenda \n1. Call to Order - Welcome and Introductions \n2. Citizen Comment - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the \nUrban Design and Historic Preservation Commission regarding any matter posted on \nthe Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission Agenda, shall complete the \nsign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than 5:30 p.m. \n3. Approval of Minutes \n \n3.1 May 19, 2026, Regular Meeting \n \n4. Certificate of Appropriateness - Public Hearings \n[Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission has final authority for approval.] \n \n4.1 Request of City of Lubbock \n \nConsider a request for a Certificate of Appropriateness to alter the historical \narchitectural features on a property with a Local Historic Designation (St. Paul’s on \nthe Plains Episcopal Church), to replace the existing wood-shingled rood with a \nnew multi-width composite shingle system, at: \n\n• 4011 University Avenue, located east of University Avenue and south of 40th \nStreet, Alta Vista Acres Addition, Block 13, Lots 1 through 4, less 100 by \n132.5 feet of Lot 3. \n \n5. Adjourn \n \nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted on the bulletin board outside the \neast entrance of Citizens Tower 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the 9th day of July \n2026 at 10:30 AM. \n \n By: Rosie Gray"}, {"title": "2026-07-21 - The Customer Engagement Committee of the Electric Utility Board Meeting- 8:45 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07212026-148", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "CUSTOMER ENGAGEMENT COMMITTEE AGENDA July 21, 2026 8:45am OPEN SESSION: CITIZENS TOWER 1314 Avenue K Lubbock, Texas 79401 5th Floor Conference Rooms 501A 1. Call to order. Open Session 2. Approve the minutes from the last held meeting on November 18, 2025. 3. Discussion regarding a proposed Electric Vehicle (EV) Incentive Policy. 4. Discussion regarding the City's new website and related", "text": "CUSTOMER ENGAGEMENT COMMITTEE \nAGENDA \nJuly 21, 2026 \n8:45am \nOPEN SESSION: \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Avenue K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \n5th Floor Conference Rooms 501A \n1.\nCall to order.\nOpen Session \n2.\nApprove the minutes from the last held meeting on November 18, 2025.\n3.\nDiscussion regarding a proposed Electric Vehicle (EV) Incentive Policy.\n4.\nDiscussion regarding the City's new website and related customer information and\nservices.\n5.\nDiscussion regarding services provided to the City of Lubbock.\n6.\nDiscussion regarding retail cash payment options and the use of the Kubra payment\nplatform.\n7.\nDiscussion regarding automation initiatives within the Utility Customer Center to improve\ncustomer service and operational efficiency.\n8.\nDiscussion regarding customer fees collected by the city.\n9.\nAdjourn\nPosted on the bulletin board outside the south entrance of \nCitizen’s Tower, 1314 Ave. K., Lubbock, Texas, on the \n15th day of July 2026, at 2:00 P.M. \nBy: \n******************************************************************************************************** \nCITY OF LUBBOCK CUSTOMER ENGAGEMENT COMMITTEE AND EUB MEETINGS ARE AVAILABLE TO ALL \nPERSONS REGARDLESS OF DISABILITY. IF YOU REQUIRE SPECIAL ASSISTANCE, PLEASE CONTACT THE CITY \nSECRETARY AT (806) 775-2026 OR WRITE P. O. BOX 2000, LUBBOCK, TEXAS 79457, AT LEAST 48 HOURS IN \nADVANCE OF THE MEETING. \n********************************************************************************************************\nBeatrice Duenez"}, {"title": "2026-07-21 - North Point Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting Agenda - 5:30 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07212026-128", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "North Point Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at 1314 Avenue K, We", "text": "North Point Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you \nrequire special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, \nTexas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at 1314 Avenue K, \nWest of the revolving doors, Lubbock, Texas, on the ______ day of _______, 2026 at _________ AM/PM\n____________________________________________\nNorth Point Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 21, 2026\n5:30 P.M.\nCentennial Elementary School | Cafeteria\n1301 North Utica, Lubbock, Texas\n1.0\nWelcome\nJonathan Blackwell, Chair\n2.0\nIntroduction of the North Point PID Advisory Board Members\nJonathan Blackwell, Chair\n3.0\nUpdate from the Board on Fiscal Year 2025-26 Board Activities and Projects\nJonathan Blackwell, Chair\n4.0\nNorth Point PID Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan\nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n5.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the North Point PID to voice concerns, suggestions, and \npreferences for North Point PID and the North Point PID Service and Assessment Plan.\n6.0\nVoting on Four Positions for the North Point PID Advisory Board"}, {"title": "2026-07-21 - Electric Utility Board Meeting- 11:00 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07212026-147", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "The Electric Utility Board of Lubbock Power & Light Agenda July 21, 2026 11:00 a.m. Edwin “Butch” Davis, P.E., Chair Eddie Schulz, Vice Chair Gwen Stafford, Secretary Dr. Solomon Fields Dr. Gonzalo Ramirez Dr. Craig Rhyne Rhea Hill Mike Stevens Thomas Parker Mark McBrayer, Ex-Officio www.lpandl.com Joel Ivy, Chief Administrative Officer Keli Swan, General Counsel CITIZENS TOWER 1", "text": "The Electric Utility Board of \nLubbock Power & Light Agenda \nJuly 21, 2026 \n11:00 a.m. \nEdwin “Butch” Davis, P.E., Chair \nEddie Schulz, Vice Chair \nGwen Stafford, Secretary \nDr. Solomon Fields \nDr. Gonzalo Ramirez \nDr. Craig Rhyne \nRhea Hill \nMike Stevens \nThomas Parker \nMark McBrayer, Ex-Officio \nwww.lpandl.com \nJoel Ivy, Chief Administrative Officer \nKeli Swan, General Counsel \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Ave K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \nOPEN SESSION: \nCity Council Chambers \nEXECUTIVE SESSION: \nCitizens Tower \nConference Room 201A and 201B \n1.\nCall to Order.\n2.\nPublic Comment. Public comment is an opportunity for the public to make comments and\nexpress a position on agenda items.\na.\nThis period, of up to thirty minutes, is dedicated to public comment. Each member\nof the public will have three minutes to speak. Any member of the public that\nrequires the assistance from a translator will have six minutes to speak. Comments\nmust be limited to only those items posted on the Electric Utility Board agenda.\nAny member of the public wishing to speak shall sign up on the public comment\nsheet on the day of the Electric Utility Board Meeting, prior to the beginning of the\nmeeting. The Electric Utility Board shall follow the order of persons wishing to\nspeak as listed on the sign-up sheet. If everyone who signed up to speak has been\ngiven an opportunity to speak and the time designated for public comment has not\nbeen exhausted, the Electric Utility Board may, at its discretion, open the floor to\nanyone else wishing to address the Electric Utility Board, subject to all the same\nprocedures and guidelines.\n3.\nThe Electric Utility Board may make any comments related to board member activities,\nevents, community engagement, or current or future agenda items.\n1 of 193\n\nExecutive Session \n4.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.086,\n§552.133, and §552.1331 to discuss and deliberate, on the following competitive matters\nof Lubbock Power & Light, reasonably related to the following categories:\n(i) Discussion and deliberation of generation matters, including but not limited to\ngeneration unit and/or portfolio sale opportunities including the potential for unused\nland designated for other uses;\n(ii) Discussion regarding real estate purchase and transactions for future substation\ndevelopments;\n(iii) Discussion of risk management, contracts, and strategies, customer billing, and usage\ninformation, system load characteristics, and ERCOT power analyses and strategies;\n(iv) Plans, studies, proposals, and analyses for system improvements, additions, or sales\n(other than transmission and distribution system improvements inside the service area\nfor which the public power utility is the sole certificated retail provider);\n(v) info collected as part of an advanced metering system for usage, services, and billing,\nincluding amounts billed or collected for utility usage\n5.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.071,\nseeking the advice of legal counsel about pending or contemplated litigation or settlement\noffers and hold a consultation with the attorney on matters in which the duty of the attorney\nto the Electric Utility Board under the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct\nof the State Bar of Texas conflicts with Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code:\n(i)\nlegal advice regarding issues herein listed on the agenda;\n(ii)\nlegal advice and counsel regarding contemplated litigation matters;\n(iii)\nlegal advice and counsel regarding North American Electric Reliability\nCorporation Critical Infrastructure Protection (NERC CIP) standards;\n(iv)\nAjay Mitchell v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No. DC-\n2025-CV-1728, proceeding in the 72nd District Court of Lubbock County, Texas.\n(v)\nMaria Jesus Tucker v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No.\nDC-2026-CV-0096, proceeding in the 99th District Court of Lubbock County,\nTexas.\n6.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.072, to\ndeliberate the purchase, exchange, lease, and value of real property.\n2 of 193\nBack to Agenda\n\n7.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1)\nto deliberate the appointment and/or reappointment of a public officer or employee\n(Electric Utility Board).\n8.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1)\nto deliberate the appointment, employment, evaluation, and duties of a public officer or\nemployee (Director of Electric Utilities/Chief Administrative Officer).\n11:30 a.m. – Approximately - Following completion of the Executive Session, the Electric \nUtility Board will reconvene in open session to discuss the items listed below. It is anticipated \nthat the open session will begin at 11:30 a.m. However, this is an approximation, and the \nBoard may begin the open session earlier or later depending on when the Executive Session is \ncomplete. \nOpen Session \n9.\nApprove the minutes from the Regular Electric Utility Board meeting held on June 16, \n2026.\n10.\nPresentation and discussion of the update/report by the LP&L Chief Administrative \nOfficer, or his designee, regarding strategic planning, board committee reports, association \nmatters, ordinance change requests, EUB policy and procedures, customer service, \nbusiness center practices, procedures and policies, transition to retail customer choice, \ncustomer concerns and complaints, billing system, forms and procedures, status of \ntransmission and distribution projects, generation, mutual aid, weather events, outage and \nrestoration efforts, grid matters, Electric Reliability Council of Texas (“ERCOT”) matters \nand committee participation, staffing levels, personnel, regulatory matters, training, \ncybersecurity, mutual aid agreement, and operational performance metrics.\n11.\nPresentation and discussion of financial and capital statements, budget, financing options, \naudits, and financial policies of Lubbock Power & Light relating to debt issuance issues \nand historical debt matters, reserve account funding, cost allocation, revenue and expense \nprojections, customer metering, tariff and billing, service and administrative issues, and \nrisk management information including rates.\n12.\nDiscuss and take action on the Lubbock Power & Light FY 2026-27 Operating Budget and \nCapital Program and make appropriate recommendations to the City Council of the City of \nLubbock pursuant to §2.03.415(a) of the Code of Ordinances, City of Lubbock, Texas. \nConsent \n13. \nConsider a resolution authorizing the Purchasing Manager, or his designee, to execute \nPurchase Order, by and between the City of Lubbock, acting by and through Lubbock Power \n& Light, and SHI Government Solutions of Austin, Texas, The Texas Department\n3 of 193\nBack to Agenda\n\nof Information Resources (DIR) contract DIR-CPO-5170. This DIR quote for $339,225.60, \nwill be for purchase of a three-year VMware Cloud Foundation subscription and support \nservices and is required to maintain manufacturer support, receive software security \nupdates and patches, preserve warranty and technical support eligibility, and ensure the \ncontinued operation of LP&L's virtual infrastructure hosting mission-critical Operational \nTechnology systems. Failure to maintain an active subscription would result in Grid \nOperational instability, unsupported infrastructure, and increased cybersecurity risk. \n14.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Purchasing Manager, or his designee, to execute\nPurchase Order, by and between the City of Lubbock, acting by and through Lubbock\nPower & Light, and Enterprise Security Solutions of Texas, Inc., of Justin, Texas, The\nInterlocal Purchasing System (TIPS) contract 24020301. This TIPS quote for $180,407.74\nwill be for fencing repairs, gate replacements, and physical access control improvements\nat the LP&L Operations Facility at Municipal Drive and is to be completed within 90 days\nafter receipt of order.\n15.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Purchasing Manager, or his designee, to execute\nPurchase Order, by and between the City of Lubbock, acting by and through Lubbock\nPower & Light and Wesco Distribution of Amarillo, Texas for $1,023,535.86 with a 68-\nweek delivery, Request for Proposal (RFP) 7124-26-ELD - LP&L Substation Equipment\nand Materials. This order includes but is not limited to the following items: 115kV and\n23kV switches, 15kV voltage transformers, surge arrestors, fuses, nuts & bolts, wire,\ngeneral hardware, and other components for Erskine’s substation capacity upgrade project.\n16.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Chief Administrative Officer, or his designee, to\nexecute Professional Service Agreement by and between the City of Lubbock, acting by\nand through Lubbock Power & Light, and Archer Energy Solutions, LLC, of Clackamas,\nOregon, Request for Proposal (RFP) 7126-26-ELD - LP&L NERC CIP Staffing and\nConsulting Services. This agreement for $530,400.00 will be for a term of one (1) year\nwith the option to extend for four (4) additional one (1) year terms so long as the amount\nof the payable hereunder does not increase. This agreement includes professional and\nstaffing services for North American Electric Reliability Corporation Critical\nInfrastructure Protection (NERC CIP) compliance support, including, but not limited to,\nstaff augmentation, program management, and operational support with ongoing\ncompliance activities.\n17.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Chief Administrative Officer, or his designee, to\nexecute for and on behalf of the City of Lubbock, acting by and through Lubbock Power\n& Light (“LP&L”), the purchase and sale agreement for the land located at the Northeast\nCorner of 66th and Iola in an amount not to exceed $1,925,000.00 and recommending\napproval of same to the Lubbock City Council.\n18.\nAdjourn.\nTHE ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD RESERVES THE RIGHT TO ADJOURN INTO EXECUTIVE SESSION AS AUTHORIZED BY \nTEX. GOV’T CODE § 551.001, ET. SEQ. (THE TEXAS OPEN MEETINGS ACT) ON ANY ITEM ON ITS OPEN MEETING AGENDA \nOR TO RECONVENE IN A CONTINUATION OF THE EXECUTIVE SESSION ON THE EXECUTIVE SESSION ITEMS NOTED \nABOVE, IN ACCORDANCE WITH THE TEXAS OPEN MEETINGS ACT, INCLUDING, WITHOUT LIMITATION §§ 551.071-551.086. \n4 of 193\nBack to Agenda\n\nPosted on the bulletin board outside the south \nentrance \nof \nCitizens \nTower, \n1314 \nAve. \nK., Lubbock, Texas, on the 15th July 2026, at \n2:00 P.M.\nBy:______________________________ \nCITY OF LUBBOCK ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD MEETINGS ARE AVAILABLE TO ALL PERSONS REGARDLESS OF DISABILITY. IF YOU \nREQUIRE SPECIAL ASSISTANCE, PLEASE CONTACT THE CITY SECRETARY AT (806)775-2026 OR WRITE P. O. BOX 2000, LUBBOCK, \nTEXAS 79457, AT LEAST 48 HOURS IN ADVANCE OF THE MEETING. \n5 of 193\nBeatrice Duenez\nBack to Agenda\n\nThe Electric Utility Board of \nLubbock Power & Light Minutes \nJune 16, 2026 \n11:00 a.m. \nEdwin “Butch” Davis, P.E., Chair \nEddie Schulz, Vice Chair \nGwen Stafford, Secretary \nDr. Solomon Fields \nDr. Gonzalo Ramirez \nDr. Craig Rhyne \nRhea Hill \nMike Stevens \nThomas Parker \nMark McBrayer, Ex-Officio \nwww.lpandl.com \nJoel Ivy, Chief Administrative Officer \nKeli Swan, General Counsel \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Ave K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \nOPEN SESSION: \nCity Council Chambers \nEXECUTIVE SESSION: \nCitizens Tower \nConference Room 201A and 201B \nEUB \nCity Staff \nLP&L Staff \nButch Davis \nEddie Schulz \nGwen Stafford \nRhea Hill \nDr. Solomon Fields \nDr. Gonzalo Ramirez \nDr. Craig Rhyne \nMike Stevens \nMatt Wade \nJoel Ivy \nKeli Swan \nBeatrice Duenez \nCady Mello \nKacey Johnson \nMatt Rose \nTina Cooper \nClint Gardner \nSkylar Borunda \nRenee Treat \nKody Morris \nHarvey Hall \nRachel Hendrix \nTamara Randles \nDaniel Garcia \nMarty Muniz \nColby Morris \nNoah Henry \nQuincy Gutierrez \nPaul Koberlein \nCity Council \nMark McBrayer \nChristy-Martinez Garcia \n6 of 193\nBack to Agenda\n\n1.\nCall to Order.\nBoard Chair Butch Davis called the meeting to order at 11:00 a.m.\n2.\nPublic Comment. Public comment is an opportunity for the public to make comments and\nexpress a position on agenda items.\na.\nThis period, of up to thirty minutes, is dedicated to public comment. Each member\nof the public will have three minutes to speak. Any member of the public that\nrequires the assistance of a translator will have six minutes to speak. Comments\nmust be limited to only those items posted on the Electric Utility Board agenda.\nAny member of the public wishing to speak shall sign up on the public comment\nsheet on the day of the Electric Utility Board Meeting, prior to the beginning of the\nmeeting. The Electric Utility Board shall follow the order of persons wishing to\nspeak as listed on the sign-up sheet. If everyone who signed up to speak has been\ngiven an opportunity to speak and the time designated for public comment has not\nbeen exhausted, the Electric Utility Board may, at its discretion, open the floor to\nanyone else wishing to address the Electric Utility Board, subject to all the same\nprocedures and guidelines.\nNo public comments.\n3.\nThe Electric Utility Board may make any comments related to board member activities,\nevents, community engagement, or current or future agenda items.\nBoard Member Dr. Gonzalo Ramirez commented on the Technical Review Committee’s\nintroduction to new LP&L staff members and presentation of operational updates.\nMember Ramirez also thanked LP&L Chief Operation Officer Blair McGinnis for the\npresentations, as well as a tour of the Massingale and Yellow House facilities.\nLP&L Chief Administrative Officer Joel Ivy introduced LP&L Chief Compliance Officer\nTina Cooper to thank LP&L staff on work for the North American Electric Reliability\nCorporation’s Critical Infrastructure Protection audit. Chief Cooper commented in\nmore detail on the audit and introduced the teams that assisted with the audit to thank\nthem for their work, including the reliability, emergency management, and compliance\nteams. Chief Cooper introduced the following individuals and thanked them for their\nwork on the audit: Cody Kirk, Israel Garcia, Francisco Manzanares, Baylor Campbell,\nRoger Valdez, Cody Francis, Alejandro Loviso, Sam Brito, Paul Koberlein, Colby\nMorris, Josh Romo, Michael Coomer, Kody Morris, Rachel Hendrix, and Laura Gomez.\n7 of 193\nBack to Agenda\n\nExecutive Session \nThe Board recessed into executive session at 11:08 a.m. \n4.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.086,\n§552.133, and §552.1331 to discuss and deliberate, on the following competitive matters\nof Lubbock Power & Light, reasonably related to the following categories:\n(i) Discussion and deliberation of generation matters, including but not limited to\ngeneration unit and/or portfolio sale opportunities including the potential for unused\nland designated for other uses;\n(ii) Discussion of risk management, contracts, and strategies, customer billing, and usage\ninformation, system load characteristics, and ERCOT power analyses and strategies;\n(iii) Plans, studies, proposals, and analyses for system improvements, additions, or sales\n(other than transmission and distribution system improvements inside the service area\nfor which the public power utility is the sole certificated retail provider);\n(iv) info collected as part of an advanced metering system for usage, services, and billing,\nincluding amounts billed or collected for utility usage\n5.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.071,\nseeking the advice of legal counsel about pending or contemplated litigation or settlement\noffers and hold a consultation with the attorney on matters in which the duty of the attorney\nto the Electric Utility Board under the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct\nof the State Bar of Texas conflicts with Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code:\n(i)\nlegal advice regarding issues herein listed on the agenda;\n(ii)\nlegal advice and counsel regarding contemplated litigation matters;\n(iii)\nlegal advice and counsel regarding North American Electric Reliability\nCorporation Critical Infrastructure Protection (NERC CIP) standards;\n(iv)\nAjay Mitchell v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No. DC-\n2025-CV-1728, proceeding in the 72nd District Court of Lubbock County, Texas.\n(v)\nMaria Jesus Tucker v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No.\nDC-2026-CV-0096, proceeding in the 99th District Court of Lubbock County,\nTexas.\n6. Hold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1)\nto deliberate the appointment and/or reappointment of a public officer or employee\n(Electric Utility Board).\n8 of 193\nBack to Agenda\n\n7. Hold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1)\nto deliberate the appointment, employment, evaluation, and duties of a public officer or\nemployee (Director of Electric Utilities/Chief Administrative Officer).\nOpen Session \nThe Board reconvened in open session at 12:30 p.m. \n8.\nApprove the minutes from the Regular Electric Utility Board meeting held on May 19,\n2026.\nBoard Vice-Chair Eddie Schulz made a motion to pass the minutes, which was\nseconded by Board Member Rhea Hill. The Board approved the minutes by a vote of 8-\n0.\n9.\nPresentation and discussion of the update/report by the LP&L Chief Administrative\nOfficer, or his designee, regarding strategic planning, board committee reports, association\nmatters, ordinance change requests, EUB policy and procedures, customer service,\nbusiness center practices, procedures and policies, transition to retail customer choice,\ncustomer concerns and complaints, billing system, forms and procedures, status of\ntransmission and distribution projects, generation, mutual aid, weather events, outage and\nrestoration efforts, grid matters, Electric Reliability Council of Texas (“ERCOT”) matters\nand committee participation, staffing levels, personnel, regulatory matters, training,\ncybersecurity, mutual aid agreement, and operational performance metrics.\nChief Ivy reminded the Board that voting on LP&L’s budget would occur during the\nJuly meeting and then introduced LP&L staff members Colby Morris and Paul\nKoberlein for the ERCOT summer readiness program presentation. Morris and\nKoberlein presented the ERCOT capacity reserves plan summary information for 2025\ncompared with 2026, discussed the energy emergency alert system and threshold of\nalerts, and ERCOT ancillary services report – a free and publicly available resource that\ncontains live generation data updates. Morris and Koberlein confirmed engineering is\nnot concerned about ERCOT’s summer readiness.\n10.\nPresentation and discussion of financial and capital statements, budget, financing options,\naudits, and financial policies of Lubbock Power & Light relating to debt issuance issues\nand historical debt matters, reserve account funding, cost allocation, revenue and expense\nprojections, customer metering, tariff and billing, service and administrative issues, and\nrisk management information including rates.\nLP&L Director of Finance Kacey Johnson presented the balance sheet and cash flows\nfor April 2026. Director Johnson indicated the lower cash flow was due to lower billing\nin March, which matches historical data. Director Johnson reviewed financial ratios\nand LP&L’s income statement.\n9 of 193\nBack to Agenda\n\nConsent \nChair Davis made a motion to remove item 15 from the consent agenda and Member \nRamirez seconded the motion. \nConsent items 11-14 and 16-18 were considered. \nA motion to approve consent items 11-14 and 16-18 was made by Board Secretary Gwen \nStafford and seconded by Vice-Chair Schulz. The motion to approve was passed by a vote of \n8-0.\n11.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Chief Administrative Officer, or his designee to\nexecute a contract to The Big Red for grant consulting services. The contract is a six-\nmonth term with retainer fees not to exceed $150,000.00 and an additional one-time\nperformance fee of five (5) percent of grant awarded funds"}, {"title": "2026-07-16 - Zoning Board of Adjustment @ 8:30 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07162026-135", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Deborah Bigness, Chair Baynetta Jordan, Vice-Chair Daniel Biase Mary Crites John Garcia III Randy Henson Patsy Jackson Chad Plunket Sheila Powell Sally Shelton Matthew Weaver Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission Agenda Regular Meeting July 21, 2026 5:30pm Citizens Tower, Council Chambers 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas The Urban Design and Historic Preservation Com", "text": "Deborah Bigness, Chair \nBaynetta Jordan, Vice-Chair \nDaniel Biase \nMary Crites \nJohn Garcia III \nRandy Henson \nPatsy Jackson \nChad Plunket \nSheila Powell \nSally Shelton \nMatthew Weaver \n \nUrban Design and Historic Preservation Commission Agenda \nRegular Meeting \nJuly 21, 2026 \n5:30pm \n \nCitizens Tower, Council Chambers \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n\nThe Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission meetings are available to all persons \nregardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at \n806-775-2108, email cityplanning@mylubbock.us, or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, \n79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \nRegular Agenda \n1. Call to Order - Welcome and Introductions \n2. Citizen Comment - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the \nUrban Design and Historic Preservation Commission regarding any matter posted on \nthe Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission Agenda, shall complete the \nsign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than 5:30 p.m. \n3. Approval of Minutes \n \n3.1 May 19, 2026, Regular Meeting \n \n4. Certificate of Appropriateness - Public Hearings \n[Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission has final authority for approval.] \n \n4.1 Request of City of Lubbock \n \nConsider a request for a Certificate of Appropriateness to alter the historical \narchitectural features on a property with a Local Historic Designation (St. Paul’s on \nthe Plains Episcopal Church), to replace the existing wood-shingled rood with a \nnew multi-width composite shingle system, at: \n\n• 4011 University Avenue, located east of University Avenue and south of 40th \nStreet, Alta Vista Acres Addition, Block 13, Lots 1 through 4, less 100 by \n132.5 feet of Lot 3. \n \n5. Adjourn \n \nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted on the bulletin board outside the \neast entrance of Citizens Tower 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the 9th day of July \n2026 at 10:30 AM. \n \n By: Rosie Gray"}, {"title": "2026-07-16 - Lubbock Business Park Tax Increment Financing Reinvestment Zone - 1:30 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07162026-117", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the South entrance of Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______________, 2026 at___________ A.M./P.M. _________________________________________ Cassandra Henry, Chair Tony Whitehead, Vice Chair Jason Coleman County Commissioner Cary Shaw Jeff Dane Sonny Garza Christopher Hook Kathy Rollo Tyler Y", "text": "The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the South entrance of Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue \nK, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______________, 2026 at___________ A.M./P.M. \n\n_________________________________________ \n \nCassandra Henry, Chair \nTony Whitehead, Vice Chair \nJason Coleman \nCounty Commissioner Cary Shaw \nJeff Dane \nSonny Garza \nChristopher Hook \nKathy Rollo \nTyler Young \n\nLubbock Business Park Tax Increment \nFinancing Reinvestment Zone Board \nAgenda \n July 16, 2026 | 1:30 P.M. \nCitizens Tower 2nd Floor, Room 201A/B \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n \nDanielle Williams, Business Development Director \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager \n\nLubbock Business Park Tax Increment Financing Reinvestment Zone Board meetings are available to all persons regardless of \ndisability. If you require special assistance, please contact the Business Development Department at 806.775.3082, e-mail \nbusinessdevelopment@mylubbock.us, or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of \nthe meeting. \n \n1. \nCall to order \n \n2. \nCitizen Comments - Any citizen wishing to appear before the Lubbock Business Park Tax Increment \nFinancing Reinvestment Zone Board, regarding any matter posted on the agenda below, shall complete the \nsign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than 1:30 p.m. Citizen Comments is an opportunity for \ncitizens to make comments and express a position on agenda items. Each comment will be read aloud for a \nperiod of time not to exceed three minutes pursuant to City Council rules. Any comment received that is in \nthe nature of asking a question will be referred to an appropriate member of the City staff for a response \nand will not be read aloud at the meeting. \n \n3. \nRegular Session \n \n3.1 \nApproval of Minutes – January 14, 2026 \n \n3.2 \nDiscuss and take appropriate action on the Financial Report \n \n3.3 \nDiscuss and take appropriate action on the Fiscal Year 2025-26 Budget \n \n4. \nWork Session \n \n4.1 \nLubbock Economic Development Alliance Annual Report \n \n5. \nSchedule Next Meeting – The suggested meeting date is January 14, 2027, at 1:30 P.M"}, {"title": "2026-07-15 - The Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board of Trustees will conduct a regular meeting on Wednesday, July 15, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. in the City Bank Conference Room. (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07152026-144", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "NOTICE OF A REGULAR MEETING OF THE LUBBOCK FIRE PENSION FUND BOARD OF TRUSTEES The Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board of Trustees will conduct a regular meeting on Wednesday, July 15, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. in the City Bank Conference Room, located at 611 University Avenue, Lubbock, TX 79401, for the purposes set forth in the following agenda: AGENDA Discussion and Action may be taken on the followi", "text": "NOTICE OF A REGULAR MEETING OF THE \nLUBBOCK FIRE PENSION FUND \nBOARD OF TRUSTEES \nThe Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board of Trustees will conduct a regular meeting on Wednesday, July 15, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. in the City \nBank Conference Room, located at 611 University Avenue, Lubbock, TX 79401, for the purposes set forth in the following agenda: \nAGENDA \nDiscussion and Action may be taken on the following items: \n1.\nCall to Order \n2.\nPublic Comment – Any member of the public wishing to address the Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board, regarding any matter on\nthe Agenda shall email lubbockfire@lauterbachamen.com by 11:00 a.m. on the day of the Board meeting. Comments by any\nindividual may not exceed five minutes in length unless extended by the Chairman. \n3.\nBenefit Applications, Changes, and QDROs (Qualified Domestic Relations Order) \n4.\nConsent Agenda – All matters listed under the Consent Agenda, are considered routine by the Board and will be enacted by one\nmotion. These include authorization of payment expenses. There will not be separate discussion of these items. If any Trustee \ndesires to discuss an item on the Consent Agenda, it will be moved to the Regular agenda for further consideration. \na)\nJune 10, 2026 Board Meeting Minutes \nb)\nLauterbach & Amen invoice in the amount of $7,515 for May 2026 professional services \nc)\nCAPTRUST invoice in the amount of $21,094.58 for April 1, 2026 through June 30, 2026 investment advisory fees \nd)\nJackson Walker LLP in the amount of $12,337.50 for June 2026 legal services rendered \n5.\nConsultant Presentation by CAPTRUST \na)\nPreliminary June 2026 Performance\nb)\nDiscussion and Possible Action on Fund Rebalancing Recommendations \n6.\nOld Business \na)\nDiscussion and Possible Action on Plan Document Update \n7.\nNew Business \na)\nDiscussion and Action on the Financial Audit of December 31, 2025 \nb)\nDiscussion and Action on the December 31, 2025 GASB 67 Report \n8.\nAdministrator’s Report \na)\nMonthly Financial Report \nb)\nDemographics \nc)\nInvestments Update \nd)\nOffice Update \ne)\nTrustee Training \n9.\nCommittee Reports \na)\nMembership Committee \nb)\nLegislative Committee \nc)\nNominations Committee \nd)\nInvestments Committee \n10.\nAdjournment \nThe next regular meeting is scheduled for Wednesday, August 12, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. \nPosted: July 9, 2026 \nBy: Elizabeth Adelmann \nCity of Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, \nplease contact Elizabeth Adelmann at (630) 821-0201 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-07-15 - Special City Council Meeting, July 15, 2026 (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07152026-143", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Special City Council Meeting July 15, 2026 Mark McBrayer, Mayor Dr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5 Christy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1 Gordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 David Brueger Councilman, District 3 Brayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 Tim Collins, Councilman, District 6 W. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager Matt Wade, City Attorney Courtn", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nSpecial City Council Meeting\n July 15, 2026\nMark McBrayer, Mayor\nDr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5\nChristy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1\nGordon Harris, Councilman, District 2\nDavid Brueger Councilman, District 3\nBrayden Rose, Councilman, District 4\nTim Collins, Councilman, District 6\n \nW. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager\nMatt Wade, City Attorney\nCourtney Paz, City Secretary\nhttp://www.mylubbock.us\nConference Rooms CT201A and CT201B, Conference Center, 2nd Floor, Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \nCity of Lubbock City Council Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance,\nplease contact the City Secretary's Office at 775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours\nin advance of the meeting. \nNote:Executive Session Disclosure Statement: The City Council reserves the right to adjourn into executive session at any time\nduring the course of the meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized by Chapter 551 of the Texas Government\nCode, including but not necessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with Attorney), §551.072 (Deliberations regarding Real\nProperty), §551.073 (Deliberations regarding Gifts and Donations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076 (Deliberations\nregarding Security Devices), §551.086 (Public Power Utilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations regarding\nEconomic Development Negotiations). \nA quorum of the City Council will be physically present in CT201A and CT201B, Conference Center, 2nd Floor, in Citizens\nTower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, as it is the intent of the City Council to have a quorum physically present at this\nlocation. One or more members of the City Council, however, may participate in the meeting by video-conference call as\npermitted under Section 551.127 of the Texas Government Code. \nNote: On occasion the City Council may consider agenda items out of order.\n8:30 a.m. - City Council convenes in Conference Rooms CT201A and CT201B in the Conference Center on the\n2nd Floor of Citizens Tower in Work Session, regarding items for presentation and discussion on such issues\nthat may require in-depth consideration by the City Council. No action will be taken by the City Council in\nWork Session.\n\n1.\nHear and discuss presentations regarding the following and provide guidance to staff as appropriate. \n \n1. 1.\nDiscuss planning, goals, and budget priorities for the Lubbock City Council and the City of Lubbock, and\ndiscuss the programs, expenditures and policies of the City Council and the City of Lubbock, including\nbut not limited to the Lubbock City Charter, ARPA funding, annexation and growth, aquatics, substance\nabuse, municipal fees, transportation, infrastructure, solid waste, streets, Gateway Fund, Civic Center,\nLubbock Animal Services, libraries, community engagement, capital improvements, facilities, parks and\nrecreation, development services, legislative agenda, public safety, public health, public safety\ninfrastructure, code enforcement, staffing levels, and future needs. This discussion may include a wide\nrange of topics related to the programs, operation, and policies of the City Council and the City of\nLubbock. The City Council may give direction to the City Manager in regard to determining the priorities\nand goals discussed."}, {"title": "2026-07-15 - Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee - Noon (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07152026-134", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "7/6/2026S:\\solid_waste\\Keep Lubbock Beautiful\\Advisory Committee\\0 Meeting Files 0\\Agenda\\2026\\Jul152026.doc Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee Agenda July 15, 2026 12:00 p.m. 208 Municipal Dr. 1) Call to Order 2) Citizen Comment – Notice: Any citizen wishing to appear before any regular meeting of the Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee shall advise Keep Lubbock Beautiful", "text": "7/6/2026S:\\solid_waste\\Keep Lubbock Beautiful\\Advisory Committee\\0 Meeting Files 0\\Agenda\\2026\\Jul152026.doc \n \nKeep Lubbock Beautiful \nAdvisory Committee Agenda \nJuly 15, 2026 12:00 p.m. \n208 Municipal Dr. \n\n1) Call to Order \n\n2) Citizen Comment – Notice: Any citizen wishing to appear before any regular meeting of the Keep Lubbock \nBeautiful Advisory Committee shall advise Keep Lubbock Beautiful of that fact no later than seven calendar \ndays before the meeting at which the citizen wishes to appear. Any citizen wishing to appear shall inform Keep \nLubbock Beautiful, in writing, of the subject of the appearance in sufficient detail. Such notice shall contain the \nname and address of the requestor. The appearance of any citizen giving such notice to Keep Lubbock Beautiful \nshall take precedence in addressing the Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee during the Citizen \nComments period. Any citizen failing to advise Keep Lubbock Beautiful of his/her wish to appear before the \nKeep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee as herein above set forth shall, time permitting, be permitted to \naddress the Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee after those having given notice. In accordance with \nthe Texas Open Meetings Act, the Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee may not take action on public \nmatters during Citizen Comments. \n \n3) Approval of Minutes – June 17, 2026 meeting \n \n4) Work Session \n \na. Beautification Projects \nb. Community Outreach \nc. Lubbock’s Leading Landscapes \nd. Officer Selection Review \ne. Subcommittee Review \nf. Programs Review \ng. Review calendar of events \n\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING posted on the bulletin board outside the east entrance of \nCitizens Tower, 1314 Ave K, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____day of July 2026 at ________ A.M. /P.M. \n\nBy: ____________________________________________________ \n\nKeep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. \nIf you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806-775-3149 or write to P.O. Box \n2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-07-15 - Electric Utility Board Special Meeting-1:00pm (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07152026-136", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "SPECIAL MEETING OF THE ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD AGENDA July 15, 2026 1:00PM OPEN SESSION: CITIZENS TOWER 1314 Avenue K Lubbock, Texas 79401 Council Chambers EXECUTIVE SESSION: Council Workroom 1. Call to Order. Executive Session 2. Hold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1) to deliberate the appointment, employment, evaluation, and duties of a p", "text": "SPECIAL MEETING OF THE \nELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD AGENDA \nJuly 15, 2026 \n1:00PM \nOPEN SESSION: \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Avenue K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \nCouncil Chambers \nEXECUTIVE SESSION: \nCouncil Workroom \n1.\nCall to Order.\nExecutive Session \n2.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code §\n551.074(a)(1) to deliberate the appointment, employment, evaluation, and duties of\na public officer or employee (Director of Electric Utilities/Chief Administrative\nOfficer).\n3.\nAdjourn.\nTHE ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD RESERVES THE RIGHT TO ADJOURN INTO EXECUTIVE SESSION AS AUTHORIZED BY TEX. \nGOV’T CODE § 551.001, ET. SEQ. (THE TEXAS OPEN MEETINGS ACT) ON ANY ITEM ON ITS OPEN MEETING AGENDA OR TO \nRECONVENE IN A CONTINUATION OF THE EXECUTIVE SESSION ON THE EXECUTIVE SESSION ITEMS NOTED ABOVE, IN \nACCORDANCE WITH THE TEXAS OPEN MEETINGS ACT, INCLUDING, WITHOUT LIMITATION §§ 551.071-551.086. \nPosted on the bulletin board outside the south entrance \nof Citizen’s Tower, 1314 Ave. K., Lubbock, Texas, on \nthe 7th day of July 2026, at 10:00am \nBy: \nCITY OF LUBBOCK ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD MEETINGS ARE AVAILABLE TO ALL PERSONS REGARDLESS OF DISABILITY. IF YOU \nREQUIRE SPECIAL ASSISTANCE, PLEASE CONTACT THE CITY SECRETARY AT (806)775-2026 OR WRITE P. O. BOX 2000, LUBBOCK, \nTEXAS 79457, AT LEAST 48 HOURS IN ADVANCE OF THE MEETING. \nBeatrice Duenez"}, {"title": "2026-07-14 - July 14, 2026 Regular City Council Meeting (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07142026-141", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Regular City Council Meeting July 14, 2026 Mark W. McBrayer, Mayor Dr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5 Christy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1 Gordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 David Bruegel, Councilman, District 3 Brayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 Tim Collins, Councilman, District 6 W. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager Matt Wade, City Attorney Cou", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nRegular City Council Meeting\nJuly 14, 2026\nMark W. McBrayer, Mayor\nDr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5\nChristy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1\nGordon Harris, Councilman, District 2\nDavid Bruegel, Councilman, District 3\nBrayden Rose, Councilman, District 4\nTim Collins, Councilman, District 6\n \nW. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager\nMatt Wade, City Attorney\nCourtney Paz, City Secretary\nhttp://www.mylubbock.us\nCity Council Chambers, Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas\n \nCity of Lubbock City Council Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact\nthe City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the\nmeeting. \nExecutive Session Disclosure Statement: The City Council reserves the right to adjourn into executive session at any time during the course of\nthe meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized by Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code, including but not\nnecessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with Attorney), §551.072 (Deliberations regarding Real Property), §551.073 (Deliberations\nregarding Gifts and Donations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076 (Deliberations regarding Security Devices), §551.086 (Public\nPower Utilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations regarding Economic Development Negotiations). \nA quorum of the City Council will be physically present in City Council Chambers located in Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas,\nas it is the intent of the City Council to have a quorum physically present at this location. One or more members of the City Council, however,\nmay participate in the meeting by video-conference call as permitted under Section 551.127 of the Texas Government Code. \nNote: On occasion, the City Council may consider agenda items out of order. \n. \n \n1:00 p.m. - City Council convenes in City Council Chambers and immediately recesses into Executive Session.\n\n1.\nExecutive Session\n \n1. 1.\nHold an executive session in accordance with Texas Government Code, Section 551.071, with legal\ncounsel for advice about pending or contemplated litigation or settlement agreement and on matters in\nwhich the duty of the attorney under the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct conflicts\nwith Chapter 551. \n \n1. 1. 1.\nLake 7\n \n1. 2.\nHold an executive session in accordance with the Texas Government Code, Section 551.072, to\ndeliberate the purchase, exchange, lease, or value of real property. \n \n1. 2. 1.\nLake 7\n\n1. 3.\nHold an executive session in accordance with Texas Government Code Section 551.074, to discuss\npersonnel matters, regarding the duties, responsibilities, and/or appointments to the Building Board of\nAppeals, Canadian River Municipal Water Authority Board of Directors, Permit & License Appeal\nBoard, and the Zoning Board of Adjustment. \n \n1. 4.\nHold an executive session in accordance with Texas Government Code 551.0761 to discuss and take\naction on measures to protect a critical infrastructure facility. \n \n1. 4. 1.\nAnti-virus platform software intended to protect the City's technology infrastructure and critical\ninfrastructure facilities from unauthorized access, malicious software, and other cybersecurity\nthreats to public safety operations, communications, financial systems, and other essential City\nservices. \n \nAdjourn from Executive Session\n \n2:00 p.m. - City Council reconvenes in Open Session in City Council Chambers.\n \n2.\nCeremonial Items\n \n2. 1.\nInvocation \n \n2. 2.\nPledges of Allegiance \n \nCall to Order\n \n3.\nCitizen Comments - According to Lubbock City Council Rules, any citizen wishing to appear\nin-person before a regular meeting of the City Council, regarding any matter posted on the City\nCouncil Agenda below, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than\n2:00 p.m. on July 14, 2026. Citizen Comments provide an opportunity for citizens to make\ncomments and express a position on agenda items. \n \n4.\nMinutes\n \n4. 1.\nJune 9, 2026 Regular City Council Meeting \n \n5.\nConsent Agenda - Items considered to be routine are enacted by one motion without separate\ndiscussion. If the City Council desires to discuss an item, the item is removed from the Consent\nAgenda and considered separately.\n \n5. 1.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 1st Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance Amendment 31,\nrespecting the STD/HIV-DIS Grant Fund for the appropriation of funding from the Texas Department\nof State Health Services. \n\n5. 2.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 1st Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance Amendment 32,\nrespecting the Bell Farms PID Fund increasing the appropriation for public improvements related to\nsafety at Playa Lake Park \n \n5. 2. 1.\nOrdinance 1st Reading - Finance: Consider an ordinance amending Ordinance No. 2025-O0108,\n Exhibit “B”, the 2025 Service and Assessment Plan for the Bell Farms Public Improvement District,\nby including additional funding for public improvements for Contract 19454, related to safety at Playa\nLake Park. \n \n5. 3.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 1st Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance Amendment 33,\nrespecting the Cemetery Fund and Cemetery Capital Project Fund in connection with Capital\nImprovement Project 92889, Cemetery Irrigation System. \n \n5. 4.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 1st Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance Amendment 34,\nrespecting the General Fund and General Facilities System Improvements Capital Project Fund in\nconnection with Capital Improvement Project 8675, Employee Education Assistance Program - ARPA. \n \n5. 5.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0074, and related\ndocuments, abandoning and closing a portion of an alley located in Section 17, Block B, Lubbock\nCounty, Texas, on the north side of the Marsha Sharp Freeway at 29th Street. \n \n5. 6.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0075, and related\ndocuments, abandoning and closing a 0.063-acre portion of the Legacy Range Business Park, Tract\n\"D\", an addition to the City of Lubbock, Lubbock County Texas, on the south side of 116th Street, east\nof Indiana Avenue. \n \n5. 7.\nOrdinance 1st Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider an ordinance abandoning and closing a portion of\na cross access easement and drainage easement, located in the Action Gypsum Addition in Section 21,\nBlock E-2, north of 116th Street, and east of Indiana Avenue. \n \n5. 8.\nOrdinance 1st Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider an ordinance, and related documents, abandoning\nand closing a portion of a slope and drainage easement on the south-west corner of 98th Street and\nMilwaukee Avenue, in Section 22, Block AK, Lubbock. \n \n5. 9.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Memorandum of\nAgreement, and related documents, with John Webb and wife, Cheryl Webb, for the purchase of a\n0.3203-acre tract of land located in Section 17, Block E, Lubbock County, Texas (Parcel 43), located\non the east side of Avenue P between 143rd Street and 144th Street, to be utilized for the 2024 Street\nBond Project. \n \n5. 10.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Contract\n19577, and related documents, by and between the City of Lubbock and Milwaukee 140, LLC, for the\nsale of real property, a 3.27-acre tract out of Section 22, Block AK, H.E.& W.T.RR. Co. Survey,\nAbstract No. 749, Lubbock County, Texas, located at 10202-10304 Milwaukee Avenue. \n \n5. 11.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Change Order No.\n4 to Contract 18190, and related documents, by and between the City of Lubbock and Lone Star Dirt &\nPaving, LTD, for the 66th Street Channel Drainage Project.\n\n5. 12.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Public Works\nContract 19729, with Fuquay, Inc., for Pipeline Rehabilitation, for the Downtown Sewer Line\nRehabilitation Project Phase 2B. \n \n5. 13.\nResolution - Public Health Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a\nMemorandum of Understanding, and all related documents, between the City of Lubbock, on behalf of\nthe Lubbock Health Department, and Lubbock County, on behalf of the Lubbock County Juvenile\nJustice Center, related to the Substance Use Service Assistance Network, to link priority populations\nwith treatment services. \n \n5. 14.\nResolution - Facilities Management: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Contract\n19662, with Otis Elevator Company, for elevator preventative maintenance and inspection services. \n \n5. 15.\nResolution - Business Development: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Contract\n19561 and related documents, with West Texas Services, Inc. dba Tom's Tree Place for Bell Farms\nPublic Improvement District (PID) Landscape Maintenance Agreement. \n \n5. 16.\nResolution - Business Development: Consider a resolution approving an Economic Development\nGrant and Contract of Lubbock Economic Development Alliance, with Paramount Hardware and\nSupply, LLC, for expansion of its construction materials distribution facility and operations, pursuant to\nSection 501.073 of the Texas Local Government Code. \n \n5. 17.\nResolution - Business Development: Consider a resolution approving an Economic Development\nGrant and Contract of Lubbock Economic Development Alliance, with New Country Organics, Inc.,\nfor expansion of its animal food manufacturing operations, pursuant to Section 501.073 of the Texas\nLocal Government Code. \n \n5. 18.\nResolution - Business Development: Consider a resolution approving an Economic Development\nGrant and Contract of Lubbock Economic Development Alliance, with TTX Company, for expansion\nof its rail car repair, maintenance, and scrapping operation, pursuant to Section 501.073 of the Texas\nLocal Government Code. \n \n5. 19.\nResolution - Municipal Court: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute an Interlocal\nAgreement, and all related documents, by and between the City of Lubbock and Lubbock County,\nTexas, for Juvenile Case Manager Services. \n \n5. 20.\nResolution - Civic Center: Consider a resolution approving the recommendation from the Cultural\nArts Grant Review Sub-Committee and the Civic Lubbock, Inc. Board of Directors, for the 2026\nCultural Arts Grant Program, using Hotel Occupancy Tax funds collected at the end of Fiscal Year\n2024-25. \n \n5. 21.\nResolution - Cultural Arts: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute an amendment to\nthe Economic Development Grant Agreement, by and between the City of Lubbock and Lubbock\nExperience, Inc., a 501(c)(3) non-profit corporation, d/b/a the Lubbock Cultural Arts Foundation, for\nthe City's share of the estimated cost associated with the operation of the Lubbock Cultural Arts\nFoundation. \n\n5. 22.\nResolution - Information Technology: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute\nPurchase Order 33002142, with InsightSoftware LLC, for a 3-Year agreement and Enterprise\nSubscription Conversion, related to financial planning, reporting, analytics, and performance\nmanagement. \n \n5. 23.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Planning (District 4): Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0077, for Zone Case\n3554, a request of The Garrett Group at Keller Williams for Verlene Avant, for a zone change from\nLow Density Single-Family District (SF-2) to Office District (OF), at 12202 Upton Avenue, located\nsouth of 122nd Street and west of Upton Avenue, on 5.1 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2,\nSection 23. \n \n5. 24.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Planning (District 5): Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0078, for Zone Case\n3521-A, a request of Seventeen Services, LLC, for Milwaukee 104, LLC, for a zone change from\nNeighborhood Commercial District (NC) to Auto-Urban Commercial District (AC), at 6602 104th\nStreet, located west of Milwaukee Avenue and north of 104th Street, on 15.03 acres of unplatted land\nout of Block AK, Section 22. \n \n5. 25.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Planning (District 5): Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0079, for Zone Case\n3544, a request for zone changes in City Council District 5, from Low Density Single-Family District\n(SF-2), Heavy Commercial District (HC), Industrial Park District (IP), Light Industrial District (LI),\nand General Industrial District (GI) to Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2), Auto-Urban\nCommercial District (AC), Heavy Commercial District (HC), Industrial Park District (IP), at 5701\n130th Street, Tract A, Huffines Business Park Addition, 5701 130th Street Rear, 2.2 acres of unplatted\nland out of Block AK, Section 8, 5514, 5517, 5608, and 5619 132nd Street, Tracts H, K, P, and Q,\nHuffines Business Park Addition, 13306 and 13312 Chicago Avenue, Tracts U and V, Huffines\nBusiness Park Addition, 5215 and 5217 98th Street, 1.21 acres out of Tracts 6-A and 7-A, Southwest\nLubbock Health Center Addition, 6601-6622 Xenia Avenue, 7501-7513 68th Street, and 6601-6622\nWinfield Avenue, Tracts A-T, Camp HM Business Park Addition, 7018 82nd Street, 1 acre of\nunplatted land out of Block AK, Section 30, 7006 82nd Street, 7.06 acres of unplatted land out of\nBlock AK, Section 30, 7302 82nd Street, Tract A, Rancho Verde Estates Addition, 7304, 7314, and\n7318 82nd Street, Lots 3-A, 4-A, 5-A, and 6-A, A&O Addition, 8101 and 8102 Viola Avenue, Lots\n113-A and 116-A, Rancho Verde Estates Addition, 7412 and 7414 82nd Street, Lot 114-A, Rancho\nVerde Estates Addition, 7415, 7417, 7419, and 7421 81st Street, 7416, 7418, and 7422 82nd Street,\nand 8107 Wausau Avenue, Tract B, Rancho Verde Estates Addition, 6508 Marsha Sharp Freeway, 0.94\nacres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 35, 6602 Marsha Sharp Freeway, Tract A, Water\nRampage Addition, 6102 Norwich Avenue, 0.3 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 35,\n6901 Marsha Sharp Freeway, 12.61 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 35, 6909 Marsha\nSharp Freeway, 2.5 acres of land out of Tract A, Killeen Addition, 6628 66th Street, 3.21 acres of\nunplatted land out of Block AK, Section 35, 7005 Marsha Sharp Freeway, 3.33 acres of unplatted land\nout of Block AK, Section 35, 7101, 7103, 7105, and 7107 Santa Fe Drive, Lots 62-65, Pine Grove\nEstates Addition, 7111 and 7113 Santa Fe Drive, Tracts B-1 and B-2, Pine Grove Estates Addition,\n7102, 7104, 7106, and 7110 Santa Fe Drive, Lots 67-70, Pine Grove Estates Addition, 6409 Upland\nAvenue, the south 101.5 feet of the north 166.5 feet of the west 150 feet of Tract A, Pine Grove Estates\nAddition, 7311 Marsha Sharp Freeway, Tract B, Upland Point Addition, 7313 and 7315 Marsha Sharp\nFreeway, 2.67 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 31, 7002 Upland Avenue, 0.44 acres of\nunplatted land out of Block AK, Section 31, 7004 Upland Avenue, 0.34 acres of unplatted land out of\nBlock AK, Section 31, 7008 Upland Avenue, 0.55 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 31,\n7012 Upland Avenue, 0.68 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 31, 7302 Upland Avenue,\n2.97 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 31, 7304 Upland Avenue, 2.28 acres of unplatted\nland out of Block AK, Section 31, 7306 Upland Avenue, 0.88 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK,\nSection 31, 7308 Upland Avenue, 