820 Spur 327, 4.01 acres of\nunplatted land out of Block AK, Section 36, 5802 Spur 327, Tracts A and B, Womble Addition, 5702\n– 5828 Genoa Avenue, Lots 1-11, Heron Development Addition, 5911 57th Street, 6.88 acres of\nunplatted land out of Block AK, Section 36, 6000 Spur 327, Tracts A and B, Bob Mills Furniture\nCompany Addition, 5423, 5425, and 5435 Frankford Avenue, Tracts 1-A, 1-B, and 1-C, C C Knott\nAddition, 5613 Frankford Avenue, Tract A, Carizma Motors Addition, 5617, 5621, and 5701\nFrankford Avenue, Lots 8, 2-A, 2-B, 27, and 28, Westac Addition, 5310 Englewood Avenue, Lot 25,\nWestac Addition, 5320 Englewood Avenue, 1.39 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 28,\n5614 and 5618 Englewood Avenue, Lots 26-A and 26-B, Westac Addition, 5610, 5702, an"}, {"title": "2026-07-13 - Executive Committee of the Electric Utility Board Meeting- 12:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07132026-137", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "EXECUTIVE COMMITTEE OF THE ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD AGENDA July 13, 2026 12:00pm OPEN/EXECUTIVE SESSION: CITIZENS TOWER 1314 Avenue K Lubbock, Texas 79401 5th Floor Conference Room 501A 1. Call to order. Open Session. 2. Discuss July proposed agenda as attached which may include discussions on the listed agenda items. Executive Session 3. Discuss July proposed agenda as", "text": "EXECUTIVE COMMITTEE \nOF THE \nELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD \nAGENDA \nJuly 13, 2026 \n12:00pm \n \nOPEN/EXECUTIVE SESSION: \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Avenue K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \n5th Floor Conference Room 501A \n\n1. \nCall to order. \n \nOpen Session. \n \n2. \nDiscuss July proposed agenda as attached which may include discussions on the \nlisted agenda items. \n\nExecutive Session \n \n3. \nDiscuss July proposed agenda as attached which may include discussions on the \nfollowing executive session items on the agenda: \n \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.086, \n§552.133, and §552.1331 to discuss and deliberate, on the following competitive matters \nof Lubbock Power & Light, reasonably related to the following categories: \n \n(i) Discussion and deliberation of generation matters, including but not limited to \ngeneration unit and/or portfolio sale opportunities including the potential for unused \nland designated for other uses; \n \n(ii) Discussion regarding real estate purchase and transactions for future substation \ndevelopments; \n \n(iii) Discussion of risk management, contracts, and strategies, customer billing, and usage \ninformation, system load characteristics, and ERCOT power analyses and strategies; \n \n(iv) Plans, studies, proposals, and analyses for system improvements, additions, or sales \n(other than transmission and distribution system improvements inside the service area \nfor which the public power utility is the sole certificated retail provider); \n \n(v) info collected as part of an advanced metering system for usage, services, and billing, \nincluding amounts billed or collected for utility usage \n\n4. \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § \n551.071, seeking the advice of legal counsel about pending or contemplated \nlitigation or settlement offers and hold a consultation with the attorney on matters \nin which the duty of the attorney to the Electric Utility Board under the Texas \nDisciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct of the State Bar of Texas conflicts with \nChapter 551 of the Texas Government Code: \n \n(i) \nlegal advice regarding issues herein listed on the agenda; \n(ii) \nlegal advice and counsel regarding contemplated litigation matters; \n(iii) \nlegal advice and counsel regarding North American Electric Reliability \nCorporation Critical Infrastructure Protection (NERC CIP) standards; \n \n(iv) \nAjay Mitchell v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No. DC-\n2025-CV-1728, proceeding in the 72nd District Court of Lubbock County, Texas. \n \n(v) \nMaria Jesus Tucker v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No. \nDC-2026-CV-0096, proceeding in the 99th District Court of Lubbock County, \nTexas. \n \n5. \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § \n551.072, to deliberate the purchase, exchange, lease, and value of real property. \n \n6. \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § \n551.074(a)(1) to deliberate the appointment and/or reappointment of a public \nofficer or employee (Electric Utility Board). \n \n7. \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § \n551.074(a)(1) to deliberate the appointment, employment, evaluation, and duties of \na public officer or employee (Director of Electric Utilities/Chief Administrative \nOfficer). \n \n8. \nAdjourn. \n \nPosted on the bulletin board outside the south \nentrance of Citizen’s Tower, 1314 Ave. K., \nLubbock, Texas, on the 7th day of July 2026 at \n10:00 A.M. \nBy: \n______________________________ \n \n********************************************************************************************* \nCITY OF LUBBOCK AD HOC COMMITTEE/ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD MEETINGS ARE AVAILABLE TO ALL PERSONS \nREGARDLESS OF DISABILITY. IF YOU REQUIRE SPECIAL ASSISTANCE, PLEASE CONTACT THE CITY SECRETARY AT \n775-2026 OR WRITE P. O. BOX 2000, LUBBOCK, TEXAS 79457, AT LEAST 48 HOURS IN ADVANCE OF THE MEETING. \n********************************************************************************************* \n\n**This is a draft copy prepared for the Electric Utility Board of Lubbock Power & Light’s \nExecutive Committee July meeting. Captions are not final and any items listed herein are \nsubject to change prior to the July 21, 2026 Electric Utility Board meeting. \nThe Electric Utility Board of \nLubbock Power & Light Agenda \nJuly 21, 2026 \n11:00 a.m. \nEdwin “Butch” Davis, P.E., Chair \nEddie Schulz, Vice Chair \nGwen Stafford, Secretary \nDr. Solomon Fields \nDr. Gonzalo Ramirez \nDr. Craig Rhyne \nRhea Hill \nMike Stevens \nThomas Parker \nMark McBrayer, Ex-Officio \nwww.lpandl.com \nJoel Ivy, Chief Administrative Officer \nKeli Swan, General Counsel \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Ave K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \nOPEN SESSION: \nCity Council Chambers \nEXECUTIVE SESSION: \nCitizens Tower \nConference Room 201A and 201B \n1.\nCall to Order.\n2.\nPublic Comment. Public comment is an opportunity for the public to make comments and\nexpress a position on agenda items.\na.\nThis period, of up to thirty minutes, is dedicated to public comment. Each member\nof the public will have three minutes to speak. Any member of the public that\nrequires the assistance from a translator will have six minutes to speak. Comments\nmust be limited to only those items posted on the Electric Utility Board agenda.\nAny member of the public wishing to speak shall sign up on the public comment\nsheet on the day of the Electric Utility Board Meeting, prior to the beginning of the\nmeeting. The Electric Utility Board shall follow the order of persons wishing to\nspeak as listed on the sign-up sheet. If everyone who signed up to speak has been\ngiven an opportunity to speak and the time designated for public comment has not\nbeen exhausted, the Electric Utility Board may, at its discretion, open the floor to\nanyone else wishing to address the Electric Utility Board, subject to all the same\nprocedures and guidelines.\n3.\nThe Electric Utility Board may make any comments related to board member activities,\nevents, community engagement, or current or future agenda items.\n\n**This is a draft copy prepared for the Electric Utility Board of Lubbock Power & Light’s \nExecutive Committee July meeting. Captions are not final and any items listed herein are \nsubject to change prior to the July 21, 2026 Electric Utility Board meeting. \nExecutive Session \n\n4. \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.086, \n§552.133, and §552.1331 to discuss and deliberate, on the following competitive matters \nof Lubbock Power & Light, reasonably related to the following categories: \n \n(i) Discussion and deliberation of generation matters, including but not limited to \ngeneration unit and/or portfolio sale opportunities including the potential for unused \nland designated for other uses; \n \n(ii) Discussion regarding real estate purchase and transactions for future substation \ndevelopments; \n \n(iii) Discussion of risk management, contracts, and strategies, customer billing, and usage \ninformation, system load characteristics, and ERCOT power analyses and strategies; \n \n(iv) Plans, studies, proposals, and analyses for system improvements, additions, or sales \n(other than transmission and distribution system improvements inside the service area \nfor which the public power utility is the sole certificated retail provider); \n \n(v) info collected as part of an advanced metering system for usage, services, and billing, \nincluding amounts billed or collected for utility usage \n \n5. \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.071, \nseeking the advice of legal counsel about pending or contemplated litigation or settlement \noffers and hold a consultation with the attorney on matters in which the duty of the attorney \nto the Electric Utility Board under the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct \nof the State Bar of Texas conflicts with Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code: \n \n(i) \nlegal advice regarding issues herein listed on the agenda; \n(ii) \nlegal advice and counsel regarding contemplated litigation matters; \n(iii) \nlegal advice and counsel regarding North American Electric Reliability \nCorporation Critical Infrastructure Protection (NERC CIP) standards; \n \n(iv) \nAjay Mitchell v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No. DC-\n2025-CV-1728, proceeding in the 72nd District Court of Lubbock County, Texas. \n \n(v) \nMaria Jesus Tucker v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No. \nDC-2026-CV-0096, proceeding in the 99th District Court of Lubbock County, \nTexas. \n \n6. \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.072, to \ndeliberate the purchase, exchange, lease, and value of real property. \n\n**This is a draft copy prepared for the Electric Utility Board of Lubbock Power & Light’s \nExecutive Committee July meeting. Captions are not final and any items listed herein are \nsubject to change prior to the July 21, 2026 Electric Utility Board meeting. \n7. \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1) \nto deliberate the appointment and/or reappointment of a public officer or employee \n(Electric Utility Board). \n \n8. \nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1) \nto deliberate the appointment, employment, evaluation, and duties of a public officer or \nemployee (Director of Electric Utilities/Chief Administrative Officer). \n\n11:30 a.m. – Approximately - Following completion of the Executive Session, the Electric \nUtility Board will reconvene in open session to discuss the items listed below. It is anticipated \nthat the open session will begin at 11:30 a.m. However, this is an approximation, and the \nBoard may begin the open session earlier or later depending on when the Executive Session is \ncomplete. \n \nOpen Session \n \n9. \nApprove the minutes from the Regular Electric Utility Board meeting held on June 16, \n2026. \n \n10. \nPresentation and discussion of the update/report by the LP&L Chief Administrative \nOfficer, or his designee, regarding strategic planning, board committee reports, association \nmatters, ordinance change requests, EUB policy and procedures, customer service, \nbusiness center practices, procedures and policies, transition to retail customer choice, \ncustomer concerns and complaints, billing system, forms and procedures, status of \ntransmission and distribution projects, generation, mutual aid, weather events, outage and \nrestoration efforts, grid matters, Electric Reliability Council of Texas (“ERCOT”) matters \nand committee participation, staffing levels, personnel, regulatory matters, training, \ncybersecurity, mutual aid agreement, and operational performance metrics. \n \n11. \nPresentation and discussion of financial and capital statements, budget, financing options, \naudits, and financial policies of Lubbock Power & Light relating to debt issuance issues \nand historical debt matters, reserve account funding, cost allocation, revenue and expense \nprojections, customer metering, tariff and billing, service and administrative issues, and \nrisk management information including rates. \n \n12. \nDiscuss and take action on the Lubbock Power & Light FY 2026-27 Operating Budget and \nCapital Program and make appropriate recommendations to the City Council of the City of \nLubbock pursuant to §2.03.415(a) of the Code of Ordinances, City of Lubbock, Texas. \n\nConsent \n \n13. \nConsider a resolution authorizing a purchase order for operational software services. \n\n**This is a draft copy prepared for the Electric Utility Board of Lubbock Power & Light’s \nExecutive Committee July meeting. Captions are not final and any items listed herein are \nsubject to change prior to the July 21, 2026 Electric Utility Board meeting. \n14. \nConsider a resolution authorizing a purchase order for fencing and other access control \nitems and improvements. \n15. \nConsider a resolution authorizing a purchase order under Request for Proposal (RFP) 7124-\n26-ELD - LP&L Substation Equipment and Materials for transformers and related \nequipment. \n \n16. \nConsider a resolution authorizing the execution of an agreement under Request for \nProposal (RFP) 7126-26-ELD - LP&L NERC CIP Staffing and Consulting Services. \n \n17. \nPurchase and sale transaction of real property. \n \n18. \nAdjourn. \n \nTHE ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD RESERVES THE RIGHT TO ADJOURN INTO EXECUTIVE SESSION AS AUTHORIZED BY \nTEX. GOV’T CODE § 551.001, ET. SEQ. (THE TEXAS OPEN MEETINGS ACT) ON ANY ITEM ON ITS OPEN MEETING AGENDA \nOR TO RECONVENE IN A CONTINUATION OF THE EXECUTIVE SESSION ON THE EXECUTIVE SESSION ITEMS NOTED \nABOVE, IN ACCORDANCE WITH THE TEXAS OPEN MEETINGS ACT, INCLUDING, WITHOUT LIMITATION §§ 551.071-551.086. \n \nPosted on the bulletin board outside the south \nentrance of Citizens Tower, 1314 Ave. K., \nLubbock, Texas, on the 14th July 2026, at 10:30 \nA.M. \n \nBy:______________________________ \n\nCITY OF LUBBOCK ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD MEETINGS ARE AVAILABLE TO ALL PERSONS REGARDLESS OF DISABILITY. IF YOU \nREQUIRE SPECIAL ASSISTANCE, PLEASE CONTACT THE CITY SECRETARY AT (806)775-2026 OR WRITE P. O. BOX 2000, LUBBOCK, \nTEXAS 79457, AT LEAST 48 HOURS IN ADVANCE OF THE MEETING."}, {"title": "2026-07-09 - Transportation Advisory Committee July 9, 2026, 10:00 a.m., 916 Main Street, 2nd Floor Conference Room, Lubbock, Texas 79401 (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07092026-133", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Transportation Advisory Committee July 9, 2026, 10:00 a.m. 916 Main Street, 2nd Floor Conference Room Lubbock, Texas 79401 1. Call to Order of the Transportation Advisory Committee 2. Acknowledgment of Quorum 3. Safety Procedures 4. Public Comment and Acknowledgment of Guests Members of the public may comment on items listed on the agenda. Comments are limited to three minutes per s", "text": "Transportation Advisory Committee \nJuly 9, 2026, 10:00 a.m. \n916 Main Street, 2nd Floor Conference Room \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \n \n1. \nCall to Order of the Transportation Advisory Committee \n2. \nAcknowledgment of Quorum \n3. \nSafety Procedures \n4. \nPublic Comment and Acknowledgment of Guests \nMembers of the public may comment on items listed on the agenda. Comments are limited \nto three minutes per speaker. \n5. \nConsider approval of the June 4, 2026, meeting minutes for the Transportation Advisory \nCommittee. \nAction Required: \nConsider approval of the June 4, 2026, Transportation Advisory Committee meeting \nminutes. \nStaff Recommendation: \nApprove. \n \n6. \nReceive notice regarding the National Association of Development Organizations \n(NADO) Excellence Award. \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide information regarding the NADO Research Foundation Excellence \nAward for the South Plains Association of Governments South Plains Regional \nCommuter Mobility and Transit Demand Study. The award is scheduled to be recognized \nat the 2026 NADO conference. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion only. No action required. Report findings to the \nTransportation Policy Committee as appropriate. \n \n7. \nDiscuss the Waco model as a potential framework for establishing a predictable annual \nlocal contribution for grant matching and project development. \n\nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide a case study review of how other governmental entities have \nestablished local funding mechanisms to support grant match requirements, project \ndevelopment, and other non-federal funding needs. The discussion will include the Waco \nmodel and other comparable approaches that may assist the MPO in evaluating a \npredictable annual local contribution framework. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion only. No action required. Report findings to the \nTransportation Policy Committee as appropriate. \n \n8. \nReceive an update regarding office space procurement and lease negotiation authority. \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide an update on the current MPO office lease, expiration timing, and the \nneed to evaluate available public and private space options for continued MPO \noperations. The discussion will include the status of procurement and lease negotiation \nauthority previously discussed by the Transportation Policy Committee. \nAction Requested: Discussion only. No action required. Report findings to the \nTransportation Policy Committee as appropriate. \n9. \nFollow up and discussion regarding the DRAFT Metropolitan Transportation Program \n(MTP) full project list. \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide additional information regarding the Draft Metropolitan Transportation \nPlan project list. MTP project lists are reviewed periodically to ensure that listed projects \nremain current, reflect regional transportation needs, and remain consistent with \napplicable TxDOT and federal planning requirements. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion only. No action required. Report findings to the \nTransportation Policy Committee as appropriate. \n \n10. \nReports: \n• \nTxDOT \n• \nCitibus \n1. Update regarding 10CR Fund Transfer \n• \nLPD/DPS \n• \nTexas Tech University \n• \nLMPO’s \n1. MPO, Fiscal Agent, and TxDOT Contract \n2. Grant Pursuits \n11. \nAdjourn \n\nComité Asesor de Transporte \nJulio 9, 2026, 10:00 a. m. \n916 Main Street, Sala de Conferencias, 2nd Floor \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \n1. Llamada al orden del Comité Asesor de Transporte \n2. Confirmación del quórum \n3. Procedimientos de seguridad \n4. Comentarios del público y reconocimiento de invitados \nLos miembros del público pueden comentar sobre los temas incluidos en la agenda. Los \ncomentarios están limitados a tres minutos por orador. \n5. Considerar la aprobación de las actas de la reunión del Junio 4, 2026 del Comité Asesor de \nTransporte. \nAcción requerida: \nConsiderar la aprobación de las actas de la reunión del 4 de junio de 2026 del Comité Asesor \nde Transporte. \nRecomendación del personal: \nAprobar. \n6. Recibir aviso sobre el Premio a la Excelencia de la Asociación Nacional de Organizaciones de \nDesarrollo (NADO). \nDiscusión y recomendación: \nEl personal proporcionará información sobre el Premio a la Excelencia de la Fundación de \nInvestigación de NADO otorgado al Estudio de Movilidad de Viajeros y Demanda de Tránsito \nde la Región de los Llanos del Sur de la Asociación de Gobiernos de los Llanos del Sur. El \npremio será reconocido en la conferencia NADO 2026. \nAcción solicitada: Solo discusión. No se requiere acción. Informar los hallazgos al Comité de \nPolítica de Transporte según corresponda. \n7. Analizar el modelo de Waco como un posible marco para establecer una contribución local \nanual predecible para la combinación de subvenciones y el desarrollo de proyectos. \nDiscusión y recomendación: \nEl personal proporcionará un análisis de caso sobre cómo otras entidades gubernamentales \nhan establecido mecanismos de financiamiento local para respaldar los requisitos de \naportación para subvenciones, el desarrollo de proyectos y otras necesidades de \nfinanciamiento no federal. La discusión incluirá el modelo de Waco y otros enfoques \ncomparables que puedan ayudar al MPO a evaluar un marco de contribución local anual \npredecible. \nAcción solicitada: Solo discusión. No se requiere acción. Informar los hallazgos al Comité de \nPolítica de Transporte según corresponda. \n8. Recibir una actualización sobre la adquisición de espacio de oficina y la autoridad de \nnegociación de arrendamientos. \n\nDiscusión y recomendación: \nEl personal proporcionará una actualización sobre el contrato de arrendamiento actual de la \noficina del MPO, el tiempo de expiración y la necesidad de evaluar opciones disponibles de \nespacio público y privado para continuar las operaciones del MPO. La discusión incluirá el \nestado de la adquisición y la autoridad de negociación de arrendamientos previamente \ndiscutida por el Comité de Política de Transporte. \nAcción solicitada: Solo discusión. No se requiere acción. Informar los hallazgos al Comité de \nPolítica de Transporte según corresponda. \n9. Seguimiento y discusión sobre la lista completa de proyectos del BORRADOR del Programa \nde Transporte Metropolitano (MTP). \nDiscusión y recomendación: \nEl personal proporcionará información adicional sobre la lista de proyectos del Borrador del \nPlan de Transporte Metropolitano. Las listas de proyectos del MTP se revisan periódicamente \npara asegurar que los proyectos incluidos sigan siendo actuales, reflejen las necesidades de \ntransporte regional y permanezcan consistentes con los requisitos de planificación aplicables \nde TxDOT y del gobierno federal. \nAcción solicitada: Solo discusión. No se requiere acción. Informar los hallazgos al Comité de \nPolítica de Transporte según corresponda. \n10. Informes: \n• TxDOT \n• Citibus \n11. Actualización sobre la transferencia del Fondo 10CR \n• LPD/DPS \n• Universidad Texas Tech \n• LMPO \n12. Contrato del MPO, agente fiscal y TxDOT \n13. Búsqueda de subvenciones \n14. Clausura"}, {"title": "2026-07-09 - Transportation Advisory Committee 10:00 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07092026-132", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Transportation Advisory Committee July 9, 2026, 10:00 a.m. 916 Main Street, 2nd Floor Conference Room Lubbock, Texas 79401 1. Call to Order of the Transportation Advisory Committee 2. Acknowledgment of Quorum 3. Safety Procedures 4. Public Comment and Acknowledgment of Guests Members of the public may comment on items listed on the agenda. Comments are limited to three minutes per s", "text": "Transportation Advisory Committee \nJuly 9, 2026, 10:00 a.m. \n916 Main Street, 2nd Floor Conference Room \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \n \n1. \nCall to Order of the Transportation Advisory Committee \n2. \nAcknowledgment of Quorum \n3. \nSafety Procedures \n4. \nPublic Comment and Acknowledgment of Guests \nMembers of the public may comment on items listed on the agenda. Comments are limited \nto three minutes per speaker. \n5. \nConsider approval of the June 4, 2026, meeting minutes for the Transportation Advisory \nCommittee. \nAction Required: \nConsider approval of the June 4, 2026, Transportation Advisory Committee meeting \nminutes. \nStaff Recommendation: \nApprove. \n \n6. \nReceive notice regarding the National Association of Development Organizations \n(NADO) Excellence Award. \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide information regarding the NADO Research Foundation Excellence \nAward for the South Plains Association of Governments South Plains Regional \nCommuter Mobility and Transit Demand Study. The award is scheduled to be recognized \nat the 2026 NADO conference. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion only. No action required. Report findings to the \nTransportation Policy Committee as appropriate. \n \n7. \nDiscuss the Waco model as a potential framework for establishing a predictable annual \nlocal contribution for grant matching and project development. \n\nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide a case study review of how other governmental entities have \nestablished local funding mechanisms to support grant match requirements, project \ndevelopment, and other non-federal funding needs. The discussion will include the Waco \nmodel and other comparable approaches that may assist the MPO in evaluating a \npredictable annual local contribution framework. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion only. No action required. Report findings to the \nTransportation Policy Committee as appropriate. \n \n8. \nReceive an update regarding office space procurement and lease negotiation authority. \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide an update on the current MPO office lease, expiration timing, and the \nneed to evaluate available public and private space options for continued MPO \noperations. The discussion will include the status of procurement and lease negotiation \nauthority previously discussed by the Transportation Policy Committee. \nAction Requested: Discussion only. No action required. Report findings to the \nTransportation Policy Committee as appropriate. \n9. \nFollow up and discussion regarding the DRAFT Metropolitan Transportation Program \n(MTP) full project list. \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide additional information regarding the Draft Metropolitan Transportation \nPlan project list. MTP project lists are reviewed periodically to ensure that listed projects \nremain current, reflect regional transportation needs, and remain consistent with \napplicable TxDOT and federal planning requirements. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion only. No action required. Report findings to the \nTransportation Policy Committee as appropriate. \n \n10. \nReports: \n• \nTxDOT \n• \nCitibus \n1. Update regarding 10CR Fund Transfer \n• \nLPD/DPS \n• \nTexas Tech University \n• \nLMPO’s \n1. MPO, Fiscal Agent, and TxDOT Contract \n2. Grant Pursuits \n11. \nAdjourn \n\nComité Asesor de Transporte \nJulio 9, 2026, 10:00 a. m. \n916 Main Street, Sala de Conferencias, 2nd Floor \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \n1. Llamada al orden del Comité Asesor de Transporte \n2. Confirmación del quórum \n3. Procedimientos de seguridad \n4. Comentarios del público y reconocimiento de invitados \nLos miembros del público pueden comentar sobre los temas incluidos en la agenda. Los \ncomentarios están limitados a tres minutos por orador. \n5. Considerar la aprobación de las actas de la reunión del Junio 4, 2026 del Comité Asesor de \nTransporte. \nAcción requerida: \nConsiderar la aprobación de las actas de la reunión del 4 de junio de 2026 del Comité Asesor \nde Transporte. \nRecomendación del personal: \nAprobar. \n6. Recibir aviso sobre el Premio a la Excelencia de la Asociación Nacional de Organizaciones de \nDesarrollo (NADO). \nDiscusión y recomendación: \nEl personal proporcionará información sobre el Premio a la Excelencia de la Fundación de \nInvestigación de NADO otorgado al Estudio de Movilidad de Viajeros y Demanda de Tránsito \nde la Región de los Llanos del Sur de la Asociación de Gobiernos de los Llanos del Sur. El \npremio será reconocido en la conferencia NADO 2026. \nAcción solicitada: Solo discusión. No se requiere acción. Informar los hallazgos al Comité de \nPolítica de Transporte según corresponda. \n7. Analizar el modelo de Waco como un posible marco para establecer una contribución local \nanual predecible para la combinación de subvenciones y el desarrollo de proyectos. \nDiscusión y recomendación: \nEl personal proporcionará un análisis de caso sobre cómo otras entidades gubernamentales \nhan establecido mecanismos de financiamiento local para respaldar los requisitos de \naportación para subvenciones, el desarrollo de proyectos y otras necesidades de \nfinanciamiento no federal. La discusión incluirá el modelo de Waco y otros enfoques \ncomparables que puedan ayudar al MPO a evaluar un marco de contribución local anual \npredecible. \nAcción solicitada: Solo discusión. No se requiere acción. Informar los hallazgos al Comité de \nPolítica de Transporte según corresponda. \n8. Recibir una actualización sobre la adquisición de espacio de oficina y la autoridad de \nnegociación de arrendamientos. \n\nDiscusión y recomendación: \nEl personal proporcionará una actualización sobre el contrato de arrendamiento actual de la \noficina del MPO, el tiempo de expiración y la necesidad de evaluar opciones disponibles de \nespacio público y privado para continuar las operaciones del MPO. La discusión incluirá el \nestado de la adquisición y la autoridad de negociación de arrendamientos previamente \ndiscutida por el Comité de Política de Transporte. \nAcción solicitada: Solo discusión. No se requiere acción. Informar los hallazgos al Comité de \nPolítica de Transporte según corresponda. \n9. Seguimiento y discusión sobre la lista completa de proyectos del BORRADOR del Programa \nde Transporte Metropolitano (MTP). \nDiscusión y recomendación: \nEl personal proporcionará información adicional sobre la lista de proyectos del Borrador del \nPlan de Transporte Metropolitano. Las listas de proyectos del MTP se revisan periódicamente \npara asegurar que los proyectos incluidos sigan siendo actuales, reflejen las necesidades de \ntransporte regional y permanezcan consistentes con los requisitos de planificación aplicables \nde TxDOT y del gobierno federal. \nAcción solicitada: Solo discusión. No se requiere acción. Informar los hallazgos al Comité de \nPolítica de Transporte según corresponda. \n10. Informes: \n• TxDOT \n• Citibus \n11. Actualización sobre la transferencia del Fondo 10CR \n• LPD/DPS \n• Universidad Texas Tech \n• LMPO \n12. Contrato del MPO, agente fiscal y TxDOT \n13. Búsqueda de subvenciones \n14. Clausura"}, {"title": "2026-07-09 - North Overton Tax Increment Financing Reinvestment Zone Board - 7:30 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07092026-118", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the South entrance of Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______________, 2026 at___________ A.M/P.M. _____________________________ Jeff Dane, Chair Timothy Pridmore, Vice Chair Jason Coleman County Commissioner Cary Shaw Kurt Metcalf Patrick Murfee Senator Charles Perry House Representative Carl Tepper Lisa", "text": "The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the South entrance of Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, \nLubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______________, 2026 at___________ A.M/P.M.\n _____________________________\nJeff Dane, Chair\nTimothy Pridmore, Vice Chair\nJason Coleman\nCounty Commissioner Cary Shaw\nKurt Metcalf\nPatrick Murfee\nSenator Charles Perry\nHouse Representative Carl Tepper\nLisa West\nNorth Overton Tax Increment Financing Reinvestment Zone Board \nAgenda\n July 9, 2026 | 7:30 A.M.\nCitizens Tower, 2nd Floor, Room 201A/B\n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas\nDanielle Williams, Business\nDevelopment Director\nMolly Ballesteros, Special\nDistricts Manager\nNorth Overton Tax Increment Financing Reinvestment Zone Board meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability.\nIf you require special assistance, please contact the Business Development Department at 806.775.3082, e-mail\nbusinessdevelopment@mylubbock.us, or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of\nthe meeting.\n1.\nCall to order\n2.\nCitizen Comments - Any citizen wishing to appear before the North Overton Tax Increment Financing \nReinvestment Zone Board, regarding any matter posted on the agenda below, shall complete the sign-up \nform provided at the meeting, no later than 7:30 a.m. Citizen Comments is an opportunity for citizens to \nmake comments and express a position on agenda items. Each comment will be read aloud for a period of \ntime not to exceed three minutes pursuant to City Council rules. Any comment received that is in the nature\nof asking a question will be referred to an appropriate member of the City staff for a response and will not \nbe read aloud at the meeting.\n3.\nRegular Session\n3.1\nApproval of Minutes – April 2, 2026\n3.2\nDiscuss and take appropriate action on the Financial Report\n3.3\nDiscuss and take appropriate action on the Fiscal Year 2026-27 Budget\n3.4\nDiscuss and take appropriate action on a Tree Management Scope of Work\n4.\nWork Session\n4.1\nPublic Improvement Project Updates\n5.\nSchedule Next Meeting – The suggested meeting date is January 17, 2027, at 7:30 A.M."}, {"title": "2026-07-09 - July 9, 2026 Special City Council Meeting (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07092026-130", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Special City Council Meeting July 9, 2026 Mark W. McBrayer, Mayor Dr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5 Christy Martinez-Garcia, District 1 Gordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 David Bruegel, Councilman, District 3 Brayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 Tim Collins, Councilman, District 6 W. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager Matt Wade, City Attorney Courtney", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas \nSpecial City Council Meeting \nJuly 9, 2026\nMark W. McBrayer, Mayor \nDr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5 \nChristy Martinez-Garcia, District 1 \nGordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 \nDavid Bruegel, Councilman, District 3 \nBrayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 \nTim Collins, Councilman, District 6\n \nW. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager \nMatt Wade, City Attorney \nCourtney Paz, City Secretary\nhttp://www.mylubbock.us \n \nConference Rooms CT201A and CT201B, Conference Center, 2nd Floor of Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas\nCity of Lubbock City Council Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please \ncontact the City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in \nadvance of the meeting.\nExecutive Session Disclosure Statement: The City Council reserves the right to adjourn into executive session at any time during the \ncourse of the meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized by Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code, \nincluding but not necessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with Attorney), §551.072 (Deliberations regarding Real Property), \n§551.073 (Deliberations regarding Gifts and Donations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076 (Deliberations regarding \nSecurity Devices), §551.086 (Public Power Utilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations regarding Economic \nDevelopment Negotiations).\nA quorum of the City Council will be physically present in CT201A and CT201B in the Conference Center on the 2nd Floor of \nCitizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, as it is the intent of the City Council to have a quorum physically present at this \nlocation. One or more members of the City Council, however, may participate in the meeting by video-conference call as permitted \nunder Section 551.127 of the Texas Government Code.\nNote: On occasion, the City Council may consider agenda items out of order.\n1:00 p.m. - City Council convenes in Conference Rooms CT201A and CT201B in the Conference \nCenter on the 2nd Floor of Citizens Tower in Work Session, regarding items for presentation and \ndiscussion on such issues that may require in-depth consideration by the City Council. No action \nwill be taken by the City Council in Work Session. \n\n1.\nHear and discuss presentations regarding the following and provide guidance to staff as \nappropriate.\n \n1.1.\nDiscuss planning, goals, and budget priorities for the Lubbock City Council and the City of \nLubbock, and discuss the programs, expenditures and policies of the City Council and the City of \nLubbock, including but not limited to the Lubbock City Charter, ARPA funding, annexation and \ngrowth, aquatics, substance abuse, municipal fees, transportation, infrastructure, solid waste, \nstreets, Gateway Fund, Civic Center, Lubbock Animal Services, libraries, community \nengagement, capital improvements, facilities, parks and recreation, development services, \nlegislative agenda, public safety, public health, public safety infrastructure, code enforcement, \nstaffing levels, and future needs. This discussion may include a wide range of topics related to the \nprograms, operation, and policies of the City Council and the City of Lubbock. The City Council \nmay give direction to the City Manager in regard to determining the priorities and goals discussed."}, {"title": "2026-07-08 - Willow Bend Villas Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting - 4:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07082026-125", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "1314 Avenue K ◊ Lubbock, Texas 79401 ◊ 806.775.3083 ◊ businessdevelopment@mylubbock.us June 17, 2026 Current Owner «Mailing_Address» Re: Property «Property_Address» Dear Property Owner: The annual Willow Bend Villas Public Improvement District (PID) Property Owners’ Meeting will be held Wednesday, July 8, 2026, in the City Council Chambers on the first floor of Citizens Tower, located at 1314 Aven", "text": "1314 Avenue K ◊ Lubbock, Texas 79401 ◊ 806.775.3083 ◊ businessdevelopment@mylubbock.us\nJune 17, 2026\nCurrent Owner\n«Mailing_Address»\nRe: Property\n«Property_Address»\nDear Property Owner:\nThe annual Willow Bend Villas Public Improvement District (PID) Property Owners’\nMeeting will be held Wednesday, July 8, 2026, in the City Council Chambers on the first\nfloor of Citizens Tower, located at 1314 Avenue K, from 4:00 to 5:00 p.m.\nYou are invited to attend and provide input for the proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service\nand Assessment Plan. Once the Service and Assessment Plan is finalized, it will be\npresented to the City Council for approval. A public hearing is held prior to consideration\nby the City Council, and all property owners will be notified of the time and date of the\npublic hearing. \nCity staff looks forward to seeing you there!\nThank you,\nMolly Ballesteros\nSpecial Districts Manager"}, {"title": "2026-07-08 - Upland Crossing 3 Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting Agenda - 2:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07082026-122", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Upland Crossing 3 Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting Agenda July 8, 2026 2:00 P.M. Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers 1.0 Welcome and Introduction of City Staff Molly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager 2.0 Discussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan Molly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager 3.0 Public Comment Open forum for the", "text": "Upland Crossing 3 Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 8, 2026\n2:00 P.M.\nCitizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers\n1.0\nWelcome and Introduction of City Staff\nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n2.0\nDiscussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n3.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the Upland Crossing 3 PID to voice\nconcerns, suggestions, and preferences for Upland Crossing 3 PID and the Upland\nCrossing 3 Service and Assessment Plan.\nUpland Crossing 3 Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons\nregardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at\n806.775.3083 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of\nthe meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at\n1314 Avenue K, West of the revolving doors, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______, 2026\nat _________ A.M./P.M.\nBy: ______________________________________"}, {"title": "2026-07-08 - Upland Crossing 2 Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting Agenda - 1:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07082026-124", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Upland Crossing 2 Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting Agenda July 8, 2026 1:00 P.M. Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers 1.0 Welcome and Introduction of City Staff Molly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager 2.0 Discussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan Molly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager 3.0 Public Comment Open forum for the", "text": "Upland Crossing 2 Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 8, 2026\n1:00 P.M.\nCitizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers\n1.0\nWelcome and Introduction of City Staff\nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n2.0\nDiscussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n3.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the Upland Crossing 2 PID to voice\nconcerns, suggestions, and preferences for Upland Crossing 2 PID and the Upland\nCrossing 2 Service and Assessment Plan.\nUpland Crossing 2 Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons\nregardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at\n806.775.3083 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of\nthe meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at\n1314 Avenue K, West of the revolving doors, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______, 2026\nat _________ A.M./P.M.\nBy: ______________________________________"}, {"title": "2026-07-08 - North Overton Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting - 12:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07082026-129", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "North Overton Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at 1314 Avenue K, Wes", "text": "North Overton Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless\nof disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083\nor write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at\n1314 Avenue K, West of the revolving doors, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______, 2026\nat _________ A.M./P.M. by Molly Ballesteros. \nNorth Overton Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 8, 2026\n12:00 P.M.\nCitizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers\n1.0\nWelcome and Introduction of City Staff\nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n2.0\nDiscussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n3.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the North Overton PID to voice concerns,\nsuggestions, and preferences for North Overton PID and the North Overton PID\nService and Assessment Plan."}, {"title": "2026-07-08 - Cypress Ranch Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting - 3:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07082026-127", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Cypress Ranch Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at 1314 Avenue K, Wes", "text": "Cypress Ranch Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless\nof disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083\nor write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at\n1314 Avenue K, West of the revolving doors, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______, 2026\nat _________ A.M./P.M. by Molly Ballesteros.\nCypress Ranch Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 8, 2026\n3:00 P.M.\nCitizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers\n1.0\nWelcome and Introduction of City Staff\nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n2.0\nDiscussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n3.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the Cypress Ranch PID to voice concerns,\nsuggestions, and preferences for Cypress Ranch PID and the Cypress Ranch\nService and Assessment Plan."}, {"title": "2026-07-08 - Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers - 1:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07082026-120", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "North Overton Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. The above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at 1314 Avenue", "text": "North Overton Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless \nof disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 \nor write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at \n1314 Avenue K, West of the revolving doors, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____ day of ______, 2026 \nat _________ A.M./P.M. by Molly Ballesteros. \n \nNorth Overton Public Improvement District \nProperty Owners Meeting \nAgenda \n \nJuly 8, 2026 \n1:00 P.M. \nCitizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K | City Council Chambers \n\n1.0 Welcome and Introduction of City Staff \n \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager \n \n2.0 Discussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager \n \n3.0 Public Comment \nOpen forum for the property owners in the North Overton PID to voice concerns, \nsuggestions, and preferences for North Overton PID and the North Overton PID \nService and Assessment Plan."}, {"title": "2026-07-07 - July 7, 2026 Special City Council Meeting (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07072026-114", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Special City Council Meeting July 7, 2026 Mark W. McBrayer, Mayor Dr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5 Christy Martinez-Garcia, District 1 Gordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 David Bruegel, Councilman, District 3 Brayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 Tim Collins, Councilman, District 6 W. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager Matt Wade, City Attorney Courtney Paz, City", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nSpecial City Council Meeting\nJuly 7, 2026\nMark W. McBrayer, Mayor\nDr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5\nChristy Martinez-Garcia, District 1\nGordon Harris, Councilman, District 2\nDavid Bruegel, Councilman, District 3\nBrayden Rose, Councilman, District 4\nTim Collins, Councilman, District 6\n \nW. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager\nMatt Wade, City Attorney\nCourtney Paz, City Secretary\nhttp://www.mylubbock.us\n Lubbock Memorial Civic Center, 2nd Floor, Terrace Suite, 1501 Mac Davis Lane, Lubbock, Texas\n \nCity of Lubbock City Council Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact\nthe City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the\nmeeting. \nExecutive Session Disclosure Statement: The City Council reserves the right to adjourn into executive session at any time during the course of\nthe meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized by Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code, including but not\nnecessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with Attorney), §551.072 (Deliberations regarding Real Property), §551.073 (Deliberations\nregarding Gifts and Donations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076 (Deliberations regarding Security Devices), §551.086 (Public\nPower Utilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations regarding Economic Development Negotiations). \nA quorum of the City Council will be physically present in the Terrace Suite located on the 2nd Floor of the Lubbock Memorial Civic Center,\n1501 Mac Davis Lane, Lubbock, Texas, as it is the intent of the City Council to have a quorum physically present at this location. One or more\nmembers of the City Council, however, may participate in the meeting by video-conference call as permitted under Section 551.127 of the\nTexas Government Code. \nNote: On occasion, the City Council may consider agenda items out of order. \n. \n \n5:00 p.m. - City Council convenes in Open Session in the Lubbock Memorial Civic Center Terrace Suite, at\n1501 Mac Davis Lane, Lubbock, Texas, and immediately recesses into Executive Session.\n\n1.\nExecutive Session\n \n1. 1.\nHold an executive session in accordance with the Texas Government Code, Section 551.076 to deliberate\nthe deployment and/or implementation of security personnel or devices. \n \nAdjourn from Executive Session\n \nImmediately Following Executive Session, the City Council will reconvene in the Terrace Suite in Open\nSession.\n \n2.\nCitizen Comments - According to Lubbock City Council Rules, any citizen wishing to appear\nin-person before a regular meeting of the City Council, regarding any matter posted on the City\nCouncil Agenda below, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than 5:00\np.m. on July 7, 2026. Citizen Comments provide an opportunity for citizens to make comments and\nexpress a position on agenda items.\n\n3.\nRegular Agenda\n \n3. 1.\nResolution - City Secretary: Consider a resolution canvassing the returns and declaring the results of the\nCity of Lubbock Special Election held on June 27, 2026, and ordering a Runoff Election to be held on\nAugust 1, 2026."}, {"title": "2026-07-07 - July 7, 2026 Public Meeting - Data Centers (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07072026-115", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Special City Council Meeting - Public Meeting July 7, 2026 Mark W. McBrayer, Mayor Dr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5 Christy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1 Gordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 David Bruegel, Councilman, District 3 Brayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 Tim Collins, Councilman, District 6 W. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager Matt Wade, C", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nSpecial City Council Meeting - Public Meeting\nJuly 7, 2026\nMark W. McBrayer, Mayor\nDr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5\nChristy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1\nGordon Harris, Councilman, District 2\nDavid Bruegel, Councilman, District 3\nBrayden Rose, Councilman, District 4\nTim Collins, Councilman, District 6\n \nW. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager\nMatt Wade, City Attorney\nCourtney Paz, City Secretary\nhttp://www.mylubbock.us\nLubbock Memorial Civic Center, Banquet Hall, 1501 Mac Davis Lane, Lubbock, Texas\nCity of Lubbock City Council Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the\nCity Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \nExecutive Session Disclosure Statement: The City Council reserves the right to adjourn into executive session at any time during the course of\nthe meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized by Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code, including but not\nnecessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with Attorney), §551.072 (Deliberations regarding Real Property), §551.073 (Deliberations\nregarding Gifts and Donations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076 (Deliberations regarding Security Devices), §551.086 (Public Power\nUtilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations regarding Economic Development Negotiations). \nA quorum of the City Council will be physically present in the Lubbock Memorial Civic Center Banquet Hall, 1501 Mac Davis Lane, Lubbock,\nTexas, as it is the intent of the City Council to have a quorum physically present at this location. One or more members of the City Council,\nhowever, may participate in the meeting by video-conference call as permitted under Section 551.127 of the Texas Government Code. \nNote: On occasion, the City Council may consider agenda items out of order. \n\n5:30 p.m. - City Council convenes in Open Session in the Lubbock Memorial Civic Center Banquet Hall at\n1501 Mac Davis Lane, to receive a brief presentation from City Staff regarding large-scale data centers, and\nfor the City Council and City Staff to hear citizen comments regarding their thoughts, concerns, and\nperspectives on large-scale data centers. The City Council will not take any official action during this meeting.\nCitizens wishing to speak at the meeting may sign up in person outside the Banquet Hall before or during the\nmeeting."}, {"title": "2026-07-07 - Financial Review Committee and Special Meeting of the Electric Utility Board (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07072026-113", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "FINANCIAL REVIEW COMMITTEE AND SPECIAL MEETING OF THE ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD AGENDA July 7, 2026 11:00am OPEN SESSION: CITIZENS TOWER 1314 Avenue K Lubbock, Texas 79401 Council Workroom 101A Open Session 1. Call to order. 2. Public Comment. Public comment is an opportunity for the public to make comments and express a position on agenda items. a. This period, of up to thirty minutes, is", "text": "FINANCIAL REVIEW COMMITTEE AND SPECIAL MEETING OF THE \nELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD \nAGENDA \nJuly 7, 2026 \n11:00am \nOPEN SESSION: \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Avenue K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \nCouncil Workroom 101A \nOpen Session \n1.\nCall to order.\n2.\nPublic Comment. Public comment is an opportunity for the public to make comments and\nexpress a position on agenda items.\na.\nThis period, of up to thirty minutes, is dedicated to public comment. Each member of the\npublic will have three minutes to speak. Any member of the public that requires assistance\nof a translator will have six minutes to speak. Comments must be limited to only those\nitems posted on the Financial Review Committee /EUB agenda. Any member of the public\nwishing to speak shall sign up on the public comment sheet on the day of the Financial\nReview Committee /EUB meeting. The Financial Review Committee /EUB shall follow\nthe order of people wishing to speak as listed on the sign-up sheet. If everyone who signed\nup to speak has been given an opportunity to speak and the thirty minutes designated for\npublic comment has not been exhausted, the Financial Review Committee /EUB may, at\nits discretion, open the floor to anyone else wishing to address the Financial Review\nCommittee /EUB, subject to all the same procedures and guidelines.\n3. Approve the minutes from the last Financial Review Committee meeting held on February\n11, 2026.\n4. Presentation and discussion of the Fiscal Year 2026-27 Draft Budget. Discussion may include\nbut not limited to, revenue and expense projections, tariff and billing, service and\nadministrative issues, including risk management information and rates and strategic plan,\nborrowing related to transmission projects, and proceeds from generation sale.\n5. Adjourn.\nPosted on the bulletin board outside the south entrance of \nCitizen’s Tower, 1314 Ave. K., Lubbock, Texas, on the \n29th day of June 2026, at 4:30pm. \nBy: \n************************************************************************************************************************ \nCITY OF LUBBOCK FINANCIAL REVIEW COMMITTEE /ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD MEETINGS ARE AVAILABLE TO ALL \nPERSONS REGARDLESS OF DISABILITY. IF YOU REQUIRE SPECIAL ASSISTANCE, PLEASE CONTACT THE CITY SECRETARY \nAT 775-2026 OR WRITE P. O. BOX 2000, LUBBOCK, TEXAS 79457, AT LEAST 48 HOURS IN ADVANCE OF THE MEETING. \n************************************************************************************************************************\nBeatrice Duenez\n\nFINANCIAL REVIEW COMMITTEE OF THE \nELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD \nMINUTES \nFebruary 11, 2026 \n2:00PM \nOPEN SESSION: \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Avenue K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \nCouncil Workroom 101A \nEUB \nLP&L Staff \nEddie Schulz \nGwen Stafford \nDr. Craig Rhyne \nRhea Hill \nHarvey Hall \nKeli Swan \nJoel Ivy \nKacey Johnson \nEric Von Heuvel \nMatt Rose \nDaniel Garcia \nBlair McGinnis \nBrian Atwood \nRicky Rodriguez \nOpen Session \nThe meeting was called to order at 2 p.m. Central time. \n1. Approve the minutes from the last Financial Review Committee meeting held on October 21, 2025.\nA motion to approve the minutes from the October 2025 meeting was made by Vice Chair Eddie Schulz,\nwhich was seconded by Secretary Gwen Stafford. The motion passed by a vote of 4-0.\n2. Presentation and discussion of the Fiscal Year 2025-26 Draft Annual Financial Report. Discussion may\ninclude but limited to revenue and expense projections, tariff and billing, service and administrative issues,\nincluding risk management information and rates.\nChief Financial Officer Harvey Hall discussed the history of the financial impact of the transition into\nERCOT. Auditor Jennifer Ripka joined the meeting to answer questions about the audit. Mr. Hall handed\nout an Annual Report to the Financial Review Committee members.\nKacey Johnson discussed operating revenues and trends from FY24-25. Ms. Ripka noted that she\nanticipates a letter indicating the highest level of confidence in the financial report.\n3. Adjourn\n\nThere being no further business to come before the Board, Committee Chair Rhyne adjourned the \nmeeting at 2:49pm. \nPrepared and Approved by: \n________________________________ \nDr. Craig Rhyne, Chair \n\n…securing the future \nDRAFT\n\nFY 2026-27 \nProposed Operating Budget & Capital Program (DRAFT) \n\nFY 2026-27 Proposed Operating Budget & Capital Program (DRAFT) \n \nElected, Appointed and Principal Officials/Key Finance Staff \n\nCity Council \nMark McBrayer \nMayor \nChristy Martinez-Garcia \nCouncil Member – District 1 \nGordon Harris \nCouncil Member – District 2 \nDavid Bruegel \nCouncil Member – District 3 \nBraden Rose \nCouncil Member – District 4 \nDr. Jennifer Wilson \nCouncil Member – District 5 \nTim Collins \nCouncil Member – District 6 \n \nElectric Utility Board \n\nEdwin E. “Butch” Davis \nChair \nEddie Schulz \nVice Chair \nGwen Stafford \nSecretary \nDr. Gonzalo Ramirez \nBoard Member \nDr. Solomon Fields \nBoard Member \nDr. Craig Rhyne \nBoard Member \nRhea Hill \nBoard Member \nMike Stevens \nBoard Member \nThomas Parker \nBoard Member \n\nPrincipal Officials and Financial Management \n \nJoel Ivy \nChief Administrative Officer \nKeli Swan \nGeneral Counsel – LP&L \nHarvey Hall \nChief Financial Officer \nBlair McGinnis \nChief Operating Officer \nDaniel Garcia \nDirector of Financial Planning and Analysis \nKacey Sylvia \nDirector of Finance \nMatthew Rose \nGovernment Relations and Public Affairs \n\nFY 2026-27 Proposed Operating Budget & Capital Program (DRAFT) \n\nTable of Contents \n\nBudget Overview\n1\nFinancial Model\n \n7\nStaffing\n \n9\nFund Overview\n11\nDepartment Overview\n \n12\nFunctional Overviews\n \n14\nDepartment Expenses\n \n18\nCapital Program – Appropriation Summary\n \n23\nCapital Program – FERC Category Summary\n \n24\nCapital Program – Funding Summary\n \n25\nCapital Program – Capital Project Details \n \n27\nList of Terms and Acronyms\n \n81\n\nLubbock Power & Light Overview \n \nMission and Purpose \nThe purpose of Lubbock Power and Light (LP&L) is “the provision of dependable and reliable electric service at reasonable \nand competitive rates to the citizens of Lubbock while being financially self-sustaining.” (Lubbock City Charter) The mission \nof Lubbock Power & Light is to deliver reliable and resilient utility services through the effective operation and maintenance \nof: \n \nLP&L: Electric transmission, distribution, substation, market, outage call center, metering, billing, and revenue \nmanagement systems. \n \nCOLU (City of Lubbock Utilities): Water, wastewater, solid waste and storm water utilities customer service call \ncenter, metering, billing, and revenue management systems. \n \nStrategic Plan \nVision: To be the leading municipally owned utility, consistently delivering exceptional system delivery services and ensuring \ncustomer satisfaction. LP&L is dedicated to true business sustainability through forward-thinking innovation, data-driven and \nrisk informed decision-making, and a commitment to employee respect while effectively addressing customer needs. \n \nStrategy: To foster a culture of innovation, safety, and excellence. We strive to build a performance-driven environment with \na target of zero safety incidents, accidents, or failures. This commitment is achieved by empowering employees and \nleadership to be open-minded, adaptable, fast-paced, resilient, and skilled in implementing effective and timely innovations \nand technologies. \n \nObjective 1: Deliver exceptional customer value by optimizing operations for efficiency, reliability, and responsiveness across \nall service touchpoints. As industry leaders in operational management, we strive to be the employer of choice by fostering \ninnovation, prioritizing safety, and investing in our people, technology, and equipment. Our commitment to excellence drives \nus to continuously improve while staying true to our mission and purpose. \n \nPerformance Measure: Employee Lost Time Incidents \n \nPerformance Measure: Employee Injury Rate \n \nPerformance Measure: System Outage Call Center Activity – Total calls and handling time. \n \nPerformance Measure: System Outage Calls vs. Wait Time \n \nSystem Delivery Rates compared with other TDU’s - Annually \n \nObjective 2: Achieve operational excellence through investment and improvements in grid reliability and resilience, \nleveraging advanced technologies and proactive risk management. \n \nPerformance Measure: Average Customer Outage Restoration Time – “Customer Average Interruption Duration \nIndex (CAIDI)” \n \nPerformance Measure Average System Outage Restoration Time – “System Average Interruption Duration Index \n(SAIDI)” \n \nPerformance Measure: “System Average Interruption Frequency Index (SAIFI)” \n \nObjective 3: Strengthen financial resilience and long-term sustainability by leveraging advanced analytics, dynamic reporting, \nand integrated enterprise risk management (ERM). We deliver timely, accurate, and insightful decision-making information \nto the Board and Executive staff to drive strategic success. \n \nPerformance Measure: Days Cash on Hand \n \nPerformance Measure: Debt Service Reserve Ratio \n \nPerformance Measure: Debt/Asset Ratio \n \nPerformance Measure: ERM Inventory Top 10 score \n \nAccomplishments for Fiscal Year (FY) 2025-26 \n \nApplied for a Department of Energy SPARK Grant to support transmission system upgrades and system \nmodernization projects. \n \nReduced average outage restoration time from 66 minutes to 50 minutes through operational improvements. \n \nCompleted ARC Flash Project in accordance with the NFPA 70E to improve employee safety. \n \nCompleted engineering design work for upgrades at Erskine Substation to support future load growth. \nFY 2026-27 Proposed Operating Budget and Capital Program - 1\nTable of Contents\n\nLubbock Power & Light Overview \n \n \nCompleted design and construction of multiple distribution system upgrades identified in 15-year plan, including \nthree reconductors to support improve reliability and capacity. \n \nContinued work to eliminate legacy 4 kV infrastructure to improve system safety and reliability. \n \nDeveloped a GIS data model for LP&L’s fiber optic network to improve management and planning. \n \nSupported ERCOT weatherization inspection resulting in full compliance. \n \nCompleted phase one of the router replacement initiative, upgrading network infrastructure and improving system \nperformance. \n \nTransitioned streetlights from High Pressure Sodium and Mercury Vapor lights to energy-efficient LED lighting. \n \nEstablished a maintenance program to inspect and perform preventative maintenance on “trouble circuits” \nimproving overall system reliability. \n \nCompleted multiple feeder line buildouts and two reconductoring projects to improve load balancing and support \nfuture growth. \n \nAdvanced SCADA/EMS upgrade to improve system monitoring and control capabilities. \n \nAdvanced deployment and evaluation of cybersecurity tools. \n \nEnhanced cybersecurity through system scanning and implementation of recommended improvements. \n \nInstalled backup servers to improve system resiliency and reduce risk of service interruption. \n \nLaunched Supervisor/Emerging Leader Academy and continued the Functional Manager Academy to strengthen \nworkforce development. \n \nImplemented Smart Meter Texas integration, improving customer access to usage data and supporting long-term \nsystem modernization. \n \nExpanded operational support for Water Utilities, including: \no \nTransition of water dispatching responsibilities to Revenue Management \no \nOwnership of IWMP processes within CIS \no \nManagement of Fire Hydrant account processes \no \nJoint ownership between Customer Service & Revenue Management of the Water Adjustment process \n \nObjectives for FY 2026-27 \n \nImplement Governance, Risk, and Compliance platform to strength compliance and oversight. \n \nContinue design and begin construction of West Loop Transmission Project. \n \nImplement an Enterprise Risk Management program to identify and mitigate system and operational risks. \n \nContinue conversion of the legacy 4KV infrastructure to improve reliability and reduce system risks. \n \nBegin preparation for replacement of the Posey T2 and Oliver T4 substation transformers. \n \nComplete the fiber upgrade for the Emergency Operations Center and backup control rooms. \n \nCommission Erskine substation and associated feeders to increase capacity in the downtown area. \n \nBegin design of Collier and McCalla substation to support future load growth. \n \nReplace 115kV Breakers across the system to improve reliability. \n \nContinue Northeast Voltage Conversion to expand the 23kV loop to improve reliability and capacity. \n \nBegin deployment of advanced transformer grid monitoring to improve asset visibility and performance monitoring. \n \nEvaluate system impacts of approximately 30 MW of new large customer load. \n \nEvaluate and support implementation of an Asset Management System. \n \nComplete system upgrades between McCollough, Red Raider, and Yellowhouse substations to improve load transfer \ncapability and system resiliency. \n \nComplete full ADMS System Replacement to help modernize operations. \n \nContinue optimization of the SCADA/EMS systems to improve system performance, reliability, and usability. \n \nIntegrate SCADA/EMS with enterprise systems (OMS, GIS, PI, AMI, CCS, IVR) to enhance operational visibility and \ndata-driven decision-making. \n \nImplement Outage Management System and Outage Coordination Software to improve restoration times, crew \ncoordination, and customer communication. \n \nComplete implementation of ArcFM/Designer XI and the ESRI Utility Network for improved mapping and system \nmodeling. \n \nComplete replacement of remaining field routers to fully optimize the AMI system across the service area. \n \nSupport large kW customers in the installation and integration of distributed generation systems utilizing large-scale \nbattery storage. \nFY 2026-27 Proposed Operating Budget and Capital Program - 2\nTable of Contents\n\nLubbock Power & Light Overview \n \n \nAdvance Phase two of the PowerConnect integration connecting CIS and phone systems to enhance customer \nservice efficiency. \n \nDevelop proactive customer notification capabilities, including billing, leak alerts, and outage communications to \nimprove transparency and customer awareness. \n \nFunding Sources Overview \nOverall, budgeted revenues increased $12.9 million, or 7.4 percent, for FY 2026-27 and include the following changes: \n \nInterest earnings increased $1.2 million, or 74.8 percent, compared to the prior budget of $1.6 million, reflecting \nhigher expected yields in the pooled investment portfolio due to a sustained higher short-term interest rate \nenvironment consistent with broader economic expectations. \n \nDistribution System Revenue decreased $3.1 million, or 2.4 percent, due to lower-than-anticipated electric \nconsumption based on historical load. This revenue was derived from a cost-of-service study “COS”. The revenue \nrequirement for operational costs shifted significantly as the Utility transitioned to a delivery only provider in the \nretail market. These revenues will cover the operational costs of the Utility as a Transmission & Distribution Utility \n(TDU). \n \nFFE revenues decreased $0.1 million, or 0.6 percent, due to a slight decrease in projected load. The FFE revenue is \ncalculated as a “cents-per-kilowatt hour” (¢/kWh). \n \nTCOS revenues decreased $0.2 million, or 0.5 percent. The TCOS rate filing incorporates all the transmission assets \nrelated to the ERCOT integration, along with the appropriate FFEs, and is approved by the PUC. Final TCOS revenues \nare subject to PUC approval and could be higher or lower than the forecast. Of those TCOS revenues, $2.1 million is \nsplit into a line item in the expense section that properly accounts for the collection of FFE funds that will be passed \nthrough to the City. \n \nERCOT Hold Harmless Credit totals $7.3 million and is an offset to TCOS revenues. In PUC Docket No. 47576, the \norder approving LP&L’s initial integration into ERCOT, the PUC required LP&L to pay $22.0 million each year for the \nfirst five years, which will be credited to ERCOT wholesale transmission customers to mitigate integration costs. This \namount is shown as an offset to TCOS revenues in the model. Payments began in February 2022 and will continue \nmonthly until January 2027. The $14.7 million, or 66.7 percent, decrease to the credit reflects the FY 2026-27 budget \ncapturing only the remaining four months of payments under the original schedule. \n \nFees and Charges increased by $0.2 million, or 13.3 percent. This is due to the continued collection of Disconnect \nand Reconnect service fees. This increase is based on current and prior year collection trends and adjusted for \nexpected activity levels. \n \nOutside Work Orders and Street Lights increased by $1.8 million, or 121.1 percent, primarily due to the anticipated \npayment for requests for interconnect studies. This increase is offset by expenses in the engineering cost center for \nthe completion of the studies. \n \nMiscellaneous Revenues decreased $1.7 million, due to one-time subrogation revenue from insurance proceeds \nfrom the Brandon Substation damages. \n \nExpense Overview \nBudgeted expenses increased $19.2 million, or 10.6 percent, for FY 2026-27. These changes are detailed in the following \nsections: \n \nCompensation increased $0.6 million, or 2.6 percent, primarily due to a three percent cost of living adjustment \n(COLA) totaling $0.6 million. LP&L will align its COLA with the City’s non-civil service adjustment, provided the \nadjustment is not less than 2 percent. \n \nBenefits increased $0.2 million, or 2.3 percent, driven primarily by a $0.4 million increase in health benefit costs and \nthe impact of the three percent COLA, these increases were offset by a $0.2 million decrease in TMRS. \n \nSupplies increased by $0.1 million or 9.8 percent primarily due to a $0.1 million increase in safety supplies. \n \nTransmission increased by $2.9 million or 7.1 percent, primarily due to an increase in the postage stamp rate in line \nwith overall ERCOT 4CP system load growth. \n \nMaintenance costs increased by $0.5 million, or 13.9 percent, due to an increase of $0.3 million in maintenance of \noverhead lines in distribution cost center, a $0.1 increase in maintenance underground lines, and a $0.1 million \nincrease in vehicle maintenance. \nFY 2026-27 Proposed Operating Budget and Capital Program - 3\nTable of Contents\n\nLubbock Power & Light Overview \n \n \nProfessional Services and Training costs increased $3.2 million, or 28.3 percent, due to an increase of $1.3 million \nfor a tree trimming contract and an increase of $1.7 million for interconnection studies which will be offset by the \nrevenue received to perform the studies. \n \nOther charges decreased by $1.3 million, or 83.5 percent, due to tree trimming contract being moved under \nProfessional Services. \n \nScheduled charges decreased $0.1 million, or 1.2 percent, mainly due to a decrease of $0.1 million in property \ninsurance premiums. \n \nThe reimbursement from the Other City Utilities for the customer service function increased $1.1 million, or 15.3 \npercent. The increase is due to the increase in Collections allocation and Customer Service allocation to the City of \nLubbock Utilities. The calculation of the reimbursement is based on a negotiated methodology agreed upon by the \nCity and LP&L using audited numbers from FY 2024-25. \n \nDebt service decreased $0.8 million, or 2.1 percent, due to a reduction in principal and interest payments. \n \nFFE decreased $0.1 million, or 0.6 percent, due to a slight decrease in projected load. The FFE revenue calculation is \nbased on a “cents-per-kilowatt hour” (¢/kWh) included on the delivery rate. \n \nFFE TCOS – General Fund totals $2.1 million to properly account for the collection of FFE funds that will be passed \nthrough to the City recovered in the TCOS rate. \n \nPayment in Lieu of Taxes (PILOT) decreased slightly by 0.8 percent, the PILOT transfer by ord"}, {"title": "2026-07-07 - Appointments Advisory Board - 11:30 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07072026-112", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Brett Underwood, Chair Bruce Boyd, Vice Chair Cathy Allen, District 6 Rep. Colton Davis, District 3 Rep. Monica Delgado, District 2 Rep. Rafael Gutierrez, District 1 Rep. Ryan Henry, District 6 Rep. Heather Keister, Mayor Rep. Margaret Kelley, District 5 Rep. Appointments Advisory Board Agenda July 7, 2026 11:30 A.M. Citizens Tower 2nd Floor Conference Rooms 201A/B 1314 Ave. K", "text": "Brett Underwood, Chair \nBruce Boyd, Vice Chair \nCathy Allen, District 6 Rep. \nColton Davis, District 3 Rep. \nMonica Delgado, District 2 Rep. \nRafael Gutierrez, District 1 Rep. \nRyan Henry, District 6 Rep. \nHeather Keister, Mayor Rep. \nMargaret Kelley, District 5 Rep. \n\nAppointments Advisory Board \nAgenda \n July 7, 2026 \n 11:30 A.M. \n \nCitizens Tower \n2nd Floor Conference Rooms 201A/B \n1314 Ave. K , Lubbock, Texas \n\n Margarita Olivarez, District 1 Rep. \n Andrea Parker, District 2 Rep. \n Keith Patrick, Mayor Rep. \n Tiffany Robinson, District 3 Rep. \n Amy Sikes, District 4 Rep. \n Tyler Young, District 5 Rep. \n Courtney Paz, City Secretary \n Jimmy Maynard, Deputy City Secretary \n\nAppointments Advisory Board meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please \ncontact the Office of the City Secretary at 806.775.2026, e-mail ocs@mylubbock.us, or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas \n79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \nPursuant to the City Council’s policy on Boards, Commissions, and Committees, one or more members of the Appointments Advisory \nBoard may participate in the meeting by video-conference call as permitted under Section XVIII of the policy. \n \n1. \nCall to order \n \n2. \nReflections \n \n3. Minutes \n \n3.1 \nJune 2, 2026 Regular Appointments Advisory Board Meeting \n \n4. \nRegular Session \n \n4.1 3rd Quarter Boards \n \n4.1.1 \nBuilding Board of Appeals – Consider recommendations for appointments \n \n4.1.2 \nZoning Board of Adjustment – Consider recommendations for appointments \n \n4.1.3 \nDiscussion and Action if necessary on the Following Boards from the April 7, 2026, May \n5, 2026, and June 2, 2026 Meetings – Appointments Advisory Board, Audit & Investment \nCommittee, Canadian River Municipal Water Authority Board of Directors, Citizens Traffic \nCommission, Civic Lubbock, Inc. Board of Directors, Lubbock Water Advisory Commission, \nand the Permit & License Appeal Board \n \n4.2 4th Quarter Boards \n \n4.2.1 \nAirport Board – Consider recommendations for appointments \n \n4.2.2 \nHealth/Educational Facilities Development Corporation Board – Consider \nrecommendations for appointments \n \n4.2.3 \nLubbock Emergency Communications District Board of Managers – Consider \nrecommendations for appointments \n \n4.2.4 \nModel Codes & Construction Advisory Board – Consider recommendations for \nappointments \n \n4.2.5 \nMuseum & Art Standing Sub-Committee – Consider recommendations for appointments \n \n4.2.6 \nPlanning & Zoning Commission – Consider recommendations for appointments \n\n5. \nWork Session \n \n 5.1 \nDiscuss and consider recruiting and participation methods for boards and commissions service \n \n 5.2 \nDiscussion – A discussion pertaining to boards and commissions policies and procedures, \nrecommendation process for prospective board members, board applicants, housekeeping items \n(including board packets), directives to the staff liaison and future meeting agenda items."}, {"title": "2026-07-06 - Building Board of Appeals Agenda for July 6, 2026, 1:30PM Meeting (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07062026-111", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Building Board of Appeals Regular Meeting Agenda July 6, 2026 1:30 p.m. Conference Rooms 201 A/B Citizens Tower | 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas Building Board of Appeals meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the Office of the City Secretary at 806.775.3191, e-mail mvillagrana@mylubbock.us, or write to Post Office", "text": "Building Board of Appeals \n Regular Meeting Agenda \nJuly 6, 2026 1:30 p.m. \nConference Rooms 201 A/B \n Citizens Tower | 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n\nBuilding Board of Appeals meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special \nassistance, please contact the Office of the City Secretary at 806.775.3191, e-mail mvillagrana@mylubbock.us, \nor write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \nThe Building Board of Appeals requires citizens to sign up prior to the meeting with the attached \nform. Citizens are limited to 3 minutes and may only speak to items posted on the agenda. \n \nCitizen Comments - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Building Board \nof Appeals, regarding any matter posted on the Building Board of Appeals Agenda, shall complete \nthe sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than 1:30 P.M. \n \n“Pursuant to the City Council Policy and Procedures for Boards, Commissions and Committees, \nthe Building Board of Appeals Board will convene in-person.” \n\nI. Call to Order \n \nWelcome and Introductions \n \nII. Citizen Comments \n \nIII. Approval of Minutes \nJanuary 5, 2026– Regular Board Meeting \nIV. Action Items \nThe Board will hear an update on Model Codes and Construction Advisory Board is \nworking on. \n \nV. Schedule Next Regular Board Meeting the suggested date is October 5, 2026, at 1:30 p.m. \n\nTrey Strong-Chairman \nRussell Thoma-Vice Chairman \nSandra Bagwell \nDonovan Beckwith \nShannon Powell \nDickie Hudgens \nRicky Lawrence \nRicardo Martinez \nC.J. Marquette \nJeremy Steen \nBen Jordan \nNeal Turner \nChristina Wier"}, {"title": "2026-07-05 - Upland Crossing Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting - 5:30 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07052026-131", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Upland Crossing Public Improvement District Property Owners Meeting Agenda July 29, 2026 5:30 P.M. City Council Chambers Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas 1.0 Welcome Austin Lear, Chair 2.0 Introduction of the Upland Crossing PID Advisory Board Members Austin Lear, Chair 3.0 Update from the Board of FY 2025-26 Board Activities and Projects Austin Lear 4.0 Discussion of Proposed Fisca", "text": "Upland Crossing Public Improvement District\nProperty Owners Meeting\nAgenda\nJuly 29, 2026\n5:30 P.M.\nCity Council Chambers\n Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas\n1.0\nWelcome\nAustin Lear, Chair\n2.0\nIntroduction of the Upland Crossing PID Advisory Board Members\nAustin Lear, Chair\n3.0 Update from the Board of FY 2025-26 Board Activities and Projects\n Austin Lear\n4.0 Discussion of Proposed Fiscal Year 2026-27 Service and Assessment Plan \nMolly Ballesteros, Special Districts Manager\n5.0\nPublic Comment\nOpen forum for the property owners in the Upland Crossing PID to voice concerns,\nsuggestions, and preferences for Upland Crossing PID and the Upland Crossing PID\nService and Assessment Plan.\n6.0 Voting on Two Positions for the Upland Crossing PID Advisory Board\nUpland Crossing Public Improvement District public meetings are available to all persons regardless of\ndisability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806.775.3083 or write to\nP.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting.\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted at the South side of Citizens Tower at 1314\nAvenue K, West of the revolving doors, Lubbock, Texas, on the ______ day of ______, 2026 at\n_______AM/PM By: Molly Ballesteros"}, {"title": "2026-07-02 - Planning and Zoning Commission Meeting - Citizen's Tower @ 6 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07022026-109", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Planning and Zoning Commission Agenda July 2, 2026 Regular Meeting 6:00 P.M. Citizens Tower Council Chambers 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas Commission members Chair James Bell Vice-Chair Tarek Redwan, Renee Cage Kirk Franklin Scott Gloyna Drew Gray Terri Morris Stacey Rogers Robert Wood The Planning and Zoning Commission meetings are available to all persons regardless of disabil", "text": "Planning and Zoning Commission Agenda \nJuly 2, 2026 \nRegular Meeting 6:00 P.M. \nCitizens Tower Council Chambers \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n \nCommission members \nChair James Bell \nVice-Chair Tarek Redwan, \nRenee Cage \nKirk Franklin \nScott Gloyna \nDrew Gray \nTerri Morris \nStacey Rogers \nRobert Wood \n \nThe Planning and Zoning Commission meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you \nrequire \nspecial \nassistance, \nplease \ncontact \nthe \nCity \nof \nLubbock \nat \n806-775-2108, \nemail \ncityplanning@mylubbock.us, or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours in advance \nof the meeting. \n \nThe Planning and Zoning Commission of the City of Lubbock, Texas reserves the right to meet in closed \nsession on any of the items listed below should the need arise and if applicable pursuant to authorization \nby Section 551.071 of the Texas Government Code. \n \nRegular Agenda \n \n1. Call to Order \n \n1.1 Citizen Comments – According to Lubbock Planning and Zoning Commission Rules, \nany citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Planning and Zoning \nCommission, regarding any matter posted on the Planning and Zoning Commission \nAgenda below, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than \n6:00 p.m. \n \n2. Approval of Minutes \n \n2.1 June 4, 2026, Regular Planning and Zoning Commission Meeting minutes. \n \n3. Zone Changes - Public Hearings \nPlanning & Zoning Commission makes recommendation; City Council has final authority for \napproval. \n\nDistrict 1 \n3.1 Zone Case 3145-H: Brad Andrews Realty, LLC, for Borde Management Corp, request \nfor a zone change from Broadway District (MU-2) to Neighborhood Commercial \nDistrict (NC), at: \n• 1701 Broadway, 1705 Broadway, and 1216 Avenue Q, located south of \nBroadway and west of Avenue Q, Overton Addition, Block 11, Lots 1 through 5. \n \nDistrict 1 \n3.2 Zone Case 3555: Hugo Reed and Associates, Inc. for Lubbock Economic Development \nAlliance, request for a zone change from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2) to \nGeneral Industrial District (GI), at: \n• Generally located north of Keuka Street and west of I-27, on 52.4 acres of \nunplatted land out of Block A, Section 35 . \nDistrict 5 \n3.3 Zone Case 3417-B: Centerline for Amicus Trust Company, LLC, FBO Holly \nBetenbough Self-Directed IRA, request for a zone change from Office District (OF) to \nLight Industrial District (LI), at: \n• 6605, 6607, 6609, 6611, 6803, and 6805 Alcove Avenue and 7804 68th Street, \nlocated east of Alcove Avenue and north of 69th Street, West Business Park \nAddition, Tracts B, C, D, E, F, Z, and AA. \nDistrict 5 \n3.4 Zone Case 3556: Hugo Reed and Associates, Inc. for Duhan Family Limited \nPartnership, request for a zone change from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2) \nto Neighborhood Commercial District (NC) and Auto-Urban Commercial District (AC), \nat: \n• \n7801 34th Street, and 7301, 7402, 7403, 7502, and 7528 50th Street, located \nwest of Upland Avenue and north and south of 50th Street, on 75.8 acres of \nunplatted land out of Block AK, Sections 34 and 39. \nDistrict 6 \n3.5 Zone Case 2538-SS: Mountain Top Engineering for JKC Ranch LLC, request for a zone \nchange from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2) and Heavy Commercial District \n(HC) to Heavy Commercial District (HC), at: \n• 8805 and 8825 34th Street, located south of 34th Street and west of Inler Avenue, \non 25.44 acres of unplatted land out of Block D-6, Section 7. \n\n4. UDC Amendments - Public Hearings \n Planning & Zoning Commission makes recommendation; City Council has final authority for \napproval. \n \n4.1 Amend Sections 39.02.016 and 39.2.004.a.3 to allow Manufactured Homes as a \npermitted use in accordance with the requirements of Texas Senate Bill 785, effective \nSeptember 1, 2026. \n \n5. Adjourn \n \nThe above-referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted on the south entrance of Citizens Tower, \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the_________ day of June at _________AM./P.M. \n\nBy: _____________________________________________"}, {"title": "2026-07-01 - Public Transit Advisory Board - 11:30 am (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_07012026-110", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "NOTICE OF LUBBOCK PUBLIC TRANSIT ADVISORY BOARD MEETING JULY 1, 2026 – 11:30 A.M. CITIBUS CONFERENCE ROOM, 801 TEXAS LUBBOCK, TEXAS 1. Call to Order 2. Citizen Comments - According to HB 2840, any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Public Transit Advisory Board, regarding any matter posted on the Public Transit Advisory Board Agenda, shall sign-up no later than 11", "text": "NOTICE OF LUBBOCK PUBLIC TRANSIT ADVISORY BOARD MEETING \nJULY 1, 2026 – 11:30 A.M. \nCITIBUS CONFERENCE ROOM, 801 TEXAS \nLUBBOCK, TEXAS \n \n1. Call to Order \n \n2. Citizen Comments - According to HB 2840, any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting \nof the Public Transit Advisory Board, regarding any matter posted on the Public Transit Advisory \nBoard Agenda, shall sign-up no later than 11:15 a.m. on the day of the meeting by the following \nmethods: \n2.1.1. E-mail sgarrett@citibus.com; \n2.1.2. Call Citibus at 806-775-3640; or \n2.1.3. In person at 801 Texas Avenue \n \n3. Minutes \n\n3.1. Consider approval of the minutes of the May 28, 2026, regular meeting of the Lubbock Public \nTransit Advisory Board (TAB) \n \n4. Regular Session/Action Items \n \n4.1. Acceptance of Citibus Financial and Operating Reports \n \n4.2. Consider Recommendation of Item to be Presented to City Council for Approval \n4.2.1 Approval of Texas Tech University Transportation Services Contract \n \n5. Work Session \n \n5.1. Update on Route and Network Redesign. \n \n5.2. Update on Citibus Marketing \n \n5.3. Update on Citibus Operations \n \n5.4. Update on Citibus Maintenance Operations \n \n5.5. General Manager Update \n \n6. Adjourn \n \nNOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the South entrance \nof Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the 25th \nday of June 2026 at ______P.M. \n \nBy: ______________________________________ \n Shannon Garrett, Board Support Staff Member \n\nThe Lubbock Public Transit Advisory Board meetings are available to all persons regardless of \ndisability. If you require special assistance, please contact Shannon Garrett at (806) 775-3434, or \nwrite to her at P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-06-24 - Model Codes and Construction Advisory Board-3:30pm (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_06242026-104", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Current Board Members Gary Ferguson, Chair James Jordan, Vice Chair Kendall Wood, Master Electrician Ryan Farquhar, Commercial Contractor David Small, Engineer Logan West, Facilities Manager Craig Ray, WTHBA Brian Robertson, Engineer Wendell Burris, Lubbock Apartment Marvin White, Licensed HVAC George Henson, L