-January 2026 \nd. \n2021/2024 International Fuel Gas Code – Completed-March 2026 \ne. \n2021/2024 International Swimming Pool and Spa Code – June 2026 \nf. \n2021/2024 International Plumbing Code- June 2026 \ng. \n2021/2024 International Mechanical Code – June 2026 \nh. \n2021/2024 International Residential Code- July 2026 \n\ni. \n2020/2023/2026 National Electric Code – August 2026 \nj. \n2021/2024 International Energy Conservation Code –August 2026 \n \nNext meeting is scheduled for July 22, 2026."}, {"title": "2026-06-24 - Airport Advisory Board Agenda - 12:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_06242026-101", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Airport Advisory Board Agenda June 24, 2026 12:00 P.M. – Airport Blue Room 5401 North Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd. Lubbock, TX Any citizen wishing to address the Airport Advisory Board, regarding any matter posted on this Agenda, may submit a public comment by email to tshelley@mylubbock.us, no later than 4:00 p.m. on June 23, 2026. Submitted comments must include name and address. Each co", "text": "Airport Advisory Board Agenda \nJune 24, 2026 \n12:00 P.M. – Airport Blue Room \n5401 North Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd. Lubbock, TX \n \nAny citizen wishing to address the Airport Advisory Board, regarding any matter posted on this Agenda, \nmay submit a public comment by email to tshelley@mylubbock.us, no later than 4:00 p.m. on June 23, \n2026. Submitted comments must include name and address. Each comment will be read aloud for a period \nnot to exceed three minutes pursuant to Airport Advisory Board Rules. Public Comment is an opportunity \nfor citizens to make comments and express a position on agenda items. Any comment received that is in \nthe nature of asking a question will be referred to an appropriate member of the City staff for a response. \n \n1) Call to Order \nWelcome and Introductions \n \n2) Approval of Minutes \nApril 29, 2026 \n \n3) Citizen Comments \n \n4) Action Items \n \n4.1 \nConsider a recommendation to City Council authorizing the Mayor to execute contract \n19432 with Fuse Connect, LLC dba Fuse Advancement for the Terminal Advertising \nConcession at Lubbock Preston Smith International Airport. \n\n5) Work Session \n \n5.1 \nAdministration Report – Melissa Trevino \n5.2 \nOperations Report – Chuck Farina \n5.3 \nExecutive Director’s Report – Kelly Campbell \n\n6) Adjourn \nNext Airport Advisory Board Meeting will be held July 29, 2026."}, {"title": "2026-06-23 - Regular City Council Meeting, June 23, 2026 (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_06232026-102", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Regular City Council Meeting June 23, 2026 Mark W. McBrayer, Mayor Dr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5 Christy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1 Gordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 David Bruegel, Councilman, District 3 Brayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 Tim Collins, Councilman, District 6 W. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager Matt Wade, City Attorney C", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nRegular City Council Meeting\nJune 23, 2026\n \nMark W. McBrayer, Mayor\nDr. Jennifer Wilson, Mayor Pro Tem, District 5\nChristy Martinez-Garcia, Councilwoman, District 1\nGordon Harris, Councilman, District 2\nDavid Bruegel, Councilman, District 3\nBrayden Rose, Councilman, District 4\nTim Collins, Councilman, District 6\n \nW. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager\nMatt Wade, City Attorney\nCourtney Paz, City Secretary\nhttp://www.mylubbock.us\nCity Council Chambers, Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas\n \nCity of Lubbock City Council Meetings are available to all persons, regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact\nthe City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the\nmeeting. \nExecutive Session Disclosure Statement: The City Council reserves the right to adjourn into executive session at any time during the course of\nthe meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized by Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code, including but not\nnecessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with Attorney), §551.072 (Deliberations regarding Real Property), §551.073 (Deliberations\nregarding Gifts and Donations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076 (Deliberations regarding Security Devices), §551.086 (Public\nPower Utilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations regarding Economic Development Negotiations). \nA quorum of the City Council will be physically present in City Council Chambers located in Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas,\nas it is the intent of the City Council to have a quorum physically present at this location. One or more members of the City Council, however,\nmay participate in the meeting by video-conference call as permitted under Section 551.127 of the Texas Government Code. \nNote: On occasion, the City Council may consider agenda items out of order. \n. \n \n12:00 p.m. - City Council convenes in City Council Chambers and immediately recesses into Executive\nSession.\n\n1.\nExecutive Session\n \n1. 1.\nHold an executive session in accordance with Texas Government Code, Section 551.071, with legal\ncounsel for advice about pending or contemplated litigation or settlement agreement and on matters in\nwhich the duty of the attorney under the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct conflicts\nwith Chapter 551. \n \n1. 1. 1.\nMeadowbrook Estates\n \n1. 1. 2.\nLubbock Riverwalk, LLC/Lake 7\n \n1. 1. 3.\nChapter 22 - City Code of Ordinances\n \n1. 1. 4.\nLubbock Power & Light - Ty Cooke Gas Generating Facility\n\n1. 2.\nHold an executive session in accordance with the Texas Government Code, Section 551.072, to\ndeliberate the purchase, exchange, lease, or value of real property. \n \n1. 2. 1.\nChapter 22 - City Code of Ordinances\n \n1. 2. 2.\nLubbock Power & Light - Ty Cooke Gas Generating Facility\n \n1. 3.\nHold an executive session in accordance with Texas Government Code Section 551.074, to discuss\npersonnel matters, regarding the duties, responsibilities, and/or appointments to the Building Board of\nAppeals, Canadian River Municipal Water Authority Board of Directors, Permit & License Appeal\nBoard, and the Zoning Board of Adjustment. \n \n1. 4.\nHold an executive session in accordance with Tex. Gov’t Code § 551.086 to discuss and deliberate, on\nthe following competitive matters of Lubbock Power & Light, related to generation matters, including\nbut not limited to generation unit and/or portfolio sale opportunities (Ty Cooke Gas Generating\nFacility). \n \nAdjourn from Executive Session\n \n2:00 p.m. - City Council reconvenes in Open Session in City Council Chambers.\n \n2.\nCeremonial Items\n \n2. 1.\nInvocation \n \n2. 2.\nPledges of Allegiance \n \nCall to Order\n \n3.\nCitizen Comments - According to Lubbock City Council Rules, any citizen wishing to appear\nin-person before a regular meeting of the City Council, regarding any matter posted on the City\nCouncil Agenda below, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than\n2:00 p.m. on June 23, 2026. Citizen Comments provide an opportunity for citizens to make\ncomments and express a position on agenda items.\n \n4.\nMinutes\n \n4. 1.\nMay 26, 2026 Regular City Council Meeting \n \n5.\nConsent Agenda - Items considered to be routine are enacted by one motion without separate\ndiscussion. If the City Council desires to discuss an item, the item is removed from the Consent\nAgenda and considered separately.\n\n5. 1.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance No.\n2026-O0071, Amendment 30, respecting the General Fund and Grant Fund in connection with\naccepting grant funding from the Texas Department of State Health Services, for the Texas Spay and\nNeuter Program Grant Fund. \n \n5. 2.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0072, and related\ndocuments, abandoning and closing a portion of right-of-way adjacent to Utica Avenue at 142nd Street,\nlocated in Section 5, Block AK, Lubbock County. \n \n5. 3.\nOrdinance 1st Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider an ordinance, and related documents, abandoning\nand closing a portion of an alley located in Section 17, Block B, Lubbock County, Texas, on the north\nside of the Marsha Sharp Freeway at 29th Street. \n \n5. 4.\nOrdinance 1st Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider an ordinance, and related documents, abandoning\nand closing a 0.063-acre portion of the Legacy Range Business Park, Tract \"D\", an addition to the City\nof Lubbock, Lubbock County Texas, on the south side of 116th Street, east of Indiana Avenue. \n \n5. 5.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Memorandum\nof Agreement with DMILES, LLC, a California Limited Liability Company, for the purchase of a\n0.2347-acre parcel located in Section 42, Block AK, Lubbock County, Texas (Parcel 77), located on\nthe west side of Upland Avenue, south of 21st Street, and north of 22nd Street, to be utilized for the\n2022 Street Bond Project. \n \n5. 6.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Memorandum\nof Agreement, with United Sky Capital, L.L.C., a Texas limited liability company, for the purchase of\na 0.026-acre tract of land located in Section 22, Block AK, Lubbock County, Texas (Parcel 34), located\non the east side of Upland Avenue, south of 98th Street, and north of 100th Street, to be utilized for the\n2024 Street Bond Project. \n \n5. 7.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Memorandum\nof Agreement with Garland Kimbrell and Donna Capron, for the purchase of a 0.1784-acre tract and a\n0.1775 acre tract of land located in Section 21, Block E-2, Lubbock County, Texas (Parcels 19 and 20),\nlocated on the south side of 114th Street, west of Akron Avenue, and east of Detroit Avenue, to be\nutilized for the 2022 Street Bond Project. \n \n5. 8.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Street Use\nLicense Agreement, and related documents, by and between the City of Lubbock and Texland\nPetroleum, L.P., concerning property in Sections 5 and 6, Block A, Lubbock County Texas, along\nGuava Avenue, and north of Northeast Loop 289, for purposes related to underground pipeline. \n \n5. 9.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Street Use\nLicense Agreement, and related documents, by and between the City of Lubbock and Texland\nPetroleum, L.P., to maintain a crude oil flow pipeline near the Intersection of North Avenue Q and\nKent Street in Section 11, Block A and Section 1, Block D-4. \n\n5. 10.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Amendment No. 4\nto Professional Services Agreement, Contract 16854, by and between the City of Lubbock and Freese\nand Nichols, Inc., for additional construction phase services related to the West Lubbock Water System\nExpansion Project. \n \n5. 11.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Corrected\nAmendment No. 1 to Professional Services Agreement, Contract 18963, and related documents, by and\nbetween the City of Lubbock and Enprotec/Hibbs & Todd, Inc., to clarify the contract duration\nlanguage and extend the term by the intended length of one year, for service of Portable Generators and\nATS Design for Emergencies. \n \n5. 12.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Professional\nServices Agreement, Contract 19710, and related documents, with Hugo Reed and Associates, Inc., for\nthe 82nd Street and Martin Luther King, Jr. Boulevard Drainage Improvements Project, to include\nWater Design, Utility Coordination, and Permitting, to be utilized for the 2022 Street Bond Project. \n \n5. 13.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Public Works\nContract 19424, and related documents, with Rink's Lease Service, Inc., for Phase 7 Waterline Change\nOut, to replace aging water infrastructure, in order to improve function and prevent leaks, system\nfailures, and emergency maintenance. \n \n5. 14.\nResolution - Public Works - Paved Streets: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute\nContract 19601 with Roadway Asset Services, LLC, for collecting pavement performance data used to\nrate the condition of City streets. \n \n5. 15.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Public Works, Traffic Management: Consider Ordinance No.\n2026-O0073, amending Section 20.05.103 of the City of Lubbock Code of Ordinances, with regard to\nthe establishment of school zones and speed limits, on Norfolk Avenue between 130th Street and 136th\nStreet, and on 135th Street between Quaker Avenue and Memphis Avenue. \n \n5. 16.\nResolution - Public Transit Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute\nContract 19418 and related documents, with Brown Investment Group, LLC dba Western Collision,\nfor Body Work and Accident Damage Repair for Citibus Vehicles. \n \n5. 17.\nResolution - Lubbock Power & Light: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a\nPurchase and Sale Agreement, with Adakon Energy, for the Ty Cooke Gas Generating Facility. \n \n5. 18.\nResolution - City Manager: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Amendment No. 3\nto Contract 16514, with MBE 3, LLC, for revisions to the Meadowbrook Estates payout schedule. \n \n6.\nRegular Agenda\n \n6. 1.\nOrdinance Single Reading - Finance: Consider an ordinance of the City of Lubbock, Texas,\nauthorizing the issuance and sale of City of Lubbock, Texas, Tax Notes, in one or more series; levying\na tax in payment thereof; and enacting other provisions relating to the subject. The Tax Notes will be\nutilized to fund Radio System equipment and high dollar value vehicles/equipment approved in the FY\n2025-26 Budget. \n\n6. 2.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 4): Consider a request for Zone Case 3554, a request of The\nGarrett Group at Keller Williams, for Verlene Avant, for a zone change from Low Density\nSingle-Family District (SF-2) to Office District (OF), at 12202 Upton Avenue, located south of 122nd\nStreet and west of Upton Avenue, on 5.1 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 23, and\nconsider an ordinance. \n \n6. 3.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 5): Consider a request for Zone Case 3521-A, a request of\nSeventeen Services, LLC, for Milwaukee 104, LLC, for a zone change from Neighborhood\nCommercial District (NC) to Auto-Urban Commercial District (AC), at 6602 104th Street, located west\nof Milwaukee Avenue and north of 104th Street, on 15.03 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK,\nSection 22, and consider an ordinance. \n \n6. 4.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 5): Consider a request for Zone Case 3544, a request for zone\nchanges in City Council District 5, from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2), Heavy\nCommercial District (HC), Industrial Park District (IP), Light Industrial District (LI), and General\nIndustrial District (GI) to Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2), Auto-Urban Commercial District\n(AC), Heavy Commercial District (HC), Industrial Park District (IP), at 5701 130th Street, Tract A,\nHuffines Business Park Addition, 5701 130th Street Rear, 2.2 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK,\nSection 8, 5514, 5517, 5608, and 5619 132nd Street, Tracts H, K, P, and Q, Huffines Business Park\nAddition, 13306 and 13312 Chicago Avenue, Tracts U and V, Huffines Business Park Addition, 5215\nand 5217 98th Street, 1.21 acres out of Tracts 6-A and 7-A, Southwest Lubbock Health Center\nAddition, 6601-6622 Xenia Avenue, 7501-7513 68th Street, and 6601-6622 Winfield Avenue