r Commission Members Chair Matthew Hadley Vice-Chair D’Juana McPherson Shannon Spencer Tracy Thomason Jose Valenciano Alternate Commission Members Michael J. Gomez Mike Kerr Maricruz Martinez Ian “Cole” Watts Pursuant to the Executive Order", "text": "Zoning Board of Adjustment Agenda \nMay 21, 2026 \nRegular Meeting 8:30 A.M. \nCitizens Tower Council Chambers \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n \nRegular Commission Members \nChair Matthew Hadley \nVice-Chair D’Juana McPherson \nShannon Spencer \nTracy Thomason \nJose Valenciano \nAlternate Commission Members \nMichael J. Gomez \nMike Kerr \nMaricruz Martinez \nIan “Cole” Watts\n \nPursuant to the Executive Orders of Governor Abbott, and other authority provided by the \nTexas Attorney General, the Zoning Board of Adjustment will convene in-person only. \nThe Zoning Board of Adjustment meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If \nyou require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806-775-2108, email \ncityplanning@mylubbock.us, or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours \nin advance of the meeting. \nThe Zoning Board of Adjustment of the City of Lubbock, Texas reserves the right to meet in closed \nsession on any of the items listed below should the need arise and if applicable pursuant to \nauthorization by Section 551.071 of the Texas Government Code. \n \nRegular Meeting \n \n1. Call to Order-Welcome and Introductions \n \n2. Citizen Comments –According to HB 2840, any citizen wishing to appear before a regular \nmeeting of the Zoning Board of Adjustment, regarding any matter posted on the Zoning Board \nof Adjustment Agenda, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than \n8:30 a.m. \n \n3. Approval of Minutes \n \n3.1 April 16, 2026 Regular Meeting. \n\n4. Variances - Public Hearings \n[Zoning Board of Adjustment has final authority for approval.] \n\n4.1 Case V-1577-J: Kimley-Horn and Associates, Inc. for The Macerich South Plains LP \n\nRequest for a Variance to vary the side setback requirement for a commercial structure \n\nfrom 5 feet to 0 feet, for a total variance of 5 feet, on property zoned Auto-Urban \n \n Commercial District (AC), per Section 39.02.006.c.3, at: \n \n• 6002 Slide Road, located south of 57th Street and west of Slide Road, South Plains \nAddition, Tract A-1 less the northeast corner (less building only) (proposed South \nPlains Addition, Tracts A-1-A and A-1-B). \n \n4.2 Case V-5180: Texas Custom Signs for Mister Car Wash \n \nRequest for a Variance to vary the side setback requirement for a monument sign from 42 \nfeet to 30 feet for a total variance of 12 feet, on property zoned Auto-Urban Commercial \nDistrict (AC), per Section 39.03.023.c.7.B, at: \n• 7027 Quaker Avenue, located east of Quaker Avenue and north of 72nd Street, West \nChase Addition, Lot 2-A. \n \n 4.3 Case V-5182: Jerry and Ann Casstevens \n \nRequest for a Variance to vary the side setback requirement for an accessory structure \nfrom 10 feet to 0 feet, for a total variance of 10 feet, on property zoned Low Density \nSingle-Family District (SF-2), per Section 39.02.004.c.4.A, at: \n \n• 8511 Miami Avenue, located east of Miami Avenue and north of 87th Street, King’s \nGate Addition, Lot 435. \n \n 4.4 Case V-5183: Joey Contreras for Jacinta Alvarez \n \nRequest for a Variance to vary the side and rear setback requirement for a detached \naccessory structure from 5 feet to 0 feet for a total variance of 5 feet, on property zoned \nLow Density Single-Family District (SF-2), per Section 39.02.020.c.B.ii, at: \n \n• 7221 91st Street, located east of Upland Avenue and south of 91st Street, Quincy Park \nAddition, Lot 582. \n \n 4.5 Case V-5184: Nelson D. and Cheryl Hogg \n\nRequest for a Variance to vary the side setback requirement for an accessory structure from \n10 feet to 0 feet, for a total variance of 10 feet, on property zoned Low Density Single-\nFamily District (SF-2), per Section 39.02.004.c.4.A, at: \n \n• 3921 86th Street, located west of Miami Avenue and south of 86th Street, King’s Gate \nAddition, Lot 462. \n \n4.6 Case V-5185: Eric Luna for Enrique and Amy Aguilar \n \nRequest for a Variance to vary the setbacks for a detached accessory building from 5 \nfeet to 2 feet, for a total variance of 3 feet, on property zoned General Industrial District \n(GI), per Section 39.02.020.b.3.F.i, at: \n\n• 2102 Clovis Road, located northeast of Clovis Road and west of North Avenue U, \nRoberts and McWhorter Addition, Block 71, Tract C \n \n5. Adjourn \n \nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted on the bulletin board outside the south \nentrance of Citizens Tower 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the day of 2026 at 9:00 A.M. \n \nBy: Rosie Gray \n Planning Department ~ Management Assistant"}, {"title": "2026-05-20 - Special City Council Meeting - Swearing-In Ceremony, May 20, 2026 (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05202026-63", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Special City Council Meeting May 20, 2026 Mark McBrayer, Mayor Christy Martinez-Garcia, Mayor Pro Tem, District 1 Gordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 David Bruegel, Councilman, District 3 Brayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 Dr. Jennifer Wilson, Councilwoman, District 5 Tim Collins, Councilman, District 6 W. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager Matt Wade, City Attorney Courtn", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nSpecial City Council Meeting\n May 20, 2026\nMark McBrayer, Mayor\nChristy Martinez-Garcia, Mayor Pro Tem, District 1\nGordon Harris, Councilman, District 2\nDavid Bruegel, Councilman, District 3\nBrayden Rose, Councilman, District 4\nDr. Jennifer Wilson, Councilwoman, District 5\nTim Collins, Councilman, District 6\n \nW. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager\nMatt Wade, City Attorney\nCourtney Paz, City Secretary\nhttp://www.mylubbock.us\nCity Council Chambers, Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas\n \nCity of Lubbock City Council Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance,\nplease contact the City Secretary's Office at 775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours\nin advance of the meeting. \nThis is a ceremonial event where a quorum of the City Council may be present, but no action will be taken by the governing\nbody as a whole.\n5:30 p.m. - City Council Convenes in City Council Chambers in Open Session.\n\n1.\nSwearing-In Ceremony\n \n1. 1.\nAdminister the Oath of Office to newly re-elected Mayor Mark McBrayer, Council\nMembers Christy Martinez-Garcia, and Dr. Jennifer Wilson, and newly elected Council\nMember David Bruegel.\n(Reception immediately following.)"}, {"title": "2026-05-20 - Permit and License Appeal Board - 11:30 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05202026-65", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "1. Call to order 2. Public Comment - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Permit & License Appeal Board regarding any matter posted on the Permit & License Appeal Board Agenda may speak. 3. Minutes 3.1 April 15, 2026 Permit & License Appeal Board Meeting 4. Regular Session 4.1 Junked Vehicle Determinations - May be enacted by one motion without sepa", "text": "1. \nCall to order \n \n2. \nPublic Comment - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Permit \n& License Appeal Board regarding any matter posted on the Permit & License Appeal \nBoard Agenda may speak. \n \n3. \nMinutes \n \n3.1 \nApril 15, 2026 Permit & License Appeal Board Meeting \n \n4. \nRegular Session \n \n4.1 \nJunked Vehicle Determinations - May be enacted by one motion without \nseparate discussion. If the Permit & License Appeal Board desires to discuss an \nitem, the item is removed and considered separately. \n \n4.1.1 CASE NO: JV-2025-010850 of Inspector Jon McDonald regarding one (1) \nvehicle at 5413 28th St. \n \n4.1.2 CASE NO: JV-2026-011079 of Inspector Courtney Brackeen regarding \none (1) vehicle at 8012 Englewood Ave. \n \n4.1.3 CASE NO: JV-2026-011187 of Inspector Randy Merrick regarding one \n(1) vehicle at 1807 E 25th St. \n \nGarrott Nalle, Chair \nJillian Nava, Vice Chair \nCecile Garcia \nBrooks Jones \nThomas Nichols \nDavid Saunders \nDonald Stubbs \n \nPermit & License Appeal Board \nAgenda \nMay 20, 2026 \n11:30 A.M. \nCourtney Paz, City Secretary \nJimmy Maynard, Deputy City \nSecretary \nRyan Brooke, Assistant City \nAttorney \n \nCitizens Tower \nCity Council Chambers, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n\nPermit & License Appeal Board meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special \nassistance, please contact the Office of the City Secretary at 806.775.2061, e-mail ocs@mylubbock.us, or write to \nPost Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \nExecutive Session Disclosure Statement: The Permit & License Appeal Board reserves the right to recess into \nexecutive session at any time during the course of the meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized \nby Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code, including but not necessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with \nAttorney), §551.072 (Deliberations regarding Real Property), §551.073 (Deliberations regarding Gifts and \nDonations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076 (Deliberations regarding Security Devices), §551.086 (Public \nPower Utilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations regarding Economic Development Negotiations). \n\n4.1.4 CASE NO: JV-2026-011354 of Inspector Lisa Lavender regarding one (1) \nvehicle at 1715 46th St. \n \n4.1.5 CASE NO: JV-2026-011364 of Inspector Randy Merrick regarding one \n(1) vehicle at 3418 E Broadway \n \n4.1.6 CASE NO: JV-2026-011383 of Inspector Randy Merrick regarding one \n(1) vehicle at 2405 Ave V \n \n4.1.7 CASE NO: JV-2026-011396 of Inspector Lisa Lavender regarding one (1) \nvehicle at 2305 58th St. \n \n4.1.8 CASE NO: JV-2026-011399 of Inspector Everest Williams regarding one \n(1) vehicle at 1918 16th St. \n \n4.1.9 CASE NO: JV-2026-011400 of Inspector Lisa Lavender regarding one (1) \nvehicle at 2305 58th St. \n \n4.1.10 CASE NO: JV-2026-011401 of Inspector Lisa Lavender regarding one (1) \nvehicle at 2305 58th St. \n \n4.1.11 CASE NO: JV-2026-011432 of Inspector Everest Williams regarding one \n(1) vehicle at 1901 Texas Ave. \n \n4.1.12 CASE NO: JV-2026-011433 of Inspector Randy Merrick regarding one \n(1) vehicle at 2425 E 28th St. \n \n4.1.13 CASE NO: JV-2026-011515 of Inspector Andrew Valenzuela regarding \none (1) vehicle at 312 Beech Ave. \n \n4.1.14 CASE NO: JV-2026-011783 of Inspector Randy Merrick regarding one \n(1) vehicle at 1521 25th St. \n \n4.1.15 CASE NO: JV-2026-011788 of Inspector Randy Merrick regarding one \n(1) vehicle at 2010 E 28th St. \n\n4.2 \nWater Utilities Appeal Cases \n \n4.2.1 Consider and take appropriate action with regard to a request by Eduardo \nEspinoza for the approval of additional credit due to excessive water usage \nin accordance with Chapter 22, Section 22.03.096 of the City of Lubbock \nCode of Ordinances on the property located at 7224 25th Street."}, {"title": "2026-05-20 - Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee 12:00pm (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05202026-59", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "5/13/2026S:\\solid_waste\\Keep Lubbock Beautiful\\Advisory Committee\\0 Meeting Files 0\\Agenda\\2026\\May202026.doc Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee Agenda May 20, 2026 12:00 p.m. 208 Municipal Dr. 1) Call to Order 2) Citizen Comment – Notice: Any citizen wishing to appear before any regular meeting of the Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee shall advise Keep Lubbock Beautiful", "text": "5/13/2026S:\\solid_waste\\Keep Lubbock Beautiful\\Advisory Committee\\0 Meeting Files 0\\Agenda\\2026\\May202026.doc \n \nKeep Lubbock Beautiful \nAdvisory Committee Agenda \nMay 20, 2026 12:00 p.m. \n208 Municipal Dr. \n\n1) Call to Order \n\n2) Citizen Comment – Notice: Any citizen wishing to appear before any regular meeting of the Keep Lubbock \nBeautiful Advisory Committee shall advise Keep Lubbock Beautiful of that fact no later than seven calendar \ndays before the meeting at which the citizen wishes to appear. Any citizen wishing to appear shall inform Keep \nLubbock Beautiful, in writing, of the subject of the appearance in sufficient detail. Such notice shall contain the \nname and address of the requestor. The appearance of any citizen giving such notice to Keep Lubbock Beautiful \nshall take precedence in addressing the Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee during the Citizen \nComments period. Any citizen failing to advise Keep Lubbock Beautiful of his/her wish to appear before the \nKeep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee as herein above set forth shall, time permitting, be permitted to \naddress the Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee after those having given notice. In accordance with \nthe Texas Open Meetings Act, the Keep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee may not take action on public \nmatters during Citizen Comments. \n \n3) Approval of Minutes – April 22, 2026 meeting \n \n4) Work Session \n \na. Beautification Projects \nb. Trainings Review \nc. Community Outreach \nd. Youth Engagement \ne. Programs Review \nf. Review calendar of events \n\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING posted on the bulletin board outside the east entrance of \nCitizens Tower, 1314 Ave K, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____day of May 2026 at ________ A.M. /P.M. \n\nBy: ____________________________________________________ \n\nKeep Lubbock Beautiful Advisory Committee meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. \nIf you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806-775-3149 or write to P.O. Box \n2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-05-19 - Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05192026-42", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Deborah Bigness, Chair Baynetta Jordan, Vice-Chair Daniel Biase Mary Crites John Garcia III Randy Henson Patsy Jackson Chad Plunket Sheila Powell Sally Shelton Matthew Weaver Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission Agenda Regular Meeting May 19, 2026 5:30pm Citizens Tower, Council Chambers 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas The Urban Design and Historic Preservation Comm", "text": "Deborah Bigness, Chair \nBaynetta Jordan, Vice-Chair \nDaniel Biase \nMary Crites \nJohn Garcia III \nRandy Henson \nPatsy Jackson \nChad Plunket \nSheila Powell \nSally Shelton \nMatthew Weaver \n \nUrban Design and Historic Preservation Commission Agenda \nRegular Meeting \nMay 19, 2026 \n5:30pm \n \nCitizens Tower, Council Chambers \n1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n\nThe Urban Design and Historic Preservation Commission meetings are available to all persons \nregardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 806-775-\n2108, email cityplanning@mylubbock.us, or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least \n48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \nRegular Agenda \n1. Call to Order - Welcome and Introductions \n2. Citizen Comment - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Urban Design \nand Historic Preservation Commission regarding any matter posted on the Urban Design and \nHistoric Preservation Commission Agenda, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the \nmeeting, no later than 5:30 p.m. \n3. Approval of Minutes \n \n3.1 April 21, 2026, Regular Meeting \n \n4. Work Session \n \n4.1 Discuss and take appropriate action on the nomination of the Federal Building and U.S. \nCourthouse (current name: George H. Mahon Federal Building and United States \nCourthouse) to the National Register of Historic Places, at: \n• 1205 Texas Avenue, located south of Broadway and east of Texas Avenue, Original \nTown of Lubbock Addition, Blocks 134 and 150, closed street and alley. \n \n5. Adjourn \n \nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted on the bulletin board outside the east \nentrance of Citizens Tower 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas, on the 23rd day of April 2026 at \n10:30 AM. \n \n By: Rosie Gray"}, {"title": "2026-05-19 - Transportation Policy Committee (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05192026-58", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Transportation Policy Committee May 19, 2026, 8:30 a.m. 1314 Avenue K, City Council Chambers Lubbock Texas 79401 1. Call to Order of the Transportation Policy Committee. 2. Acknowledgment of Quorum of the Transportation Policy Committee. 3. Safety Procedures. 4. Public Comment Period/Acknowledgment Opportunity for the public to comment on transportation planning activities.", "text": "Transportation Policy Committee \nMay 19, 2026, 8:30 a.m. \n1314 Avenue K, City Council Chambers \nLubbock Texas 79401 \n \n1. \nCall to Order of the Transportation Policy Committee. \n \n2. \nAcknowledgment of Quorum of the Transportation Policy Committee. \n \n3. \nSafety Procedures. \n \n4. \nPublic Comment Period/Acknowledgment \n \nOpportunity for the public to comment on transportation planning activities. \n \nAction Required: None. \n \n5. \nApproval of April 21, 2026, Transportation Policy Committee Meeting Minutes \nAction Required: \nConsider approval of the April 21, 2026, Transportation Policy Committee meeting minutes. \nStaff Recommendation: \nApprove. \n \n6. \nJudge Parish to address the Transportation Policy Committee regarding matters impacting MPO’s \nthroughout the state. \n \n7. \nFY 2025–2026 Unified Planning Work Program (UPWP) – Proposed Amendment and Public Hearing \n \nDiscussion: \nFollowing the assessment and public hearing by the Transportation Advisory Committee, staff \nsubmits the proposed adjustment to the FY 2025-2026 UPWP. The amendment eliminates certain \nTask 5 activities that no longer align with current federal eligibility, regional priorities, or staffing \ncapacity, and redirects those resources to the development of standard operating procedures, \nstandardized engineering processes, and implementation-ready planning tools. The objective is to \nensure that future MPO work with federal funds adheres to current TxDOT guidance and federal \nrequirements. \n \nAction Required: \nApproval and adoption by resolution \n \nStaff Recommendation: \nApprove as recommended by the Transportation Advisory Committee. \n \n8. \nUnified Transportation Plan (UTP) and 10 Yr Plan Review \n \nDiscussion: \nStaff will provide an update on the requirements for the next Unified Transportation Plan (UTP) and \n10 Year Plan, their purpose, and how they relate to the Transportation Improvement Program. \n \nAction Required: \nNo action required \n \nStaff Recommendation: \nStaff Recommendation: Approve as recommended by the Transportation Advisory Committee. \n\n9. \nDefense Community Infrastructure Program (DCIP) \nDiscussion: \nThe staff will present an overview of a proposed grant application for the Defense Community \nInfrastructure Program (DCIP), which is a competitive grant designed to address community \ndeficiencies that support military installation readiness and lethality. \nAction Required: \nConsider approval for the MPO to pursue the Defense Community Infrastructure Program grant \napplication. \n \nStaff Recommendation: \nStaff Recommendation: Approve as recommended by the Transportation Advisory Committee. \n\n10. \nRailroad Crossing Elimination Program (RCE) \nDiscussion: \nThe staff will present an overview of a proposed Railroad Crossing Elimination (RCE) program to \nfund highway-rail or pathway-rail grade crossing improvements. This program is focused on \nimproving the safety and mobility of people and goods. \nAction Required: \nConsider approval for the MPO to submit an application to the Federal Railroad Administration (FRA) \nfor Railroad Crossing Elimination (RCE) funding. \n \nStaff Recommendation: \nStaff Recommendation: Approve to proceed with the application and submission. \n\n11. \nReports: \nA. Monthly Financial Statement \n \nMarch 2026. \n \n12. \nAdjournment \n\nComité de Política de Transporte \nMayo 19, 2026, 8:30 a.m. \n1314 Avenue K, Sala del Ayuntamiento \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \n \n1. \nOrden del Comité de Política de Transporte. \n \n2. \nReconocimiento del quórum del Comité de Política de Transporte. \n \n3. \nProcedimientos de seguridad. \n \n4. \nPeriodo de comentarios públicos/agradecimientos \n \nOportunidad para que el público comente sobre las actividades de planificación del transporte. \n \nAcción requerida: Ninguna. \n \n5. \nAprobación de las actas de la reunión del Comité de Política de Transporte del 21 de abril de 2026 \nAcción requerida: \nConsidere la aprobación del acta de la reunión del Comité de Política de Transporte del 21 de abril \nde 2026. \nRecomendación del personal: \n Aprobar. \n \n6. \nEl juez Parish se dirigirá al Comité de Política de Transporte respecto a losagentes que afectan a los \nMPO en todo el estado. \n \n7. \nPrograma Unificado de Trabajo de Planificación (UPWP) del año fiscal 2025–2026 – Propuesta de \nenmienda y audiencia pública \n \nDiscusión: \nTras la evaluación y audiencia pública del Comité Asesor de Transporte, el personal presenta el \najuste propuesto al UPWP del año fiscal 2025-2026. La enmienda elimina ciertas actividades de la \nTarea 5 que ya no se alinean con la elegibilidad federal actual, las prioridades regionales o la \ncapacidad de personal, y redirige esos recursos al desarrollo de procedimientos operativos estándar, \nprocesos de ingeniería estandarizados y herramientas de planificación listas para implementar. El \nobjetivo es asegurar que el trabajo futuro de MPO con fondos federales cumpla con las directrices \nactuales de TxDOT y los requisitos federales. \n \nAcción requerida: \nAprobación y adopción por resolución \n \nRecomendación del personal: \nAprobar según lo recomendado por el Comité Asesor de Transporte. \n \n8. \nPlan Unificado de Transporte (UTP) y Revisión del Plan Decimal \n \nDiscusión: \nEl personal proporcionará una actualización sobre los requisitos para el próximo Plan Unificado de \nTransporte (UTP) y el Plan Decimal Anual, su propósito y cómo se relacionan con el Programa de \nMejora del Transporte. \n \nAcción requerida: \nNo se requiere ninguna acción \n \nRecomendación del personal: \nRecomendación del personal: Aprobar según lo recomendado por el Comité Asesor de Transporte. \n\n9. \nPrograma de Infraestructura Comunitaria de Defensa (DCIP) \nDiscusión: \nEl personal presentará una visión general de una solicitud de subvención propuesta para el \nPrograma de Infraestructura Comunitaria de Defensa (DCIP), que es una subvención competitiva \ndiseñada para abordar deficiencias comunitarias que favorecen la preparación y letalidad de las \ninstalaciones militares. \nAcción requerida: \nConsidera la aprobación para que la MPO lleve adelante la solicitud de subvención del Programa de \nInfraestructura Comunitaria de Defensa. \n \nRecomendación del personal: \nRecomendación del personal: Aprobar según lo recomendado por el Comité Asesor de Transporte. \n\n10. \nPrograma de Eliminación de Pasos a Nivel Ferroviarios (RCE) \nDiscusión: \nEl personal presentará una visión general de un programa propuesto de eliminación de pasos a nivel \n(RCE) para financiar mejoras en cruces a nivel entre carreteras y vías o vías. Este programa se \ncentra en mejorar la seguridad y movilidad de personas y bienes. \nAcción requerida: \nConsidere la aprobación para que la MPO presente una solicitud ante la Administración Federal de \nFerrocarriles (FRA) para la Eliminación de Pasos a Nivel Ferroviarios (RCE ). \n \nRecomendación del personal: \nRecomendación del personal: Aprobar proceder con la solicitud y la presentación. \n\n11. \nInformes: \nA. Estado financiero mensual \n \nMarzo de 2026. \n \n12. \nSuspensión de la sesión"}, {"title": "2026-05-19 - The Electric Utility Board of Lubbock Power & Light Meeting - 11:00 AM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05192026-61", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "The Electric Utility Board of Lubbock Power & Light Agenda May 19, 2026 11:00 a.m. Edwin “Butch” Davis, P.E., Chair Eddie Schulz, Vice Chair Gwen Stafford, Secretary Dr. Solomon Fields Dr. Gonzalo Ramirez Dr. Craig Rhyne Rhea Hill Mike Stevens Thomas Parker Mark McBrayer, Ex-Officio www.lpandl.com Joel Ivy, Chief Administrative Officer Keli Swan, General Counsel CITIZENS TOWER 13", "text": "The Electric Utility Board of \nLubbock Power & Light Agenda \nMay 19, 2026 \n11:00 a.m. \nEdwin “Butch” Davis, P.E., Chair \nEddie Schulz, Vice Chair \nGwen Stafford, Secretary \nDr. Solomon Fields \nDr. Gonzalo Ramirez \nDr. Craig Rhyne \nRhea Hill \nMike Stevens \nThomas Parker \nMark McBrayer, Ex-Officio \nwww.lpandl.com \nJoel Ivy, Chief Administrative Officer \nKeli Swan, General Counsel \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Ave K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \nOPEN SESSION: \nCity Council Chambers \nEXECUTIVE SESSION: \nCitizens Tower \nCouncil Workroom 101A \n1.\nCall to Order.\n2.\nPublic Comment. Public comment is an opportunity for the public to make comments and\nexpress a position on agenda items.\na.\nThis period, of up to thirty minutes, is dedicated to public comment. Each member\nof the public will have three minutes to speak. Any member of the public that\nrequires the assistance of a translator will have six minutes to speak. Comments\nmust be limited to only those items posted on the Electric Utility Board agenda.\nAny member of the public wishing to speak shall sign up on the public comment\nsheet on the day of the Electric Utility Board Meeting, prior to the beginning of the\nmeeting. The Electric Utility Board shall follow the order of persons wishing to\nspeak as listed on the sign-up sheet. If everyone who signed up to speak has been\ngiven an opportunity to speak and the time designated for public comment has not\nbeen exhausted, the Electric Utility Board may, at its discretion, open the floor to\nanyone else wishing to address the Electric Utility Board, subject to all the same\nprocedures and guidelines.\n3.\nThe Electric Utility Board may make any comments related to board member activities,\nevents, community engagement, or current or future agenda items.\n1 of 119\n\nExecutive Session \n4.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.086,\n§552.133, and §552.1331 to discuss and deliberate, on the following competitive matters\nof Lubbock Power & Light, reasonably related to the following categories:\n(i) Discussion and deliberation of generation matters, including but not limited to\ngeneration unit and/or portfolio sale opportunities including the potential for unused\nland designated for other uses;\n(ii) Discussion of risk management, contracts, and strategies, customer billing, and usage\ninformation, system load characteristics, and ERCOT power analyses and strategies;\n(iii) Plans, studies, proposals, and analyses for system improvements, additions, or sales\n(other than transmission and distribution system improvements inside the service area\nfor which the public power utility is the sole certificated retail provider);\n(iv) info collected as part of an advanced metering system for usage, services, and billing,\nincluding amounts billed or collected for utility usage\n5.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.071,\nseeking the advice of legal counsel about pending or contemplated litigation or settlement\noffers and hold a consultation with the attorney on matters in which the duty of the attorney\nto the Electric Utility Board under the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct\nof the State Bar of Texas conflicts with Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code:\n(i)\nlegal advice regarding issues herein listed on the agenda;\n(ii)\nlegal advice and counsel regarding contemplated litigation matters;\n(iii)\nlegal advice and counsel regarding North American Electric Reliability\nCorporation Critical Infrastructure Protection (NERC CIP) standards;\n(iv)\nAjay Mitchell v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No. DC-\n2025-CV-1728, proceeding in the 72nd District Court of Lubbock County, Texas.\n(v)\nMaria Jesus Tucker v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No.\nDC-2026-CV-0096, proceeding in the 99th District Court of Lubbock County,\nTexas.\n6. Hold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1)\nto deliberate the appointment and/or reappointment of a public officer or employee\n(Electric Utility Board).\n7. Hold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1)\nto deliberate the appointment, employment, evaluation, and duties of a public officer or\nemployee (Director of Electric Utilities/Chief Administrative Officer, and General\nCounsel).\n2 of 119\nBack to Agenda\n\n11:30 a.m. – Approximately - Following completion of the Executive Session, the Electric \nUtility Board will reconvene in open session to discuss the items listed below. It is anticipated \nthat the open session will begin at 11:30 a.m. However, this is an approximation and the Board \nmay begin the open session earlier or later depending on when the Executive Session is \ncomplete. \nOpen Session \n8.\nApprove the minutes from the Regular Electric Utility Board meeting held on April 21, \n2026.\n9.\nPresentation and discussion of the update/report by the LP&L Chief Administrative \nOfficer, or his designee, regarding strategic planning, board committee reports, association \nmatters, ordinance change requests, EUB policy and procedures, customer service, \nbusiness center practices, procedures and policies, transition to retail customer choice, \ncustomer concerns and complaints, billing system, forms and procedures, status of \ntransmission and distribution projects, generation, mutual aid, weather events, outage and \nrestoration efforts, grid matters, Electric Reliability Council of Texas (“ERCOT”) matters \nand committee participation, staffing levels, personnel, regulatory matters, training, \ncybersecurity, mutual aid agreement, and operational performance metrics.\n10.\nPresentation and discussion of financial and capital statements, budget, financing options, \naudits, and financial policies of Lubbock Power & Light relating to debt issuance issues \nand historical debt matters, reserve account funding, cost allocation, revenue and expense \nprojections, customer metering, tariff and billing, service and administrative issues, and \nrisk management information including rates. \nConsent \n11.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Chief Administrative Officer or his designee to \nexecute a Professional Services Agreement (Agreement) by and between the City of \nLubbock, acting by and through Lubbock Power & Light, for Independent Representative \n(Owner’s Engineering) Services with Electrical Consultants, Inc. (ECI). This will be a four-\nyear agreement with a not-to-exceed of Five Million, Two Hundred Thousand Dollars and \nzero/100 dollars ($5,200,000.00).\n12.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Chief Administrative Officer, or his designee, to \nexecute Professional Service Agreement by and between the City of Lubbock, acting by \nand through Lubbock Power & Light, and 1898 and Co., of Kansas City, Missouri, Request \nfor Proposal (RFP) 7105-26-ELD - LP&L Operations Software Implementation Program \nConsulting Services. This agreement for $1,590,000.00 ELD - LP&L Operations Software \nImplementation Program Consulting Services. This agreement for $1,590,000.00 includes \nchange order language of up to 20% of the NTE may be authorized by the Chief \nAdministrative Officer, notwithstanding his purchasing authority will be for a term of 36 \n3 of 119\nBack to Agenda\n\nmonths with the option to extend an additional two (2) one-year extensions of time so long \nas the amount of the consideration payable hereunder does not increase. This agreement \nincludes professional and project management services, guidance and oversight for \nGeographic Information System (GIS) upgrade, implementation of a new Work Asset \nManagement System, and the implementation of a new Advanced Distribution \nManagement System (ADMS). \n13.\nConsider a resolution appointing and authorizing the Chief Administrative Officer to act\nas the Authorized Representative for all Lubbock Power & Light filings with the Electric\nReliability Council of Texas, appointing and authorizing the Chief Operating Officer and\nthe Chief Compliance Officer to act as the Back-up Authorized Representative(s), and\nsuperseding and replacing Resolution No. EUB 2025-R0007 regarding the previous\nAuthorized Representative and Back-up Authorized Representative for ERCOT.\n14.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Purchasing Manager, or his designee, to execute\nPurchase Order, by and between the City of Lubbock, acting by and through Lubbock\nPower & Light and Itron Inc., of Liberty Lake, Washington, for $531,138.00 with a 15\nweek delivery, Invitation to Bid (ITB) 7125-26-ELD - Cisco Heavy Duty Routers\nPreloaded with Itron OpenWay Riva Program. Cisco IR8140 router is the replacement for\nthe Cisco CGR1240 that was discontinued. Four IR8140 routers have already been\npurchased and installed. To complete the replacement project, an additional 40 IR8140\nrouters are required. This quantity will allow for the replacement of the remaining units\nand provide two spare IR8140 routers for emergency use.\n15.\nConsider a resolution rejecting all bids for Request for Proposal (RFP) 7123-26-ELD -\nOliver Substation 50 MVA Transformer Repair Facility Services. Proposal submitted\nexceeded the cost of the unit, LP&L will look at replacing the unit with a new transformer.\n16.\nAdjourn.\nTHE ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD RESERVES THE RIGHT TO ADJOURN INTO EXECUTIVE SESSION AS AUTHORIZED BY \nTEX. GOV’T CODE § 551.001, ET. SEQ. (THE TEXAS OPEN MEETINGS ACT) ON ANY ITEM ON ITS OPEN MEETING AGENDA \nOR TO RECONVENE IN A CONTINUATION OF THE EXECUTIVE SESSION ON THE EXECUTIVE SESSION ITEMS NOTED \nABOVE, IN ACCORDANCE WITH THE TEXAS OPEN MEETINGS ACT, INCLUDING, WITHOUT LIMITATION §§ 551.071-551.086. \nCITY OF LUBBOCK ELECTRIC UTILITY BOARD MEETINGS ARE AVAILABLE TO ALL PERSONS REGARDLESS OF DISABILITY. IF YOU \nREQUIRE SPECIAL ASSISTANCE, PLEASE CONTACT THE CITY SECRETARY AT (806)775-2026 OR WRITE P. O. BOX 2000, LUBBOCK, \nXAS 79457, AT LEAST 48 HOURS IN ADVANCE OF THE MEETING. \n4 of 119\nPosted on the bulletin board outside the south \nentrance \nof \nCitizens \nTower, \n1314 \nAve. \nK., Lubbock, Texas, on the 13th May 2026, at \n11:00 A.M.\nBy:____________\nBeatrice Duenez\n__________________ \nBack to Agenda\n\nThe Electric Utility Board of \nLubbock Power & Light Minutes \nApril 21, 2026 \n11:00 a.m. \nEdwin “Butch” Davis, P.E., Chair \nEddie Schulz, Vice Chair \nGwen Stafford, Secretary \nDr. Solomon Fields \nDr. Gonzalo Ramirez \nDr. Craig Rhyne \nRhea Hill \nMike Stevens \nThomas Parker \nMark McBrayer, Ex-Officio \nwww.lpandl.com \nJoel Ivy, Chief Administrative Officer \nKeli Swan, General Counsel \nCITIZENS TOWER \n1314 Ave K \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \nOPEN SESSION: \nCity Council Chambers \nEXECUTIVE SESSION: \nCitizens Tower \nCouncil Workroom 101A \nEUB \nCity Staff \nLP&L Staff \nJoel Ivy \nGwen Stafford \nButch Davis \nJarrett Atkinson \nMatt Wade \nKeli Swan \nClint Gardner \nDr. Gonzalo Ramirez \nRhea Hill \nThomas Parker \nMike Stevens \nBeatrice Duenez \nBlair McGinnis \nKacey Johnson \nTina Cooper \nFelix Orta \nSkylar Borunda \nCity Council \nTim Collins \nMatt Rose \nKody Morris \nRachel Hendrix \nFelix Orta \nTamara Randles \nCody Kirk \nRenee Treat \nMichael Weinegeart \nMarty Muniz \nCamille Powe \nAmy Day \nErich Von Heuvel \n5 of 119\nBack to Agenda\n\n1.\nCall to Order.\nBoard Chair Butch Davis called the meeting to order at 11:00 a.m.\n2.\nPublic Comment. Public comment is an opportunity for the public to make comments and\nexpress a position on agenda items.\na.\nThis period, of up to thirty minutes, is dedicated to public comment. Each member\nof the public will have three minutes to speak. Any member of the public that\nrequires the assistance of a translator will have six minutes to speak. Comments\nmust be limited to only those items posted on the Electric Utility Board agenda.\nAny member of the public wishing to speak shall sign up on the public comment\nsheet on the day of the Electric Utility Board Meeting, prior to the beginning of the\nmeeting. The Electric Utility Board shall follow the order of persons wishing to\nspeak as listed on the sign-up sheet. If everyone who signed up to speak has been\ngiven an opportunity to speak and the time designated for public comment has not\nbeen exhausted, the Electric Utility Board may, at its discretion, open the floor to\nanyone else wishing to address the Electric Utility Board, subject to all the same\nprocedures and guidelines.\nChair Butch Davis introduced Member of Public Brad Wise, who was in attendance \nas an observer. \n3.\nThe Electric Utility Board may make any comments related to board member activities,\nevents, community engagement, or current or future agenda items.\nChair Davis shared remarks regarding the Linemen Rodeo he recently attended. Mr.\nDavis commended the linemen for their hard work, outstanding accomplishments and\nawards earned, and their continued commitment to safety.\nExecutive Session \nThe Board recessed into executive session at 11:03 a.m. \n4.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.086,\n§552.133, and §552.1331 to discuss and deliberate, on the following competitive matters\nof Lubbock Power & Light, reasonably related to the following categories:\nViviana Valdez \nWilliam Butler \nLaura Gomez \nKrista Ortiz \n6 of 119\nBack to Agenda\n\n(i) Discussion and deliberation of generation matters, including but not limited to\ngeneration unit and/or portfolio sale opportunities including the potential for unused\nland designated for other uses;\n(ii) Discussion of risk management, contracts, and strategies, customer billing, and usage\ninformation, system load characteristics, and ERCOT power analyses and strategies;\n(iii) Plans, studies, proposals, and analyses for system improvements, additions, or sales\n(other than transmission and distribution system improvements inside the service area\nfor which the public power utility is the sole certificated retail provider);\n(iv) info collected as part of an advanced metering system for usage, services, and billing,\nincluding amounts billed or collected for utility usage\n5.\nHold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.071,\nseeking the advice of legal counsel about pending or contemplated litigation or settlement\noffers and hold a consultation with the attorney on matters in which the duty of the attorney\nto the Electric Utility Board under the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct\nof the State Bar of Texas conflicts with Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code:\n(i)\nlegal advice regarding issues herein listed on the agenda;\n(ii)\nlegal advice and counsel regarding contemplated litigation matters;\n(iii)\nlegal advice and counsel regarding North American Electric Reliability\nCorporation Critical Infrastructure Protection (NERC CIP) standards;\n(iv)\nTony Fullen v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No. 5:25-\nCV-055-H, proceeding in the U.S. District Court of the Northern District of Texas,\nLubbock Division.\n(v)\nAjay Mitchell v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No. DC-\n2025-CV-1728, proceeding in the 72nd District Court of Lubbock County, Texas.\n(vi)\nOncor Electric Delivery Company, LLC v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power\nand Light, Cause No. DD-1-GN-26-000237, proceeding in the 98th District Court\nof Austin County, Texas.\n(vii)\nMaria Jesus Tucker v. City of Lubbock and Lubbock Power and Light, Cause No.\nDC-2026-CV-0096, proceeding in the 99th District Court of Lubbock County,\nTexas.\n6. Hold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1)\nto deliberate the appointment and/or reappointment of a public officer or employee\n(Electric Utility Board).\n7. Hold an executive session in accordance with V.T.C.A. Government Code § 551.074(a)(1)\nto deliberate the appointment, employment, evaluation, and duties of a public officer or\n7 of 119\nBack to Agenda\n\nemployee (Director of Electric Utilities/Chief Administrative Officer, and General \nCounsel). \nOpen Session \nThe Board reconvened in open session at 11:50 a.m. \n8.\nApprove the minutes from the Regular Electric Utility Board meeting held on March 17,\n2026.\nA correction to the minutes was noted. The adjournment of the meeting needs to reflect\nEddie Schulz, not Butch Davis. Board member Thomas Parker made a motion to pass\nthe minutes as amended which was seconded by Board Secretary Gwen Stafford. The\nBoard approved the minutes by a vote of 6-0.\n9.\nPresentation and discussion of the update/report by the LP&L Chief Administrative\nOfficer, or his designee, regarding strategic planning, board committee reports, association\nmatters, ordinance change requests, EUB policy and procedures, customer service,\nbusiness center practices, procedures and policies, transition to retail customer choice,\ncustomer concerns and complaints, billing system, forms and procedures, status of\ntransmission and distribution projects, generation, mutual aid, weather events, outage and\nrestoration efforts, grid matters, Electric Reliability Council of Texas (“ERCOT”) matters\nand committee participation, staffing levels, personnel, regulatory matters, training,\ncybersecurity, mutual aid agreement, and operational performance metrics.\nLP&L Chief Administrative Officer Joel Ivy presented the Board with a discussion\nregarding LP&L’s upcoming grant possibilities, including tasks and goals involved. Mr.\nIvy presented pillars of success slides which included tasks, goals, safety performance\nand financial metrics summary along with reliability goals.\nBoard Member Thomas Parker left the meeting at 12:03 p.m.\n10.\nPresentation and discussion of financial and capital statements, budget, financing options,\naudits, and financial policies of Lubbock Power & Light relating to debt issuance issues\nand historical debt matters, reserve account funding, cost allocation, revenue and expense\nprojections, customer metering, tariff and billing, service and administrative issues, and\nrisk management information including rates.\nDirector of Finance Kacey Johnson presented LP&L’s February financial statements.\n11.\nPresentation and discussion of Smart Meter Texas functionality, including how it provides\ncustomers and REPs with access to near real-time usage data and supports improved bill\ntransparency, reduced inquiries, and overall customer experience.\n8 of 119\nBack to Agenda\n\nChief Customer Officer Clint Gardner gave a brief introduction on Smart Meter Texas \nand introduced from his team Market Operations Manager Michael Winegeart and \nViviana Valdez Retail Electric Provider Accounts Manager, for a brief presentation on \nusage data and supports improved bill transparency, reduced inquiries, and overall \ncustomer experience. \n12.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Chief Administrative Officer or his designee to allow\nthe Public Utility Commission of Texas to designate a Provider of Last Resort (POLR) for \nLP&L service territory.\nMr. Joel Ivy presented the PUC requirement needed for the Provider of Last Resort \ndesignation. A motion was made to approve by Board Member Gonzalo Ramirez and \nseconded by Board Member Mike Stevens. The motion passed by vote of 5-0. \n13.\nConsider a resolution authorizing the Chief Administrative Officer, or his designee, to\nexecute Master Service Agreement by and between the City of Lubbock, acting by and \nthrough Lubbock Power & Light, and NovaSync LLC of Provo, Utah, Request for Proposal \n(RFP) 7102-26-ELD - LP&L Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC) Management \nSoftware Solution. This agreement for $675,000.00 will be for a term of 5 years with the \noption to extend an additional two (2) one-year extensions of time so long as the amount \nof the consideration payable hereunder does not increase is for a turnkey, Governance, \nRisk, and Compliance (GRC) management software solution system to support compliance \nwith NERC reliability standards, ERCOT Nodal Protocols/Nodal Operating Guides, and \nTexas PUCT rules. This system will centralize and automate compliance activities, \ndocumentation, evidence management, and audit processes for LP&L.\nChief Compliance Officer Tina Cooper presented an overview of the item "}, {"title": "2026-05-19 - Citizen's Traffic Commission Meeting (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05192026-64", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Citizens Traffic Commission Regular Meeting Agenda May 19, 2026, 10:30 AM LP&L Training Room, 404 Municipal Drive The above-referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the south entrance of Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K Lubbock, Texas on ____________________________________________ By: ____________________________________________ Citizens Traffic Commission meetings are available to all", "text": "Citizens Traffic Commission \nRegular Meeting Agenda \nMay 19, 2026, 10:30 AM \nLP&L Training Room, 404 Municipal Drive \nThe above-referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted outside the south entrance of Citizens \nTower, 1314 Avenue K Lubbock, Texas on ____________________________________________ \nBy: ____________________________________________ \nCitizens Traffic Commission meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. \nIf you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at (806) 775-2132 or write to \nP.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n1. Call to Order: Welcome and Introductions \n2. Approval of Minutes: March 17, 2026, Regular Meeting \nMotion to approve March 17, 2026 Minutes \n3. Citizen Comments: Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Citizens \nTraffic Commission regarding any matter posted on the Citizens Traffic Commission Agenda \nshall sign up no later than 10:30 AM on the day of the meeting. \n4. Work Session: \n4.1. Presentation by Lubbock Police Department Assistant Chief Nathan White regarding a \nyear over year comparison for traffic accidents, traffic fatalities, and traffic \nenforcement within the City of Lubbock. This will include specific units and tools \ndesignated for traffic enforcement. \n4.2. Presentation regarding proposed school zone on 135th Street near Norfolk Avenue for \nLubbock-Cooper ISD Central Elementary \n5. Action Items: Proposed School Zone for Lubbock Cooper Central Elementary on 135th Street \n6. Activity Reports and Updates: \n6.1. Lubbock Police Department Activity Report \n6.2. Frenship Independent School District Activity Report \n6.3. Lubbock Independent School District Activity Report \n6.4. Lubbock-Cooper Independent School District Activity Report \n6.5. Traffic Management Department Citizen Requests Report \n6.6. Traffic Management Department Ongoing Projects Update"}, {"title": "2026-05-18 - May 2026 Library Board Meeting (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05182026-53", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Library Board Meeting May 18th, at 12:00 PM Mahon Community Room 1306 9th St, Lubbock, TX 79407 1) Call to Order Welcome and introductions – Sheri Lewis 2) Minutes Consider the approval of the April 20th, 2026 meeting minutes. 3) Citizen Comments According to City of Lubbock Board Rules, any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Library B", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas \nLibrary Board Meeting \nMay 18th, at 12:00 PM \nMahon Community Room \n1306 9th St, Lubbock, TX 79407 \n \n1) Call to Order \nWelcome and introductions – Sheri Lewis \n \n2) Minutes \nConsider the approval of the April 20th, 2026 meeting minutes. \n \n3) Citizen Comments \nAccording to City of Lubbock Board Rules, any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the \nLibrary Board, regarding any matter posted on the Library Board Agenda below, shall complete the sign-up \nform provided at the meeting, no later than 12:00 p.m. Any citizen wishing to comment on any matter posted on \nthe Library Board agenda below that will not be in attendance in person may submit a citizen comment by email \nat HStewart@mylubbock.us, no later than 8:00 a.m. on the date of the meeting. Submitted comments must \ninclude name and address. Each comment will be read aloud for a period of time not to exceed three minutes \npursuant to City of Lubbock Board Rules. Citizen Comments is an opportunity for citizens to make comments \nand express a position on agenda items. Any comment received that is in the nature of asking a question will be \nreferred to an appropriate member of the library or City staff for a response and will not be read aloud at the \nmeeting. In accordance with the Texas Open Meetings Act, the Libraries Board may not take action on public \nmatters during Citizen Comments. \n \n4) Presentations \n 4.1) Meet the Staff \n 4.2) Lubbock Library Director Report – Hannah Stewart, Lubbock Library Director \n 4.3) FOL President Report – Lynne Strickland, FOL Chair \n 4.4) Genealogical Society Report- Kathy Kitten \n\n5) Work Session \n 5.1) Discussion – A discussion pertaining to housekeeping items and future meeting agenda items. \n \n 5.2) Next regularly scheduled meeting will be at noon on June 15th, 2026 at the Mahon Library. \n\nLubbock Libraries Board meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of \nLubbock at 806-775-2826 or write to 1306 9th Street, Lubbock, TX 79401 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-05-15 - Board of Health-12:30 PM. (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05152026-55", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Board of Health Lubbock Public Health District May 15, 2026 12:30 PM Board of Health meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 775-2673 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. 1. Call to Order – Crockett Tidwell 2. Citizen Comments - An", "text": "Board of Health \nLubbock Public Health District \nMay 15, 2026 \n12:30 PM \n \nBoard of Health meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require \nspecial assistance, please contact the City of Lubbock at 775-2673 or write to P.O. Box 2000, \nLubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting. \n \n1. Call to Order – Crockett Tidwell \n \n2. Citizen Comments - Any citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Board \nof Health, regarding any matter posted on the agenda below, shall complete the sign-up \nform provided at the meeting, no later than 12:30 p.m. on the date of the meeting. Any \ncitizen wishing to comment on any matter posted on the Board of Health agenda below \nthat will not attend in person may submit a citizen comment by email to \njtaggart@mylubbock.us, no later than 12:30 p.m. on the date of the meeting. The \ncomments submitted must include name and address. Each citizen will have a period \nnot to exceed three minutes to address the board and each comment submitted will be \nread aloud for a period not to exceed three minutes. Citizen comments is an opportunity \nfor citizens to make comments and express a position on agenda items. Any comment \nreceived that is in the nature of asking a question will be referred to an appropriate staƯ \nmember for a response and will not be read aloud at the meeting. \n \n3. Work Session \n3.1. HIV & Hepatitis C Program Overview- Katherine Wells, DrPH \n3.2. Communicable Disease Report –Amanda Shotts/TiƯany Torres \n3.3. Health Department Update – Katherine Wells, DrPH \n \n4. Action Items \n4.1. Approval of the minutes from February 20, 2026 \n\nThe above referenced NOTICE OF MEETING was posted on the bulletin board outside the \nSouth entrance of City Hall, 1314 Ave K, Lubbock, Texas, on the _____________ Day of \n______________, 2026 at __________________ AM/PM. By: \n__________________________________________________________________"}, {"title": "2026-05-13 - The Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board of Trustees will conduct a regular meeting on Wednesday, May 13, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. in the City Bank Conference Room, located at 611 University Avenue, Lubbock, TX, for the purposes set forth in the following agenda: (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05132026-52", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "NOTICE OF A REGULAR MEETING OF THE LUBBOCK FIRE PENSION FUND BOARD OF TRUSTEES The Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board of Trustees will conduct a regular meeting on Wednesday, May 13, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. in the City Bank Conference Room, located at 611 University Avenue, Lubbock, TX 79401, for the purposes set forth in the following agenda: AGENDA Discussion and Action may be taken on the followin", "text": "NOTICE OF A REGULAR MEETING OF THE \nLUBBOCK FIRE PENSION FUND \nBOARD OF TRUSTEES \nThe Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board of Trustees will conduct a regular meeting on Wednesday, May 13, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. in the City \nBank Conference Room, located at 611 University Avenue, Lubbock, TX 79401, for the purposes set forth in the following agenda: \nAGENDA \nDiscussion and Action may be taken on the following items: \n1.\nCall to Order \n2.\nPublic Comment – Any member of the public wishing to address the Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board, regarding any matter on\nthe Agenda shall email lubbockfire@lauterbachamen.com by 11:00 a.m. on the day of the Board meeting. Comments by any\nindividual may not exceed five minutes in length unless extended by the Chairman. \n3.\nBenefit Applications, Changes, and QDROs (Qualified Domestic Relations Order) \n4.\nConsent Agenda – All matters listed under the Consent Agenda, are considered routine by the Board and will be enacted by one\nmotion. These include authorization of payment expenses. There will not be separate discussion of these items. If any Trustee \ndesires to discuss an item on the Consent Agenda, it will be moved to the Regular agenda for further consideration. \na)\nApril 15, 2026 Board Meeting Minutes \nb)\nLauterbach & Amen invoice in the amount of $7,515 for March 2026 professional services \nc)\nIndus Mokshum, LLC (PensionEZ) invoice in the amount of $7,615 for October 2025 through March 2026 website \nprofessional services \n5.\nConsultant Presentation by CAPTRUST \na)\nFirst Quarter 2026 Performance Report \nb)\nPreliminary April 2026 Performance\nc)\nDiscussion and Possible Action on Fund Rebalancing Recommendations \n6.\nOld Business \na)\nDiscussion and Possible Action on Plan Document Update \n7.\nNew Business \na)\nExpenses from the TEXPERS Annual Conference, April 26 – 29, 2026 \n8.\nAdministrator’s Report \na)\nMonthly Financial Report \nb)\nDemographics \nc)\nInvestments Update \nd)\nOffice Update \ne)\nTrustee Training \n9.\nCommittee Reports \na)\nMembership Committee \nb)\nLegislative Committee \nc)\nNominations Committee \nd)\nInvestments Committee \n10.\nAdjournment \nThe next regular meeting is scheduled for Wednesday, June 10, 2026 at 1:00 p.m. \nPosted: May 7, 2026 \nBy: Elizabeth Adelmann \nCity of Lubbock Fire Pension Fund Board Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, \nplease contact Elizabeth Adelmann at (630) 821-0201 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-05-13 - Lubbock Emergency Communication District Board of Managers Meeting (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05132026-54", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "The meeting of the Lubbock Emergency Communication District Board of Managers at the office of Lubbock Emergency Communication District is available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact Annie Wall at (806) 747-6911, or inquire at the LECD offices, 6032 43rd Street, Lubbock, Texas at least 48 hours in advance of the Board of Managers Meeting.", "text": "The meeting of the Lubbock Emergency Communication District Board of Managers at the office of Lubbock \nEmergency Communication District is available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special \nassistance, please contact Annie Wall at (806) 747-6911, or inquire at the LECD offices, 6032 43rd Street, Lubbock, \nTexas at least 48 hours in advance of the Board of Managers Meeting. \nLubbock Emergency Communication District \nBoard of Managers Meeting \nMay 13, 2026 \n6032 43rd Street \nLubbock, Texas \n12:00 P.M. \n1. Public Comments: \nAny citizen wishing to appear before a regular meeting of the Lubbock Emergency \nCommunication District Board of Managers, regarding any matter posted on the May 13, 2026 \nagenda, shall sign-in on the sheet provided at the meeting no later than 12:00pm. \n2. Administer Oath of Office to Board Member Kelly Rowe, reappointed by Lubbock County. \n3. Consider approval of minutes from March 25, 2026 Board of Managers meeting. \n4. Financial and Investment reports. \n5. Discuss and take action as necessary regarding adoption of the 2026 Employee Handbook. \n6. Discuss and take action as necessary regarding authorization to participate in Texas Class Local \nGovernment Investment Pool, resolution #26-05-01. \n7. District updates. \n \nCERTIFICATE OF POSTING \nThis is to certify that a true and correct copy of the forgoing agenda has been posted on this 8th day of May 2026, \non the Lubbock Emergency Communication District website (www.lubbock911.org) and has been submitted \nelectronically to the City of Lubbock, as written notice of the dates, place, and subjects of the Lubbock Emergency \nCommunication District Board of Managers meeting. A copy of the agenda has been posted at the LECD \nadministrative offices. \n\nNathan Kizer, Executive Director"}, {"title": "2026-05-13 - CANCELED - Meet and Confer Negotiations - Police - 12:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05132026-49", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Notice of Open Deliberations Pursuant to Tex. Loc. Gov’t Code § 142.063, this public notice is provided relating to meet and confer deliberations between: City of Lubbock, Texas and Lubbock Professional Police Association Date/Time: May 13, 2026 at 12:00 P.M. Location: Citizens Tower, 2nd Floor Conference Center, 201A/B, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas 79401 Agenda: Deliberations and/o", "text": "Notice of Open Deliberations \nPursuant to Tex. Loc. Gov’t Code § 142.063, this public notice is provided relating to meet and confer \ndeliberations between: \nCity of Lubbock, Texas \nand \nLubbock Professional Police Association \n \nDate/Time: \nMay 13, 2026 at 12:00 P.M. \nLocation: \nCitizens Tower, 2nd Floor Conference Center, 201A/B, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas \n79401 \nAgenda: \nDeliberations and/or related action relating to an agreement or a proposed agreement \nbetween the City of Lubbock, Texas (“City”) and the Lubbock Professional Police \nAssociation (“Association”) as provided for in Texas Local Government Code Chapter \n142, Subchapter B. \nPublic Notice: \nMembers of the public and press are encouraged to observe and monitor the deliberation. A recording of \nthe public portion of the meeting will be made available, but active participation in discussions or \npresentations is expressly prohibited unless permitted by the Ground Rules established by the City and the \nAssociation. \nRepresentatives for either the City or Association may suspend the meeting at any time during the \nproceedings for consultation with their attorney or to have a private caucus with their respective \nrepresentatives. Pursuant to Section 551.071 of the Texas Government Code and Section 142.063(b) of the \nTexas Local Government Code the deliberations during this period are closed to the public \nThe use of cellular phones or sound activated pagers is prohibited while deliberations are being conducted. \nPlease step outside the meeting room should you elect to utilize these devices. \nThis meeting is available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please \ncontact the City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas \n79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the meeting."}, {"title": "2026-05-12 - Transportation Advisory Committee (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05122026-47", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "Transportation Advisory Committee May 12th, 2026, 9:00 a.m. 916 Main Street, 2nd Floor Conference Room Lubbock, Texas 79401 1. Call to Order of the Transportation Advisory Committee 2. Acknowledgment of Quorum 3. Safety Procedures 4. Public Comment and Acknowledgment of Guests Members of the public may comment on items listed on the agenda. Comments are limited to three minute", "text": "Transportation Advisory Committee \nMay 12th, 2026, 9:00 a.m. \n916 Main Street, 2nd Floor Conference Room \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \n \n1. \nCall to Order of the Transportation Advisory Committee \n \n2. \nAcknowledgment of Quorum \n \n3. \nSafety Procedures \n \n4. \nPublic Comment and Acknowledgment of Guests \nMembers of the public may comment on items listed on the agenda. Comments are \nlimited to three minutes per speaker. \n \n5. \nConsider approval of the April 2nd, 2026, meeting minutes for the Transportation \nAdvisory Committee. \n \n6. \nUnified Transportation Plan (UTP) and 10 Yr Plan Review \n \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide an update on the requirements for the next Unified Transportation Plan \n(UTP) and 10 Year Plan, their purpose, and how they relate to the Transportation \nImprovement Program. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion, report findings to the Transportation Policy Committee. \n\n7. \nLocal Funding Mechanism \n \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nStaff will provide a case study overview of how an MPO has developed a local fund for non-\nfederalized grant matching. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion, report findings to the Transportation Policy Committee. \n \n8. \nDefense Community Infrastructure Program (DCIP) \n \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n\nThe staff will present an overview of a proposed grant application for the Defense \nCommunity Infrastructure Program (DCIP), which is a competitive grant designed to \naddress community deficiencies that support military installation readiness and lethality. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion and recommend advancing to the Transportation Policy \nCommittee for staff to pursue the Defense Community Infrastructure Program. \n\n9. \nRailroad Crossing Elimination Grant (RCE) \n \nDiscussion and Recommendation: \n \nThe staff will present an overview of a proposed Railroad Crossing Elimination (RCE) \nprogram to fund highway-rail or pathway-rail grade crossing improvements. This \nprogram is focused on improving the safety and mobility of people and goods. \n \nAction Requested: Discussion and recommend approval to the Transportation Policy \nCommittee for staff to pursue the Railroad Crossing Elimination. \n \n10. \nReports: \n• \nTxDOT \n• \nCitibus \n• \nLPD/DPS \n• \nTexas Tech University \n• \nLMPO’s \no Acronym and Document Map \n \n11. \nAdjournment \n\nComité Asesor de Transporte \nMayo 12, 2026, 9:00 a.m. \n916 Main Street, 2ª planta Sala de conferencias \nLubbock, Texas 79401 \n \n1. \nConvocatoria al Orden del Comité Asesor de Transporte \n \n2. \nReconocimiento del Quórum \n \n3. \nProcedimientos de seguridad \n \n4. \nComentarios y agradecimientos públicos a los invitados \nEl público puede comentar los puntos listados en la agenda. Los comentarios están \nlimitados a tres minutos por ponente. \n \n5. \nConsidere la aprobación del acta de la reunión Abril 2, 2026 para el Comité Asesor \nde Transporte. \n \n6. \nPlan deportivo UnifiedTran (UTP) y revisión del plan a 10 años \n \nDebate y recomendaciones: \n \nEl personal proporcionará una actualización sobre los requisitos para el próximo Plan \nUnificado de Transporte (UTP) y el Plan Decimal Anual, su propósito y cómo se relacionan \ncon elPrograma de Mejora de Transporte. \n \nSe solicita acción: Discusión, informe de las conclusiones al Comité de Política de \nTransporte. \n \n7. \nMecanismo de financiación local \n \nDebate y recomendaciones: \n \nEl personal proporcionará una visión general del estudio de caso de cómo una MPO ha \ndesarrollado un fondo local para la igualación de subvenciones no federalizadas. \n \nSe solicita acción: Discusión, informe de las conclusiones al Comité de Política de \nTransporte. \n \n8. \nPrograma de Infraestructura Comunitaria de Defensa (DCIP) \n \nDebate y recomendaciones: \n\nEl personal presentará una visión general de una solicitud de subvención propuesta \npara el Programa de Infraestructura Comunitaria de Defensa (DCIP), que es una \nsubvención competitiva diseñada para abordar las diferenciascomunitarias que apoyan \nla preparación y letalidad de las instalaciones militares. \n \nSe solicita acción: Discusión y recomendación de avance al Comité de Política de \nTransporte para que el personal siga adelante con el Programa de Infraestructura \nComunitaria de Defensa. \n \n9. \nSubvención para la Eliminación de Pasos a Nivel Ferroviarios (RCE) \n \nDebate y recomendaciones: \n \nEl personal presentará una visión general de un programa propuesto de eliminación de \npasos a nivel (RCE) para financiar mejoras en cruces a nivel entre carreteras y vías o \nvías . Este programa se centra en mejorar la seguridad y movilidad de personas y \nbienes. \nAcción solicitada: Discusión y recomendación de aprobación al Comité de Política de \nTransporte para que el personal lleve a cabo la eliminación de pasos a nivel \nferroviarios. \n \n10. \nInformes: \n• \nTxDOT \n• \nCitibus \n• \nLPD/DPS \n• \nUniversidad Tecnológica de Texas \n• \nLMPO's \no Acrónimo y mapa de documentos \n \n11. \nSuspensión de la sesión"}, {"title": "2026-05-12 - Regular City Council Meeting, May 12, 2026 (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05122026-51", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Regular City Council Meeting May 12, 2026 Mark W. McBrayer, Mayor Christy Martinez-Garcia, Mayor Pro Tem, District 1 Gordon Harris, Councilman, District 2 David Glasheen, Councilman, District 3 Brayden Rose, Councilman, District 4 Dr. Jennifer Wilson, Councilwoman, District 5 Tim Collins, Councilman, District 6 W. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager Matt Wade, City Attorney Cou", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas\nRegular City Council Meeting\nMay 12, 2026\nMark W. McBrayer, Mayor\nChristy Martinez-Garcia, Mayor Pro Tem, District 1\nGordon Harris, Councilman, District 2\nDavid Glasheen, Councilman, District 3\nBrayden Rose, Councilman, District 4\nDr. Jennifer Wilson, Councilwoman, District 5\nTim Collins, Councilman, District 6\n \nW. Jarrett Atkinson, City Manager\nMatt Wade, City Attorney\nCourtney Paz, City Secretary\nhttp://www.mylubbock.us\nCity Council Chambers, Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas\n \nCity of Lubbock City Council Meetings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact\nthe City Secretary's Office at (806)775-2061 or write to Post Office Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas 79457 at least 48 hours in advance of the\nmeeting. \nExecutive Session Disclosure Statement: The City Council reserves the right to adjourn into executive session at any time during the course of\nthe meeting to discuss any item listed on this agenda as authorized by Chapter 551 of the Texas Government Code, including but not\nnecessarily limited to §551.071 (Consultation with Attorney), §551.072 (Deliberations regarding Real Property), §551.073 (Deliberations\nregarding Gifts and Donations), §551.074 (Personnel Matters), §551.076 (Deliberations regarding Security Devices), §551.086 (Public\nPower Utilities: Competitive Matters), §551.087 (Deliberations regarding Economic Development Negotiations). \nA quorum of the City Council will be physically present in City Council Chambers located in Citizens Tower, 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, Texas,\nas it is the intent of the City Council to have a quorum physically present at this location. One or more members of the City Council, however,\nmay participate in the meeting by video-conference call as permitted under Section 551.127 of the Texas Government Code. \nNote: On occasion, the City Council may consider agenda items out of order. \n. \n \n12:00 p.m. - City Council convenes in City Council Chambers and immediately recesses into Executive\nSession. \n\n1.\nExecutive Session\n \n1. 1.\nHold an executive session in accordance with Texas Government Code, Section 551.071, with legal\ncounsel for advice about pending or contemplated litigation or settlement agreement and on matters in\nwhich the duty of the attorney under the Texas Disciplinary Rules of Professional Conduct conflicts\nwith Chapter 551. \n \n1. 1. 1.\nChapter 22 - City Code of Ordinances\n \n1. 1. 2.\nQuaker Avenue Development Agreement; Contract No. 17488; Code of Ordinances Chapter 41\nTexas Local Government Code Chapter 395 - Impact Fees\n \n1. 2.\nHold an executive session in accordance with the Texas Government Code, Section 551.072, to\ndeliberate the purchase, exchange, lease, or value of real property. \n\n1. 2. 1.\n123 North Avenue N\n \n1. 2. 2.\n10202, 10204, 10206, 10302, and 10304 Milwaukee Avenue\n \n1. 3.\n Hold an executive session in accordance with Texas Government Code Sec. 551.0761 to discuss and\ntake action on measures to protect a critical infrastructure facility.\n \n1. 3. 1.\nSoftware related to Critical Infrastructure\n \n1. 4.\nHold an executive session in accordance with Texas Government Code, Section 551.087, to discuss or\ndeliberate regarding commercial or financial information that the governmental body has received from\na business prospect that the governmental body seeks to have locate, stay, or expand in or near the\nterritory of the governmental body and with which the governmental body is conducting economic\ndevelopment negotiations; and to deliberate the offer of a financial or other incentive to a business\nprospect described herein above.\n\nAdjourn from Executive Session\n \nImmediately following Executive Session, the City Council reconvenes in City Council Chambers in Work\nSession.\n \n2.\nHear and discuss presentations regarding the following and provide guidance to staff as appropriate. \n \n2. 1.\nRecognition of 2026 Lubbock 101 Class \n \n2:00 p.m. - City Council continues in Open Session in City Council Chambers.\n \n3.\nCeremonial Items\n \n3. 1.\nInvocation \n \n3. 2.\nPledges of Allegiance \n \nCall to Order\n \n4.\nCitizen Comments - According to Lubbock City Council Rules, any citizen wishing to appear\nin-person before a regular meeting of the City Council, regarding any matter posted on the City\nCouncil Agenda below, shall complete the sign-up form provided at the meeting, no later than\n2:00 p.m. on May 12, 2026. Citizen Comments provide an opportunity for citizens to make\ncomments and express a position on agenda items. \n\n5.\nMinutes\n \n5. 1.\nApril 14, 2026 Regular City Council Meeting \n \n6.\nConsent Agenda - Items considered to be routine are enacted by one motion without separate\ndiscussion. If the City Council desires to discuss an item, the item is removed from the Consent\nAgenda and considered separately.\n \n6. 1.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance 2026-O0046, \nAmendment 23, respecting the Opioid Abatement Trust Fund in connection with accepting funding\nfrom Lubbock County in support of the Intermediary Care Clinic. \n \n6. 2.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance 2026-O0047,\nAmendment 24, respecting the Streets Capital Project Fund in connection with Capital Improvement\nProject 92811 - 98th Street: Alcove Avenue to Upland Avenue - 22B, and Capital Improvement Project\n92815 - Upland Avenue: 34th Street to 50th Street - 22B, to be utilized for the 2022 Street Bond\nProject. \n \n6. 3.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance 2026-O0025,\nAmendment 25, respecting the Wastewater Capital Project Fund, in connection with\nCapital Improvement Project 92843 - Water Reclamation Electrical Replacement and Monitor, Capital\nImprovement Project 8709 - Major Sewer line condition assessment and Repair, and Capital\nImprovement Project 92701 - Major Sewer line condition assessment and Repair. \n \n6. 4.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 2nd Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance 2026-O0049,\nAmendment 26, respecting the Stormwater Capital Project Fund in connection with Capital\nImprovement Project 92881 - Stormwater System Improvements & Maintenance, and Capital\nImprovement Project 92743 - Playa Lake 054 Lateral Extension. \n \n6. 5.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 1st Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance Amendment 27,\nrespecting the General Fund in connection with Cost Center 4211, Code Enforcement Operating\nBudget - Demolitions.\n \n6. 6.\nBudget Ordinance Amendment 1st Reading - Finance: Consider Budget Ordinance Amendment 28,\nrespecting the North Point Public Improvement District Fund, for additional funding for public\nimprovements related to Contract 18386.\n \n6. 7.\nOrdinance 1st Reading - Finance: Consider an Ordinance amending Ordinance No. 2025-O0108,\n Exhibit “B”, the 2025 Service and Assessment Plan for the North Point Public Improvement District,\nby including additional funding for public improvements for Contract 18386. \n \n6. 8.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0050, abandoning and\nclosing an overhead power line easement located in Tract A, Block 134 of Overton Addition, in Section\n2, Block O, Lubbock County, Texas, on the east side of Avenue Q, south of 10th Street, and north of\nMain Street. \n\n6. 9.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0051, abandoning and\nclosing a portion of an alley located south of Tract A, Broadview Baptist Church Addition, in Section\n6, Block JS, Lubbock County, Texas, on the east side of North Frankford Avenue, south of Princeton\nStreet, and north of Kemper Street. \n \n6. 10.\nOrdinance 1st Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider an ordinance abandoning and closing a portion of\na drainage easement located in Tracts A, H, I, and J of the Legacy Range Business Park Addition,\nwithin Section 21, Block E-2, Lubbock County, Texas, on the south side of 116th Street, east of\nIndiana Avenue. \n \n6. 11.\nOrdinance 1st Reading - Right-of-Way: Consider an ordinance abandoning and closing a portion of\ndrainage easements located to the east of the intersection of 88th Street and Urbana Avenue, within\nSection 26, Block AK, Lubbock County, Texas, located west of Upland Avenue, east of Urbana\nAvenue, at 88th Street and 90th Street.\n \n6. 12.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor of the City of Lubbock to\nexecute a Street Use License, and related documents, by and between the City of Lubbock and Texland\nPetroleum, Inc., west and north of the southeast corner of Section 9, Block A, Lubbock County Texas,\nlocated on a portion of Mesa Road, north of Kent Street, for underground flow lines. \n \n6. 13.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Memorandum\nof Agreement, by and between the City of Lubbock and Nathan Stiff, for right-of-way real property\nlocated in Section 42, Block AK, Lubbock County, Texas (Parcel 57), located on the west side of\nUpland Avenue, across from 30th Street, to be utilized for the 2022 Street Bond Project. \n \n6. 14.\nResolution - Right-of-Way: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute a Memorandum\nof Agreement, by and between the City of Lubbock and Harvest Church Lubbock, Inc., a Texas\nNon-Profit Corporation, f/k/a Family Harvest Church, Inc., a Texas Non-Profit, for fee simple title to a\n0.413-acre tract, fee simple title to a 0.826-acre tract, a Drainage Easement over a 0.11-acre tract, and a\nGrading Easement over a 0.298-acre tract of land, located in the Southeast Quarter (SE 1/4) of Section\n34, Block AK, Georgetown Railroad Co. Survey, Abstract No. 1385, Lubbock County, Texas (Parcels\n5and 6), located on the west side of Upland Avenue, between 61st Street and 66th Street, to be utilized\nfor the 2022 Street Bond Project.\n \n6. 15.\nOrdinance 2nd Reading - Engineering: Consider Ordinance No. 2026-O0052, amending Chapter 36\nof the Code of Ordinances by adding “Division 2 Paving Alleys by Petition” to Article 36.08,\nestablishing a process for the paving of unimproved alleys through a petition initiated assessment\nprocess and providing for placing a lien on property for unpaid assessments. \n \n6. 16.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Public Works\nContract 19474, with Lone Star Dirt & Paving, Ltd., for reconstruction of Upland Avenue from 34th\nStreet to 50th Street, under the 2022 Street Bond Project. \n \n6. 17.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Public Works\nContract 19440, and related documents, with Utility Contractors of America, for North Terminal\nStorage Reservoir Improvements and Valve Replacement. \n\n6. 18.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Public Works\nContract 19423, and related documents, with Landmark Structures I, L.P., for the construction of the\n34th Street 2.0 Million-Gallon Elevated Storage Tank. \n \n6. 19.\nResolution - Engineering: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Amendment No. 1\nto Professional Services Agreement, Contract 18558, and related documents, by and between the City\nof Lubbock and Carollo Engineers, Inc., for the Southeast Water Reclamation Plant Headworks Capital\nImprovements Project. \n \n6. 20.\nResolution - Water Utilities, Wastewater: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute\nContract 19542, with Fando Landscaping, LLC., to provide lawn mowing, trimming, and landscaping\nservices at the North Water Treatment Plant, South Water Treatment Plant, Northwest Water\nReclamation Plant, Southeast Water Reclamation Plant, and the Water Utilities Facility at Municipal\nHill. \n \n6. 21.\nResolution - Aviation: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Contract 19427, with\nChem-Aqua, Inc., for the Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning Water Treatment Services at\nLubbock Preston Smith International Airport. \n \n6. 22.\nResolution - Public Transit Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute\nAmendment No. 1 to the Transit System Management Contract, by and between the City of Lubbock\nand RATP Dev USA, Inc., to address the implementation of and payment for the UKG Ready\n(formerly “Kronos”) payroll management system.\n \n6. 23.\nResolution - Business Development: Consider a resolution approving a Downtown Grant Program\n(Permittable) expenditure of Market Lubbock, Inc., to be provided to CEV Group Holding Company,\nLLC, located at 1500 Broadway, pursuant to Article IV, Section 5, of the Amended and Restated\nBylaws of the Market Lubbock Development Corporation. \n \n6. 24.\nResolution - Business Development: Consider a resolution approving a Downtown Grant Program\n(Facade) expenditure of Market Lubbock, Inc., to be provided to TDRM Holdings, LLC, located at 607\nBroadway, pursuant to Article IV, Section 5, of the Amended and Restated Bylaws of the Market\nLubbock Development Corporation. \n \n6. 25.\nResolution - Business Development: Consider a resolution approving a Downtown Grant Program\n(Permittable) expenditure of Market Lubbock, Inc., to be provided to TDRM Holdings, LLC, located at\n607 Broadway, pursuant to Article IV, Section 5, of the Amended and Restated Bylaws of the Market\nLubbock Development Corporation. \n \n6. 26.\nResolution - Facilities Management: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute\nContract 19482, with Tecta America CS, LLC, for the complete roof replacement of the Godeke\nLibrary, located at 5034 Frankford Avenue in Lubbock, Texas. \n \n6. 27.\nResolution - Facilities Management: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Contract\n19449, with Z Ammons Contracting dba Core Commercial Roofing, for the complete roof replacement\nof the Copper Rawlings Community and Adult Activity Center, located at 213 40th Street in Lubbock,\nTexas. \n\n6. 28.\nResolution - Parks and Recreation: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute\nAmendment No. 4 to Interlocal Cooperation Agreement, Contract 97871, with Lubbock County, for\ninmate labor services for cleaning and maintaining public rights-of-way and other City-owned and\noperated properties. \n \n6. 29.\nResolutions - Fleet Services : Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Service Contract\n19339, with Mighty Wash Operations, LLC, dba Mighty Wash, Service Contract 19596, with Lone Star\nSuds, LLC, dba Lone Star Suds, and Service Contract 19597, with 3 BMH Investments, LLC, dba\nHurricane Beach Car Wash, for car wash services for City light and medium-duty vehicles. \n \n6. 30.\nResolution - Fleet Services: Consider a resolution authorizing the Mayor to execute Contract 19376,\nwith Lubbock Allstar Truck Wash & Lube, for wash services for City heavy-duty vehicles and\nequipment. \n \n6. 31.\nResolution - Police: Consider a resolution authorizing the City Manager to execute an Interlocal\nAgreement, with the City of Dallas, regarding the North Texas (Dallas) Internet Crimes Against\nChildren Grant, and to accept grant funding in accordance with the terms of the Interlocal Agreement,\nto provide equipment and training. \n \n6. 32.\nResolution - City Manager: Consider a resolution authorizing the City Manager to execute a real\nproperty purchase and sale agreement and any necessary ancillary documents for the sale of the\nproperty located at 123 North Avenue N (Richmond 2nd BLK 5 Lots 12, 13, 14, 15 and 16, Lubbock\nCounty).\n \n7.\nRegular Agenda\n \n7. 1.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 2): Consider a request for Zone Case 1154-D, a request of\nJeremy Davis, for a zone change from Neighborhood Commercial District (NC) to Auto-Urban\nCommercial District (AC), at 2518 Parkway Drive, located south of Parkway Drive and west of Zenith\nAvenue, Oates Addition, Lot 3, and consider an ordinance. \n \n7. 2.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 4): Consider a request for Zone Case 2911-E, a request of\nSeventeen Services, LLC, for Lequity, LLC, for a zone change from Office District (OF) to\nNeighborhood Commercial District (NC), at 10909 Wayne Avenue, located east of Slide Road and\nnorth of 110th Street, Epic North Addition, Tract Q, and consider an ordinance. \n \n7. 3.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 4): Consider a request for Zone Case 3543, a request for zone\nchanges in City Council District 4 from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2), Medium Density\nResidential District (MDR), High Density Residential District (HDR), Office District (OF),\nNeighborhood Commercial District (NC), and Heavy Commercial District (HC), to Low Density\nSingle-Family District (SF-2), Medium Density Residential District (MDR), High Density Residential\nDistrict (HDR), Office District (OF), Neighborhood Commercial District (NC), Auto-Urban\nCommercial District (AC), and Heavy Commercial District (HC), at 3201 and 3223 South Loop 289,\nTracts C and H, Live Oak Addition, 7414 Elgin Avenue, 6.47 acres out of Tract N, Melonie Gardens\nAddition, 2516, 2514, 2512, and 2510 81st Street, Lots 52 and 54-56, Gatewood Addition, 2803 114th\nStreet, 11.81 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 21, 3001 114th Street, 6.49 acres of\nunplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 21, 3101 114th Street, 25.88 acres of unplatted land out of\nBlock E-2, Section 21, 3102 116th Street, 1.91 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 21,\n12601 Indiana Avenue, 11.10 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 21, 12605 Indiana\nAvenue, Tract A, Discount Shops on Indiana Addition, 12701 Indiana Avenue, 6.7 acres of unplatted\n\nland out of Block E-2, Section 21, 12709 Indiana Avenue, Tract A, Worship Center 1 Addition, 3802\n130th Street, 3.64 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 22, 3814 130th Street, Tract A, less\nthe right-of-way, First Presbyterian Church Addition, 3815 130th Street, 1.22 acres out of Tract F-1,\nKelsey Park Addition, 13005 Norfolk Avenue, 1.23 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 4,\n3714 146th Street, 0.75 acres of unplatted land out of Block AK, Section 4, 14401 – 14412 Kenosha\nDrive, Lots 119 – 130, Eastwick at Kelsey Park Addition, 11209 Slide Road, Tract A less the west 5\nfeet of right-of-way, Raney Addition, 11912 Slide Road, 1.02 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2,\nSection 24, 12108 Slide Road, 0.91 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 24, 12116 Slide\nRoad, 1.5 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 24, 12302 Slide Road, the east 240 feet of\nthe north half of Tract A less 810.467 square feet of right-of-way, Sandy Mariah Estates Addition,\n12308 Slide Road, the east half of the south half of Tract A less the east 4.89 feet, Sandy Mariah\nEstates Addition, 5103 82nd Street, 0.08 acres out of Tract J, Lakeridge Country Club Estates\nAddition, 5105 82nd Street, Tract I-8, Lakeridge Country Club Estates Addition, 5107 82nd Street,\n2.32 acres out of Tract I-7-A, Lakeridge Country Club Estates Addition, 5109 82nd Street, Tracts I-5\nand I-6-A, Lakeridge Country Club Estates Addition, 5109 82nd Street Rear and 8215 Slide Road,\nTract I-3, Lakeridge Country Club Estates Addition, 5111 82nd Street, Tract I-4, Lakeridge Country\nClub Estates Addition, 8209 Slide Road, Tract I-1, Lakeridge Country Club Estates Addition, and 8211\nSlide Road, Tract I-2, Lakeridge Country Club Estates Addition, and consider an ordinance. \n \n7. 4.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 4): Consider a request for Zone Case 3543, Part 2, a request for\nzone changes in City Council District 4 from Low Density Single-Family District (SF-2) and\nIndustrial Park (IP) to Auto-Urban Commercial District (AC) and Heavy Commercial District (HC), at\n3102 114th Street, 9.19 acres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 19, 2802 114th Street, 13.86\nacres of unplatted land out of Block E-2, Section 19, and 3219 116th Street, 4.54 acres of unplatted\nland out of Block E-2, Section 21, and consider an ordinance. \n \n7. 5.\nPublic Hearing - Planning (District 4): Consider a request for Zone Case 3543, Part 3, a request for\nzone changes in City Council District 4 from Low Density Single-F"}, {"title": "2026-05-12 - Public Hearing - Centrepointe Public Improvement District (PID) Creation - 2:00 PM (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05122026-34", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "City of Lubbock, Texas Centerpointe Public Improvement District Public Hearing for the Proposed Centerpointe Public Improvement District May 12, 2026 at 2:00 P.M. Citizens Tower, Council Chambers Please take notice that on May 12, 2026, at 2:00 PM, the City Council of Lubbock, Texas, will conduct a public hearing in the City Council Chamber of Citizens Tower at 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, TX 7", "text": "City of Lubbock, Texas \nCenterpointe Public Improvement District \nPublic Hearing for the Proposed Centerpointe Public Improvement District \nMay 12, 2026 at 2:00 P.M. \nCitizens Tower, Council Chambers \n \nPlease take notice that on May 12, 2026, at 2:00 PM, the City Council of Lubbock, Texas, will conduct a public \nhearing in the City Council Chamber of Citizens Tower at 1314 Avenue K, Lubbock, TX 79401. The purpose of this \nhearing is to consider the establishment of the Centerpointe Public Improvement District (“District”) pursuant to \nChapter 372 of the Texas Local Government Code (The “Act”). \n \nAll interested persons will be given the opportunity to appear and be heard at such public hearing. Written or oral \nobjections will be considered. The hearing may be adjourned from time to time until the City Council makes findings \nby ordinance as to the advisability of the District. \n \nThe following information is hereby provided concerning the matters to be considered at such hearing: \n \nNature of the Services and Improvements: The general nature of the proposed public improvements is: (i) \nacquisition, construction, or drainage facilities or improvements, (a) the design, construction and maintenance of \nparks and green spaces, together with any ancillary structures, features or amenities such as playgrounds, splash pads, \npool(s), athletic facilities, pavilions, community facilities, irrigation, walkways, lighting, benches, trash receptacles \nand any similar items located there in along with all necessary grading, drainage, and similar infrastructure involved \nin the construction of such parks and greens; landscaping, hardscaping and irrigation; and (ii) payment of costs \nassociated with developing and financing the public improvements listed in subdivision (i) including costs of \nestablishing, administering and operating the District. \n \nEstimated Cost of the Services and Improvements: The proposed assessment for the first year is for tax year 2026 \nwhich will fund the budget for FY 2026-27. The total estimated cost of services provided by the District is estimated \nat $168,534 over the next five years. The estimated annual cost rises from $0 in the first year to $72,320 in year five \n(5). The costs are based on a proposed assessment rate of $0.17 per $100.00 of valuation through tax year 2031. \n \nBoundaries: The proposed boundaries of the Centerpointe Public Improvement District (PID) cover a portion of an \narea in the City of Lubbock, Lubbock County, Texas, generally bounded on the east by North Avenue U, on the south \nby Drake Street, on the west by North University, and on the north by Georgetown Street. \n \nMethod of Assessment: Properties will be assessed based on the City’s “net taxable value” as established by the \nLubbock Central Appraisal District. Each assessment will be determined using the formula: (net taxable value \ndivided by 100) multiplied by assessment rate. \n \nApportionment of Cost: The District is to supplement and enhance services within the District, but not replace or \nsupplant existing City services provided within the District. One hundred percent of the maintenance of the public \nimprovements and any construction of new public improvements included in the Service Plan will be funded by the \nPublic Improvement District. \n \nPublic hearings are available to all persons regardless of disability. If you require special assistance, please contact \nthe City Secretary’s Office at 775-2025 or write to P.O. Box 2000, Lubbock, Texas, 79457, at least 48 hours in \nadvance of the public hearing. \n \nAny questions concerning the matters described in this notice should be directed to: Molly Ballesteros, City of \nLubbock Business Development Department at 775-3083."}, {"title": "2026-05-07 - Planning and Zoning Commission (Agenda)", "url": "https://www.mylubbock.us/AgendaCenter/ViewFile/Agenda/_05072026-44", "category": "Agenda", "source": "ExposeTexas", "summary": "PK\u0003\u0004\u0014\u0000\u0006\u0000\b\u0000\u0000\u0000!\u0000�'*��\u0001\u0000\u0000�\u0006\u0000\u0000\u0013\u0000\b\u0002[Content_Types].xml �\u0004\u0002(�\u0000\u0002\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u00